window.INK_INDEX=[{"c": "First sound", "u": "first-sound.html", "h": "", "t": "Open INK. Touch one of the round pads. That is a note, and you are playing. This page gets you from a quiet screen to a sound you like, in about two minutes. 123 INK, ready to play. You start on tab 1.1Engine tabs 2Sound name 3The pads"}, {"c": "First sound", "u": "first-sound.html", "h": "", "t": "#One tab, one instrument Along the top you see four tabs: 1, 2, 3 and All. INK is three instruments in one, and each numbered tab is one of them. We call each one an engine. You start on Engine 1. Leave the others alone for now. They get their own chapter soon (more in chapter 3)."}, {"c": "First sound", "u": "first-sound.html", "h": "", "t": "#Pick a sound Every engine plays one sound. You choose it from the library. Tap the sound's name. It sits at the bottom of the card, under the waveform. 12 The sound name. An asterisk (*) after it means you changed the sound.1Sound name 2Edited asterisk 1234 The Library. Filters on top, sounds below.1Search 2Favourites star 3Type 4A sound row Each sound belongs to one of six kinds. Open Type (it reads All types until you choose) and pick one: Pad: soft and long. Lead: made to sing a tune. Bass: low and heavy. Pluck: short and bright. Keys: keyboard sounds. FX: noises and textures. QUICK STEPSLoad a sound Tap the sound name. Open Type and choose Pad. Tap any row. The Library closes and the sound is loaded. Careful If you changed the current sound, INK asks before it loads another one. Your changes would be lost."}, {"c": "First sound", "u": "first-sound.html", "h": "", "t": "#Listen before you load Every row has two small marks on the right. 123 One row: the name, a star and a play button.1Name 2Star 3Play button Tap the play button to audition a sound. You hear it, but your own sound stays put. That makes it easy to compare. Tap the star to make a sound a favourite. The star at the top of the Library then shows only your favourites. Tip Looking for something? Type in the search field. Sort puts the list in order by Type, Name or Newest. Two more filters sit beside Type: Engine and From. Ignore both today. Engine picks a kind of sound generator, not Engine 1, 2 or 3 (more in chapter 3). From picks where sounds came from, such as the Piano Room pack (more in chapter 31). If the list ever looks empty, tap Clear."}, {"c": "First sound", "u": "first-sound.html", "h": "", "t": "#Play it The pads are called the Grid. Each pad is one note. 123 The Grid. Higher up means a higher note.1One column 2Root pad 3Pitch goes up Go up a column and the notes go up. Eight columns sit side by side, so you can play chords with one finger on each. A few pads are marked. They are the root notes of the scale, a home base for your ears. Touch a pad, hold it, and slide your finger. The note comes with you. There are two other surfaces, called Keys and Chords. You will meet them later (more in chapter 8 and chapter 9)."}, {"c": "First sound", "u": "first-sound.html", "h": "", "t": "#Turn Macro A and Macro B Two knobs at the top of the Sound tab are made for playing. They are Macro A and Macro B. The screen labels them just A and B. 12 Macro A turned up to 70%.1Macro A 2Macro B Hold a note with one finger and turn them with another. What they change depends on the sound. The person who made it chose. In one pad, Macro A may open the tone. In another, it may add wobble. Listen and find out. You will find the same two knobs on All (more in chapter 4)."}, {"c": "First sound", "u": "first-sound.html", "h": "", "t": "#No sound? Look at the thin bar at the left of the card while you play. It is the output level. It moves. INK is making sound. Check your volume, your headphones or the speaker. It stays still. Tap All and look at Engine 1. Its Level may be down, or Mute may be on. 12 The All page mixer. Each engine has a Level slider and a Mute button.1Level 2Mute, switched on Under the hood MIDI keyboard? Engine 1 listens on channel 1, Engine 2 on channel 2 and Engine 3 on channel 3. Tapping a tab only changes which engine you are editing. It does not change where MIDI goes. AUv3? INK also runs inside other music apps that host AUv3 plug-ins. If you hear nothing there, check that app's audio routing too."}, {"c": "First sound", "u": "first-sound.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS Tap the sound name. Open Type, choose Pad and tap a row. Play a few pads. Slide a finger up a column. Tap the star on your favourite pad sound. Tap the sound name again. Choose Bass and load one. Hold a note. Turn Macro A and Macro B with your other hand."}, {"c": "First ten minutes", "u": "first-ten-minutes.html", "h": "", "t": "Six small tricks, one lap of the whole instrument. Don't worry about names yet. Do each one, listen, and move on. The next chapters explain everything you touch here. Start with a sustained sound, like a Pad, on Engine 1. If you skipped it, chapter 1 takes two minutes."}, {"c": "First ten minutes", "u": "first-ten-minutes.html", "h": "", "t": "#1. Press and roll Hold one note. Press harder, or roll your finger flatter on the glass. Listen. Does it get brighter? Louder? The sound reads how hard you press. We call that pressure. 1 Light touch: the trace is nearly flat.1Light touch 12 Firm touch: the trace swings wide.1Level bar rises 2The sound changes Now slide to the next pad without lifting. The note travels with you. Different sounds answer in different ways. Some open up, some get louder, some bend. Try a few. Try it Pick a Pad. Hold a note and press, then ease off. Do it three times, slowly."}, {"c": "First ten minutes", "u": "first-ten-minutes.html", "h": "", "t": "#2. Keep notes ringing Tap Hold, play three pads, then lift your fingers. The notes keep sounding. Your hands are free. That is Hold. Hold off. 12 Hold on, with three notes latched.1Hold button 2Latched pads To let go of one note, tap its pad again. To let go of everything, double-tap Hold. Try it Latch a low note and two high ones. Now play a melody on top with your free finger."}, {"c": "First ten minutes", "u": "first-ten-minutes.html", "h": "", "t": "#3. Turn held notes into an arp Open the Arp tab. At the top you see three choices: Free, Arp and Strum. Choose Arp. Your held notes now play one after another, again and again. 123 The Arp tab. The switch is set to Arp and the dots show each note in turn.1Free / Arp / Strum 2Division knob 3The trace Turn Division. The notes speed up and slow down. Strum is the third choice. It plays a group of notes once, one after another, and lets them ring. The arp has a chapter of its own (chapter 24), and so does Strum (chapter 26). Try it Choose Arp, then turn Division while the notes play. Choose Free to go back to normal playing."}, {"c": "First ten minutes", "u": "first-ten-minutes.html", "h": "", "t": "#4. Darken the sound Open the Sound tab. It is long, so it scrolls. Tap a section title, the heading above a group of knobs, to open a list of sections. Choose FILTER. 12 Tap a section title and a list opens. Choose FILTER.1Section title, pinned 2Choose FILTER Turn Cutoff down. The sound gets darker and softer, as if a blanket fell over it. Turn it back up and the shine returns. Now add a little Resonance. The sound starts to sing around the cutoff point. (More in chapter 13.) 1 Cutoff wide open.1Cutoff, open 1 Cutoff turned down.1Cutoff, turned down Try it Turn Cutoff slowly from top to bottom while you hold a chord. It is the most useful knob in INK."}, {"c": "First ten minutes", "u": "first-ten-minutes.html", "h": "", "t": "#5. Meet the others Tap All. You see all three engines in one place, each with its own level. Engine 2 and Engine 3 can hold different sounds. Tap tab 2, load a Bass, then come back to All. Play both at once. 1234 The All page: three engines, one mixer.1Engine 3 2Engine 1 3Level 4Mute You will learn this page properly in chapter 4."}, {"c": "First ten minutes", "u": "first-ten-minutes.html", "h": "", "t": "#6. Keep what you made You changed the sound, so an asterisk (*) appears after its name. That means it is no longer exactly the saved one. Tap Save, beside the name, to keep it. 123 The Save sheet on a factory sound. Save as new keeps the original too.1Save over the factory sound 2Save as new... 3Cancel Choose Save as new..., type a name and pick a category. Now your sound sits in the Library next to the factory ones, and the original is untouched. The top button changes with the sound: On a factory sound it reads Save over the factory sound. Your version then takes its place, and a Restore factory version button can bring the original back. On a sound of your own it reads Save. If you load another sound while this one has unsaved changes, INK asks first, with Save, Save as new..., Discard changes and Cancel. Careful The top button replaces the sound you started from. Use Save as new... when you want to keep both. A sound is one engine's tone. To keep all three engines together, with their sounds and settings, you save a project. That is in chapter 32. Try it Save your darkened pad as a new sound. Open the Library and find it."}, {"c": "The three engines", "u": "the-three-parts.html", "h": "", "t": "You have played INK for a few minutes. Now let's draw the map. It is short, and it makes every later chapter easier."}, {"c": "The three engines", "u": "the-three-parts.html", "h": "", "t": "#Three instruments in one INK has three engines. You know them as tabs 1, 2 and 3. Each engine is a complete instrument. 1234 Three engines, each with its own sound, MIDI channel and effects. They meet at Main.1Engine 1, a Bass 2Engine 2, a Pad 3Engine 3 4The mixer, where they meet Each engine has: its own sound, its own notes and patterns, its own MIDI channel, its own effects. They meet at Main, and that is what comes out of your speaker. Each engine can play up to eight notes at once. Each of those is a voice. Three engines make 24 voices in all. Careful The word engine turns up in one more place. In the Library, the Engine filter picks a kind of sound generator, not Engine 1, 2 or 3. Engine 2 is a slot. Wavetable is a kind of tone."}, {"c": "The three engines", "u": "the-three-parts.html", "h": "", "t": "#Four ways to make a tone Every sound starts with a sound generator. You pick one from the drop-down at the top of the Sound tab. 12 The generator drop-down, with Supersaw chosen.1Generator drop-down 2Four choices Supersaw: a thick stack of saw waves, slightly out of tune with each other. Big pads and bright leads. Pulse: a hollow, square-ish wave whose width you can change. Bass and reedy leads. Wavetable: a bank of eight wave shapes. You slide across them. Organs, voices, FM bells. Granular sampler: plays recordings as a cloud of tiny grains. Your own sounds, stretched and scattered. The same knobs do different jobs in each. Chapter 11 goes through them. The sampler has chapters of its own, starting at chapter 21."}, {"c": "The three engines", "u": "the-three-parts.html", "h": "", "t": "#From finger to speaker Follow one note on its trip. 12345678 A note's trip. Every box has a chapter of its own.1You play 2Generator 3Envelope 4Filter 5Chorus / Phaser 6Delay 7Reverb 8Mix, then level and Main Your touch, a MIDI keyboard or a pattern makes the note. The generator turns it into a tone. The tone passes through a row of shaping stops: Envelope: how the note starts and fades (more in chapter 12). Filter: how bright it is (more in chapter 13). Chorus / Phaser: width and swirl (more in chapter 14). Delay: echoes (more in chapter 15). Reverb: space (more in chapter 16). Last stops: the MIX section, the engine's level, then Main (more in chapter 16). You don't need any of that today. It is here so you know where things live when you go looking. Under the hood The envelope and a pressure-sensitive low-pass gate (more in chapter 11) shape each voice first. The filter then colours every voice before they join the engine's sound bus. After that come distortion and mix controls, a high-pass control, then one shared Chorus or Phaser, then Delay and Reverb. Level and mute work on the finished sound, tails included. Each engine keeps its own effects and tails. In an AUv3 host, an engine can leave through its own output instead of Main (more in chapter 33). A raw sample preview takes a separate path too. It skips the sound's granular playback and effects."}, {"c": "The three engines", "u": "the-three-parts.html", "h": "", "t": "#A knob is a starting point Every knob sets a base value. That is where the control rests. Later you will wire things to a knob, such as your pressure, a slow wobble or an envelope. They push the value up and down from its base. The number you hear is the base and the push together. The knob itself doesn't move. 12 The knob shows only the base value. Beside it, a slow wobble (an LFO strip) shows the movement that gets added on top.1Base value 2Movement An example: Cutoff set low, with a slow wobble sent to it. The knob stays where it is. The sound opens and closes anyway. That wiring is the Matrix (more in chapter 17), and the wobble is an LFO (more in chapter 19)."}, {"c": "The three engines", "u": "the-three-parts.html", "h": "", "t": "#What belongs where INK keeps six kinds of things. It helps to know which is which when you save. 123456 Six kinds of things in INK, each with its own screen.1Sound 2Sample 3Kit 4Pattern 5Project 6Settings Sound: one engine's tone, movement and effects. You met it in chapter 1. Sample: a piece of audio you recorded or brought in. Kit: eight sample slots. Pattern: notes you recorded, with their own timing. Project: all three engines, their keys, the mixer and the patterns. Settings: your preferences, such as theme, touch feel and MIDI channels. Inside an AUv3 host there is one more: the host session. INK's state travels inside your host's saved song. The practical difference Save a favourite bass as a sound and you get a reusable bass. Save a project and you keep that bass together with the pad, the patterns and the mix that make the song. Under the hood Each engine's Root and Scale (its key) belong to the project, not the sound. Loading a new sound keeps your key (more in chapter 6). A pattern's timing is also its own, so it stays the same when you change sound."}, {"c": "The three engines", "u": "the-three-parts.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS On tab 1, open the Library and load a Bass. On tab 2, load a Pad. Play both, switching tabs. On each engine, open the Sound tab and turn Cutoff down. Turn Macro A on each. Same knobs, different sounds."}, {"c": "Find your way", "u": "find-your-way.html", "h": "", "t": "INK has a lot of controls, and they all live in the same few places. Learn those places once and you will never feel lost. This page is the map you can come back to."}, {"c": "Find your way", "u": "find-your-way.html", "h": "", "t": "#Along the top 123456789 The whole screen, with the Arp tab open.1Settings 2Key 3Hold 4Octave arrows 5Engine tabs 6Project 7Fold handle 8Page tabs 9Knobs handle AT A GLANCEAlong the top SettingsThe cog, left of the key. Opens your preferences. KeyThe key's root and scale. Tap it for the key menu (more in chapter 6). HoldKeeps notes ringing after you let go (more in chapter 7). Octave down / Octave upArrows beside Hold that move the engine up or down by octaves (more in chapter 6). 1 2 3 AllThe engine tabs. A small dot on a tab means that engine's pattern is playing. A dashed ring means it is armed to start. ProjectA folder icon and the project's name, at the right of the tabs. An asterisk after the name means you changed it (more in chapter 32). Below the card you find four page tabs: Sound, Matrix, Arp and Seq. We look at those in a moment. The chevron at the end of that row is the Knobs handle (more below)."}, {"c": "Find your way", "u": "find-your-way.html", "h": "", "t": "#Pick an engine, or play them all Tap 1, 2 or 3 to work on one engine. You get its keyboard and its four tabs. Tap All to open the All page. You play and mix the three engines there together. 12345 The All page. Engine 3 is on top, engine 1 at the bottom.1Column headings A, B, Level 2Mute 3Key 4Hold 5Rows up / Rows down Each engine has a row at the top with its macro knobs, Level and Mute. Under the mixer, each engine has its own keyboard band, with its key, its own Hold and a pair of arrows, Rows up and Rows down. Those arrows slide a band up and down the grid's eight rows. The band's heading says which ones you see, such as \"rows 3-6\". Nothing is lost. The hidden rows are still there. Tip Mute only makes an engine quiet. Its arp and pattern keep going underneath, so it comes back in time."}, {"c": "Find your way", "u": "find-your-way.html", "h": "", "t": "#Four tabs, four jobs Every engine page has the same four tabs. 1234 Sound, Matrix, Arp and Seq.1Sound 2Matrix 3Arp 4Seq Sound holds the tone, the filter, the envelopes and the effects (more in chapters 11 to 16). Matrix wires things together. For example, it lets your pressure open the filter (more in chapter 17). Arp decides how held notes are played: Free, Arp or Strum (more in chapters 24 to 26). Seq records and edits patterns (more in chapters 27 and 28). The Sound tab is long, so it scrolls. Tap a section title, such as FILTER, and the section list opens. Choose a name and you jump there. The Matrix works the same way. 12 Tap a section title to open the list.1Section title, pinned at the top 2The list of sections"}, {"c": "Find your way", "u": "find-your-way.html", "h": "", "t": "#Make room There are two handles. They do different jobs. 12 The two handles: the chevron folds the card, Knobs trades the grid for the controls.1Fold handle 2Knobs handle The chevron at the card's right end folds the card (waveform, level bar and voice dashes) to one thin row. Tap it again to open the card. The Knobs handle sits at the end of the tab row. Drag it up and the controls take more of the page, and the grid shrinks. Drag it down to get the grid back. On Sound and Arp it stops halfway first, so you see half the grid. When the grid is half-size, two small buttons switch between its upper four rows and its lower four. None of this changes the sound. It only changes what you see."}, {"c": "Find your way", "u": "find-your-way.html", "h": "", "t": "#Turn knobs well Drag a knob up or down. Go slowly for small changes. Sweep quickly to cover a lot of ground. For tiny steps, rest your finger on the knob for a moment before you move. With a mouse, hold Shift as you drag. If you swipe quickly and start on a knob, the page scrolls instead. A slow drag turns the knob. 12 Cutoff after a drag (left), and after a double-tap, back at the value the sound was saved with (right).1After a drag 2After a double-tap: home Double-tap a knob and it jumps back to its reference value, the setting the sound was saved with. That is not the same as zero. You can try any experiment and double-tap your way home. Careful In the Matrix, a double-tap on a cell clears it to zero instead (more in chapter 17)."}, {"c": "Find your way", "u": "find-your-way.html", "h": "", "t": "#Settings Tap the cog. The Settings sheet has a few things you will want early. 123 Settings: the look, the MIDI channel per engine and the playing surface per engine.1Paper, Dark, Blueprint 2MIDI channel 3Grid, Keys, Chords KEYBOARD. Choose Grid, Keys or Chords for each engine. That is its playing surface. Changing it turns that engine's Hold off. MIDI. Each engine has a channel, 1 to 16, or Off. By default Engine 1 uses 1, Engine 2 uses 2 and Engine 3 uses 3. PAPER. Choose Paper, Dark or Blueprint. It changes how INK looks, not how it sounds. TOUCH. How the pads and keys feel under your finger (more in chapter 7). 123 The same Filter section in Paper, Dark and Blueprint.1Paper 2Dark 3Blueprint Under ABOUT INK you find What's new. Close the sheet with Done."}, {"c": "Find your way", "u": "find-your-way.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS Tap the cog. Under KEYBOARD, set Engine 2 to Keys. Tap Done. Open the cog again and choose the Dark theme. Tap the chevron to fold the card, then tap it again. On the Sound tab, turn Cutoff right down. Double-tap it. It jumps back. Tap All and slide Engine 1's band with Rows up."}, {"c": "Undo and redo", "u": "undo-and-redo.html", "h": "", "t": "Turned a knob too far? Swipe, and it is back. INK remembers what you did, so you can try anything."}, {"c": "Undo and redo", "u": "undo-and-redo.html", "h": "", "t": "#Swipe in from the edge To undo, put a finger on the left edge of the screen and drag inwards. A little hexagon grows out of the edge and fills with ink. When it is full, let go. Your last change is undone. To redo, do the same from the right edge. 123 Undo in three snapshots: Cutoff at the start, turned down, and back after a swipe from the left edge. (The hexagon itself is not drawn here.)1Before 2Turned down 3After undo 12 Redo, from the right edge: Cutoff after the undo, then turned down again.1After undo 2After redo Changed your mind halfway? Pull back before you let go, and nothing happens. When the hexagon is full, your device gives one light tap. If there is nothing to undo, the bubble only nudges."}, {"c": "Undo and redo", "u": "undo-and-redo.html", "h": "", "t": "#In a host app Host apps like AUM use the screen edges themselves, so the edge swipe may never reach INK there. Swipe with three fingers instead, anywhere on the screen. Three fingers left undoes. Three fingers right redoes. It works outside a host too. 12 Three fingers left to undo, three fingers right to redo.1Left: undo 2Right: redo"}, {"c": "Undo and redo", "u": "undo-and-redo.html", "h": "", "t": "#What it keeps INK remembers your last 20 changes. Close the app and it forgets them. One swipe undoes one whole change, not every wobble of your finger on the way there. A turn of a knob is one change. So is a dice roll in the Matrix. Under the hood A change counts as one step once you have stopped for about a third of a second. These things never become steps: which page you are looking at, which engine is selected, Hold and its notes, the output level, notes you play, and a few settings you will meet later, such as Quantize (more in chapter 27) and Follow (more in chapter 30)."}, {"c": "Undo and redo", "u": "undo-and-redo.html", "h": "", "t": "#Other undos Undo and redo here cover your sound and settings. Two places have an undo of their own: 123 Three undos: the whole instrument, Seq patterns, and sample imports.1Swipe: the instrument 2Seq: Undo button 3Kit view (Undo appears after an import) The Seq tab has an Undo button for pattern edits (more in chapter 28). The sample kit has an Undo for the samples you just imported (more in chapter 23). Careful Undo is not a backup. It forgets when you close the app, and it cannot bring back files you deleted. Tip Found something good? Save it as new (more in chapter 31). Undo is for exploring. A saved sound is still there tomorrow."}, {"c": "Undo and redo", "u": "undo-and-redo.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS Open the Sound tab and turn Cutoff right down. Swipe in from the left edge. Let go when the hexagon is full. Cutoff jumps back. Swipe in from the right edge. Cutoff goes down again. Try a half swipe, then pull back. Nothing changes."}, {"c": "Key and scale", "u": "key-and-scale.html", "h": "", "t": "Every pad in INK plays a note of one key. Change the key and the same pads play different notes. It takes two taps."}, {"c": "Key and scale", "u": "key-and-scale.html", "h": "", "t": "#Read the key Look at the top left, next to the cog. That is the engine's key, written like this: C major. The letter is where the music is based. The word is the scale. 123 The key sits beside the cog. Tap it.1Key 2Hold 3Octave arrows Each engine has its own key. Tap 1, 2 or 3 and the name changes with it (more in chapter 30)."}, {"c": "Key and scale", "u": "key-and-scale.html", "h": "", "t": "#Open the key menu Tap the key. A small card drops down: the key menu. Tap anywhere outside it to close it. 123 The key menu: Root on the left, Scale on the right.1Root 2Scale 3Lock Two knobs do the work here, and you turn them like any other knob. Root picks the home note. Scale picks the notes around it. The rest of the card (the padlock, the Free Arp Strum switch and a row called Follow) has its own chapters. They come later (chapter 24 and chapter 30)."}, {"c": "Key and scale", "u": "key-and-scale.html", "h": "", "t": "#Root: the home note Root is the note the scale is built on. Turn it and the name changes: C, C#, D, and so on up to B. Root moves in whole notes. To tune a sound a hair up or down, to match a record or a friend's out-of-tune piano, turn Pitch on the Sound tab instead (chapter 11). Pitch tunes the sound. Root is the key, and the pads always show the key's notes. Notes from a MIDI keyboard play as they come in. Root moves what you play on INK's own Grid, Keys and Chords, not the notes a controller sends. Careful Root covers one octave only. To go higher or lower, use the octave arrows, not Root."}, {"c": "Key and scale", "u": "key-and-scale.html", "h": "", "t": "#Octave: higher and lower Beside Hold you find two arrows, Octave down and Octave up. They move the whole engine up or down by an octave. A small number between the arrows tells you where you are. 123 Octave arrows at 0, then one octave up.1Octave down 2Octave number 3Octave up On the Grid they move the engine up to three octaves either way. Keys treats the arrows differently (more in chapter 8). Chords simply move up and down (more in chapter 9). Tip Root changes the key. Octave changes the height. A bass and a lead can share a key and still sit three octaves apart."}, {"c": "Key and scale", "u": "key-and-scale.html", "h": "", "t": "#Scale: the notes you get Scale decides which notes exist. The Grid has no wrong notes in a scale, because every pad is already in it. INK has 27 of them. Turn Scale and the name changes at once. Here they are in families. 123456 The 27 scales, in six families. The names are as the app prints them.1Seven-note scales and modes 2Pentatonics 3Japanese scales 4Blues 5Special layouts 6Chromatic Modes. Major, Minor, Harmonic Min, Dorian, Phrygian, Lydian, Mixolydian, Aeolian and Locrian. Pentatonics. Penta Maj and Penta Min. Five notes only. Hard to play badly. Japanese scales. Hirajoshi, Insen, Iwato and Minyo. Open and a little sad. Blues. Blues Min and Blues Maj. Special layouts. Fifths, Triad Maj, Triad Min, Romanian, Wholetone, Diminished, Harmonics, Hexany and Just. Chromatic. Chromatic has every note, black and white, with nothing left out. A known typo The app spells the last mode \"Lacrian\" on screen, so look for that spelling in the list. It is a mistake in the label, and the scale is Locrian. If you are not sure, start with Major or Minor. Try a pentatonic for easy, pleasant jamming. 12 The same Grid in Major, then in Hirajoshi. The bottom-left root stays. The other roots move, because Hirajoshi has fewer notes.1Root pad 2Same pad, new note Under the hood Every Scale is a list of steps, so the pads climb in scale steps, not in fixed semitones. A setting that gives one interval in Major can give a different one in a pentatonic. Chapter 7 shows what that means for the Grid."}, {"c": "Key and scale", "u": "key-and-scale.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSSame pad, three scales On the Grid, play the third pad up in the first column. Remember how it sounds. Tap the key. Turn Scale to Minor. Tap outside the card and play the same pad. Open the key menu again. Choose Penta Min. Play the pad. Choose Hirajoshi. Play the pad. Set Scale back to Major, then tap Octave up. Play it once more."}, {"c": "Play the Grid", "u": "play-the-grid.html", "h": "", "t": "The Grid is the surface you started on. It looks like a plain block of pads. It is also a very sensitive thing. Move, press and roll your finger, and the note follows."}, {"c": "Play the Grid", "u": "play-the-grid.html", "h": "", "t": "#Eight strings Each column of pads is a string. Eight strings side by side, eight pads high. Notes rise as you go up a string. 1234 Eight strings. The dark pads are the root of the key.1String 1 2String 8 3Root pad 4Higher notes The dark pads are the root, the home note from chapter 6. Find them when you feel lost. You can touch several strings at once. Put a finger on each and you are playing a chord."}, {"c": "Play the Grid", "u": "play-the-grid.html", "h": "", "t": "#Slide, press and roll A finger keeps its voice: put it down, slide it, and the note comes along. Crossing from one string to the next does not start a new attack. It is one gesture. So position picks the pitch. How you touch shapes the sound. Press harder, or roll your finger flatter, and INK reads more of your fingertip. 123 The card while you play one pad: a light touch, then a firm one. Pressure shows in the level bar and the trace.1Light touch: level bar low 2Firm touch: level bar up 3The trace swings wide Moving left or right does not change the pressure. You stay on one note and shape the tone with the weight of your finger. Try it Hold one pad. Roll your finger from the tip to the pad of it. Then slide up a string, keeping your finger down."}, {"c": "Play the Grid", "u": "play-the-grid.html", "h": "", "t": "#Change the layout Two knobs on the Sound tab change how the Grid is laid out. You find them in the OSC section. Stride sets the gap between neighbouring strings. Try a small Stride and the strings sit close, like steps on a stair. A big one spreads them wide apart. Degree slides the whole layout up or down the scale, so the first string starts on a different note. 123 Stride and Degree. Two states of the same Grid each: Stride 5 and 7, Degree 0 and 2.1Stride 2Degree 3Pad that moved Both count in steps of the scale, so the same Stride gives other intervals in other scales. Listen after you change scale. Tip If the Grid feels cramped, raise Stride. If you want a lower first note, lower Degree."}, {"c": "Play the Grid", "u": "play-the-grid.html", "h": "", "t": "#Glide and Microtone Two more knobs sit beside them. Glide sets how long the sound takes to reach a new pitch. At zero, notes arrive straight away. Turn it up and they swoop into each other. Microtone sets how free the pitch is between the Grid's notes. 12 Glide at zero, then Glide at about 60%.1Glide 2Microtone Turn them up one at a time. Microtone is how the pitch behaves between notes. Glide is how long it takes to get there. They sound different, and it helps to hear each alone."}, {"c": "Play the Grid", "u": "play-the-grid.html", "h": "", "t": "#Keep notes with Hold Tap Hold, play a note, and lift your finger. The note stays. You tried this in chapter 2. Here is the whole story. 1234 Hold off, Hold on with notes latched, and Hold off with notes still held (the button pale).1Hold off 2Hold on 3Hold off, notes still held 4Latched pads The Grid holds one note per string, up to eight in all. Play a new note on a held string and it replaces the old one. To let one note go, give its pad a quick tap. To let everything go, double-tap Hold. Tails and effects may ring on for a moment. Each engine has its own Hold. Touch a held pad and keep your finger down, and you play its expression. The note stays latched when you lift. Turn Hold off and the notes already latched stay. No new ones latch. The button stays pale while any are still held. Play on a string that has a held note, with Hold off, and your finger borrows that voice. It gives the note back when you lift. Try it Latch a low note and two high ones. Turn Hold off. Play a melody over the top with your free finger."}, {"c": "Play the Grid", "u": "play-the-grid.html", "h": "", "t": "#Tune your touch Not every finger and every sound feel the same. On an iPhone or iPad, tap the cog and look under TOUCH. There is a box you can play in, and three sliders. 1 Settings, TOUCH, as the Mac shows it: one Mouse expression slider. On iPhone and iPad this spot holds the play box and the three sliders instead.1Mouse expression Sensitivity: how easily your touch makes expression. Move it toward More if a light touch does too little. Smoothness: Less follows your finger exactly. More irons out jumps, and also makes it a little slower to react. Curve: from Heavy to Light. It decides how your finger's range is spread across the sound. Play in the box, light then firm on one spot, and watch the drawing as you move a slider. Change one slider at a time. Aim for a light touch that speaks clearly and a firm one that reaches the sound's strongest expression. Careful This Sensitivity is about your finger. The Sound tab has another one, for the envelope (more in chapter 12)."}, {"c": "Play the Grid", "u": "play-the-grid.html", "h": "", "t": "#On a Mac With a mouse you click one point at a time. The click plays at the level of a slider called Mouse expression, found in the same place in Settings. A sound that relies on pressure can seem flat with a mouse until you raise it. (On a Mac, this slider is all you see under TOUCH.) Click the Grid so it has the keyboard, then use A S D F G H J K on your computer keyboard to play the eight strings."}, {"c": "Play the Grid", "u": "play-the-grid.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSGrid tricks Play a pad and roll your finger from tip to flat. Listen to the tone change. Slide up a string with your finger down. On the Sound tab, turn Glide up to about 60%. Slide again. Tap Hold. Play a low note and two high ones. Lift. Tap Hold off. Play a tune on top. Double-tap Hold to clear it all."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "Keys is a row of keys you can slide along. Think of a violin fingerboard or a bendy guitar neck more than a piano. Same sounds as the Grid, a different way to play them."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "#Switch to Keys Tap the cog. Under KEYBOARD, set the engine you want to Keys. Tap Done. Each engine can have its own surface, so you can keep a Grid on Engine 1 and Keys on Engine 2. 12 The Keys surface on a phone: two rows of keys, with the root key shaded.1Two rows of keys 2Root key The keys show the notes of the current scale (see chapter 6). The root key is marked. Everything on the Sound tab still works as before."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "#Slide and wobble Press a key. Now slide sideways. The pitch moves with your finger, from key to key, with no break between. (That is the default. Piano plays each key as a new note instead, see below.) Rock your finger a little around a note and you get vibrato. Small moves make a gentle wobble. Bigger ones leave the note behind. Move your finger up the key and the sound gets more expressive. Move down and it eases off. Pressure counts too, so it is not a simple height control. 12 Up the key means more expression, down means less.1Press higher: more 2Press lower: less Try it Pick a Lead. Hold one key, wobble sideways a tiny bit, then slide to the next key. Do it again with more pressure."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "#See more keys On a phone you see two rows of keys at once. To see others, use the Octave down and Octave up arrows. On Keys they move the keys you can see. They do not transpose the sound the way they do on the Grid. 12 The Octave arrows on Keys with the range you can see (here 2-3). When a note sounds out of view, the arrow on that side flickers.1Octave up arrow 2Range shown If a note sounds outside the keys you can see, the arrow on that side flickers. Tap it to follow."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "#Slide control: how a slide lands When you slide on Keys, something has to decide what the pitch does between the keys and where it ends up when you stop. That is Slide control. Tap the cog. Under KEYBOARD, open the fold called Slide control. The three choices sit under the practice box. FIGURE TO COMEch08-slide-choicesSlide control at Piano, In tune and Free. Settle shows only under In tune. Slide control at Piano, In tune and Free. Settle shows only under In tune.1Piano 2In tune 3Free 4Settle 5Practice box ChoiceWhat a slide doesGood for PianoEach key you cross starts a new note. The pitch never bends between keys.Runs and glissandos, playing like a piano. In tuneThe pitch follows your finger. When you stop, it settles onto the key under your finger. This is the default.Expressive leads that still end in tune. FreeThe pitch follows your finger and stays where you stop.Long bends, notes between the notes on purpose. All three start in tune: wherever you land on a key, you hear its note. Vibrato comes through on In tune and Free. On Piano the pitch stays on the key, so a wobble does nothing. Fast trills between two keys stay in tune on every choice."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "#Settle Under In tune sits Settle. Toward Quick, a stopped slide snaps onto its note in a few hundredths of a second. Toward Slow, it drifts there over about a second. It starts near Quick. Settle only changes what happens after you stop. Slides and vibrato sound the same at every setting."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it in the practice box Above the three choices is a box with keys in it. Play in it and it draws your finger's path as a thin pencil line. A thicker ink line shows what the sound does with your settings. Tap the play button at its top left to replay your last movement. Tap the small circling arrow at the top right to clear it. 12 The practice box, before you play. Keys sit along the bottom. Play and clear sit at the top.1Play 2Clear The box plays with your sound's own Glide, and shows it at the top, for example Glide 0 %. To change it, turn Glide on the Sound tab. Under the hood The box uses Engine 1's key and a copy of its sound, so you can test without touching your music. The choice you make is for every engine on Keys."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "#Slide control, Glide and Microtone Three things sound alike and belong to different places. FIGURE TO COMEch08-slide-glide-microtoneThree controls that move pitch, and where each one lives. Three controls that move pitch, and where each one lives.1Slide control: Settings 2Glide: Sound tab 3Microtone: Sound tab Slide control is in Settings. It decides how the keys turn your finger into pitch. Glide is on the Sound tab. It is how long the sound takes to travel (more in chapter 7). Microtone is on the Sound tab too. It is how free the pitch is between notes. In tune cannot cancel a long Glide. The key may be in tune while the sound is still on its way there."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "#Hold on Keys Tap Hold, play a key and lift. It stays, on its key's note. On Piano, slide across several keys with Hold on and only the key you lift on stays. Touch a held key again and you play it. Your finger edits its expression and it stays held. A quick tap lets it go. Double-tap Hold to clear everything. Careful A phone follows five fingers at once. Put down a sixth and iOS stops following all of them. With Hold off, every note ends. With Hold on, they stay held. Tip For a steady pad, use In tune and a modest press. Latch the notes, then play with Macro A and Macro B. For a solo, try Free, shorten Release (more in chapter 12), and start with small sideways moves."}, {"c": "Play Keys", "u": "play-keys.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSHear the three choices Open the cog and set the engine to Keys. Open KEYBOARD, then the Slide control fold. Choose Piano. In the practice box slide from the root key to the key two along. You hear each key. Choose In tune. Slide the same way and stop a little past the key. The pitch settles onto it. Choose Free. Slide and stop the same way. The pitch stays where you stopped. Back on In tune, move Settle to Slow and stop past a key again."}, {"c": "Play Chords", "u": "play-chords.html", "h": "", "t": "On the Chords surface one finger plays a whole chord. You don't pick the notes. You pick a spot, and INK builds the chord for you, in your key."}, {"c": "Play Chords", "u": "play-chords.html", "h": "", "t": "#Switch to Chords Tap the cog. Under KEYBOARD, set an engine to Chords. Tap Done. The pads are replaced by a block of tiles. 12345 The Chords surface in C Major. Seven columns, four rows.1Degree numerals 2Row 4 3Row 1 4A tile 5Rails and Spice Each rectangle is a tile. The name of the chord is written on it. Read it as you play. Under the tiles you see two bars of small boxes and a button called Spice. They dress each chord (more in chapter 10)."}, {"c": "Play Chords", "u": "play-chords.html", "h": "", "t": "#Degrees across The columns are degrees: the steps of your scale, from the root. The numerals above the tiles say which. Capitals mean a major chord, small letters a minor one, and a small circle a diminished one. In C Major the columns are C, D minor, E minor, F, G, A minor and B diminished. Change the key and the chords change with it. Most scales give seven columns. Diminished gives eight. 1 Diminished is the one scale with eight columns.1Eighth column"}, {"c": "Play Chords", "u": "play-chords.html", "h": "", "t": "#Colour going up Up the rows, the chords get richer. Row 1 is at the bottom, row 4 at the top. RowWhat you get 1Plain, familiar chords. Start here. 2The same chord with a fourth note, such as a seventh or a ninth. 3Richer and more varied. INK chooses. 4The most colourful. The chord can move on from press to press. Rows 1 and 2 are a steady base. Use them to lay down a progression. 12 The same column in two rows: row 1 is a plain chord, row 2 adds a note.1Row 1 tile 2Row 2 tile"}, {"c": "Play Chords", "u": "play-chords.html", "h": "", "t": "#Rows 3 and 4 show what comes next On rows 3 and 4 the name on the tile is the chord your next touch will play. Play it and the tile changes to a new name. So these rows never promise the same chord twice. 12 A row 3 tile before and after you play it. The name moves on.1Name before 2Name after The choice depends on the scale, on what you played just before, and on the chord engine (more in chapter 10). Play a short run of chords and listen to how they lead into each other. One lonely chord can't show that. Tip Row 4 can build up. Press the same tile again and the chord can deepen. It uses at most six notes."}, {"c": "Play Chords", "u": "play-chords.html", "h": "", "t": "#Play it with your finger A tile strikes the moment you touch it, and harder presses play louder. Slide your finger to another tile and the chord changes, with the notes gliding to their new places. If you lift and play again soon, the next chord starts close to the last one. Chord changes sound smooth and connected. A chord uses several of the engine's eight voices. It goes through your sound, filter, matrix and effects like any other notes. Short plucks make the rhythm obvious. Long pads let you hear the chords flow together. Other engines can follow the chords you play (more in chapter 30). The octave arrows move the chords up and down. Under the hood Chords in Chromatic keep the last scale you used as their harmonic context. Chromatic itself has no chord set of its own."}, {"c": "Play Chords", "u": "play-chords.html", "h": "", "t": "#Hold and the chord piano Tap Hold, play a tile and lift. The chord stays. A quick tap on the held tile lets it go. Double-tap Hold to clear everything. With Hold off, a held chord keeps sounding. A new touch borrows its voices and the old chord returns when you lift (as on the Grid, chapter 7). Want to see what you are playing? Tap the waveform in the card above the surface. It turns into a keyboard. This is the chord piano. 12 Tap the waveform and the card shows the notes sounding as dots on keys. The root is ringed.1Piano in place of the waveform 2Dots on sounding notes A dot sits on every note that is sounding now. Held notes count. The root of the chord is ringed. Tap the piano again to get the waveform back. The piano only shows on an engine's own page, not on All."}, {"c": "Play Chords", "u": "play-chords.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSPlay I, vi, IV, V Set the key to C Major (see chapter 6) and the engine's surface to Chords. Row 1: play the first, sixth, fourth and fifth columns in turn. C, A minor, F, G. Play the same four on row 2. Hear the extra note. Tap the waveform. Watch the dots as you play. Tap Hold, play a chord and lift. It keeps sounding. Double-tap Hold to clear it."}, {"c": "Chord colour", "u": "chord-colour.html", "h": "", "t": "The tiles decide which chord you play. Three controls under the tiles decide how it is dressed: where its notes sit, how wide it spreads, and how much colour it carries."}, {"c": "Chord colour", "u": "chord-colour.html", "h": "", "t": "#Meet the rails Look under the tiles. Two bars sit left and right, with a button between them. 1234 Under the tiles: Voicing on the left, Spice in the middle, Spread on the right.1Voicing rail 2Spice 3Spread rail 4Engine name The two bars are the rails. Each is a row of eight boxes. Drag your thumb along one and the boxes fill. Rails stay where you leave them. No Hold needed."}, {"c": "Chord colour", "u": "chord-colour.html", "h": "", "t": "#Voicing: where the notes sit Voicing changes how the notes of a chord are arranged. Raise it and the bass note changes, and the chord turns over. It is the same chord with a different note at the bottom, called an inversion. 123 The same chord with Voicing low, then high. The piano shows the notes moving.1Voicing low 2Voicing high 3Lowest note changes Try it with the chord piano open (chapter 9). Same chord, new arrangement."}, {"c": "Chord colour", "u": "chord-colour.html", "h": "", "t": "#Spread: how wide Spread makes the chord wider. A low setting keeps the notes close. A high one stretches them and doubles some in other octaves, which makes a bigger, fuller sound. Higher rows react more strongly. 123 Spread low, then high. The dots reach further.1Spread low 2Spread high 3Wider spacing"}, {"c": "Chord colour", "u": "chord-colour.html", "h": "", "t": "#Read at the strike Voicing and Spread are read at the strike. Set them, then play the chord. Moving a rail while a chord rings changes nothing you hear. It prepares the next one. That lets you set the next arrangement while the last chord is still going. To bring a rail home, tap exactly on its value, quickly. Or double-tap Hold, which clears both rails and Spice. Rails and Spice are saved with a pattern. Choose a pattern again and they come back (more in chapter 27). Tip Move one rail at a time. Then you know whether you hear new notes, a new register or new spacing."}, {"c": "Chord colour", "u": "chord-colour.html", "h": "", "t": "#Spice Spice adds colour to a chord. Think of the extra notes in a jazz or soul chord: a ninth, a thirteenth, a little tension. Spice picks them for you. It does not swap the chord for another one. It dresses the one you chose. On a dominant (the V chord) it can add altered notes, so not every note is a note of the plain scale. Spice is a button, drawn like Hold. Tap it to switch it on or off. Press and hold it, and it is on only while you press. Hold on latches it, until you tap it again or double-tap Hold. 123 A dominant tile, Spice off and Spice on. The piano shows the added colour.1Spice off 2Spice on 3Added notes Like the rails, Spice shapes the next chord, never one that is sounding. It works on every row. Rows 1 and 2 add one colour note. Rows 3 and 4 get richer chords."}, {"c": "Chord colour", "u": "chord-colour.html", "h": "", "t": "#Smart, Smooth and Stock INK has three ways to pick and voice chords: the chord engine. They don't change the rails or Spice. They change how the chords come out. Smart chooses chords with a model made from real song progressions. It starts here. Great for exploring. Smooth cares most about how each chord leads into the next. Good for pads and flowing changes. Stock is the original method. Each chord is voiced fresh. To switch, swipe sideways on Spice. Right goes to the next, left to the previous. The name appears above Spice for a moment. 123 Swipe on Spice. The engine name shows above it: Smart, Smooth, Stock.1Smart 2Smooth 3Stock A swipe does not turn Spice on or off. The chord that is sounding keeps its notes. The new engine plays from your next touch. It applies to every Chords surface, until you quit INK. 123 What each engine is for.1Smart: explore 2Smooth: flow 3Stock: the original Careful This switch may change before INK is finished. Treat it as something to try, not a promise."}, {"c": "Chord colour", "u": "chord-colour.html", "h": "", "t": "#Chords and the matrix On a Chords surface, the Stride and Degree knobs on the Sound tab are greyed out. Chords place their own notes. In the matrix those two rows become Voicing and Spread, so you can move them with pressure, envelopes and LFOs (more in chapter 17). If an engine's band on the All page is short, the rails and Spice may be hidden. Use the engine's own page."}, {"c": "Chord colour", "u": "chord-colour.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSSpice and engines Set a Chords engine to C Major. Play I, vi, IV, V on row 3, with a pad. The engines differ most on rows 3 and 4. Play the same four with Spice off. Then tap Spice on and play them again. Raise Voicing a little. Play. Then raise Spread. Swipe right on Spice to Smooth. Play the four again. Swipe on to Stock. Does it feel different?"}, {"c": "Oscillators", "u": "oscillators.html", "h": "", "t": "Every sound begins as a raw tone. In a few minutes you will pick that tone, change its character with one knob, then add grit, a second pitch and a little noise."}, {"c": "Oscillators", "u": "oscillators.html", "h": "", "t": "#Pick a generator Open the Sound tab. At the top, beside the two macro knobs, is the drop-down for the sound generator. Your choices are Supersaw, Pulse, Wavetable and Granular sampler. This chapter covers the first three. The sampler has its own chapters, starting at chapter 21. 1234 The same first knob under three generators. Its name changes with the generator.1Generator drop-down 2Supersaw: Detune 3Pulse: Width 4Wavetable: Position Each generator has one main tone knob, right after the drop-down. We call that idea Shape. You never see a knob called Shape on these three. The knob wears the name that fits the generator, and so does its row in the Matrix (more in chapter 17). Try it Hold a note. Pick each generator in turn and listen. Same finger, three characters."}, {"c": "Oscillators", "u": "oscillators.html", "h": "", "t": "#Supersaw: thick Supersaw is a stack of saw waves, each a hair out of tune with the others. The knob is Detune. 12 Detune low, then high. The saws drift apart and the wave gets busier.1Detune low 2Detune high Turn Detune down for a focused bass or a clear melody. Turn it up for pads and wide leads. At the top the outer saws sit 50 cents apart, which is half a note. Tip Big Detune blurs a low chord. If the bass goes muddy, play higher or turn Detune down. Under the hood Each voice stacks four saws, spread evenly around the note. The knob follows a curve, so the small settings have plenty of room to play with. Later you can link your pressure to Detune, so a held note blooms as you press (chapter 17)."}, {"c": "Oscillators", "u": "oscillators.html", "h": "", "t": "#Pulse: hollow and narrow Pulse is a square-ish wave. Its knob is Width. 12 Width at 50%, then at 3%. The trace on the card almost flattens as the pulse gets thin.1Width 50% 2Width 3% At 50% you get a square: strong, hollow, great for bass. Turn Width down toward 3% and the sound gets thin and reedy. For a reed lead, go narrow and add a touch of Glide (more in chapter 7). Tip Later you can let a slow wobble move Width for the classic moving pulse (chapter 19). Set Width in the middle of its range first, so the wobble has room both ways."}, {"c": "Oscillators", "u": "oscillators.html", "h": "", "t": "#Wavetable: pick a bank, slide across it A Wavetable sound reads a row of eight wave shapes. The drop-down under the generator picks the bank. The knob is Position. 12 The bank drop-down open, with all eight banks.1Bank drop-down 2Eight banks 12 Position 1 and Position 8 on the Basic bank: a smooth sine at one end, a thin pulse at the other.1Position 1 2Position 8 Position runs from 1 to 8 and slides smoothly between the waves. It does not play back a sample or scrub through a recording. BankWhat it sounds like BasicSines, triangles, squares, saws, pulses. The clearest place to learn Position. OrganFlute, drawbar, reed and bright organ tones. Keys and chords. AcousticReed, sax, oboe and bowed-string colours. Breathy, alive. VocalHums and vowels. Expressive leads. FMBells, tines and metallic shimmer. Keys and glassy pads. ChipStepped, low-resolution waves. Digital leads and game sounds. FoldProgressively folded shapes. Bright, complex sweeps. GrainGrit, sand and crackle. A wavetable bank, not the sampler. Tip Find a Position you like first. Later, let a gentle movement drift across a small part of the range. Sweeping all eight waves works once, as a special effect. A smaller range is more playable. Sounds imported from Plinky can bring their own wavetable (more in chapter 31)."}, {"c": "Oscillators", "u": "oscillators.html", "h": "", "t": "#Knobs every generator shares The rest of the oscillator section is the same for all three. Most of it adds grit, a second pitch or noise. 123456 The OSC section of the Sound tab. Glide, Degree, Microtone and Stride are playing knobs (chapter 7).1Generator and Shape 2Distort 3Octave 4Interval 5Noise 6LPG Filter AT A GLANCEShared oscillator knobs DistortAdds grit and density. OctaveMoves the whole sound up or down in whole octaves, from -3 to +3. PitchTunes the whole sound up or down, up to an octave, in fine steps. IntervalShifts some of the voice's oscillators by a number of semitones. NoiseMixes in noise. LPG FilterSets the sparkle of the built-in gate. Distort makes the tone thicker and edgier. It can also get louder, and louder always sounds more exciting. Turn the level back before you decide it is better. 12 Distort low, then high. The wave squares off.1Distort low 2Distort high Octave here is a knob on the sound. On the Grid and Chords surfaces, the Octave arrows beside Hold turn this same setting (more in chapter 6). Pitch tunes the sound itself. At 7.0 every note sounds a fifth higher, but the pads, the keys and the chord names still show the key's notes. Small values tune the sound to a record. A wire on Pitch makes a siren or a gentle drift, and it never moves the key. Interval makes an interval inside one voice. It is not an extra key on the keyboard. At 0 the voice has one pitch. At 7 you hear a fifth, hollow and open. At 12 you hear an octave. 12 Interval at 0, "}, {"c": "Oscillators", "u": "oscillators.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSThree generators, one note Hold one note on the Sound tab. Choose Supersaw, then sweep Detune up and down. Choose Pulse, then sweep Width. Park it near the narrow end. Choose Wavetable, sweep Position, then pick the Vocal bank. Turn Interval up to 7, then bring Noise in a little."}, {"c": "The main envelope", "u": "main-envelope.html", "h": "", "t": "Every note has a little life story: it starts, settles, holds and fades. In this chapter you shape that story with four knobs, and watch it drawn as you play."}, {"c": "The main envelope", "u": "main-envelope.html", "h": "", "t": "#See the shape Scroll the Sound tab to ENVELOPE, or use the section list. Hold a note. A drawing next to the knobs traces what the note does. 123456 The ENVELOPE section. The drawing shows the shape, and a dot follows your note along it.1The envelope drawing 2Sensitivity 3Attack 4Decay 5Sustain 6Release This is the main envelope. It works on every note, whatever the generator. The drawing is not a picture of the knobs. It shows what the sound is really doing right now, with your touch included."}, {"c": "The main envelope", "u": "main-envelope.html", "h": "", "t": "#Four stages AT A GLANCEThe four stages AttackHow long the note takes to rise. Short is a strike, long is a swell. DecayHow long it takes to fall from the peak toward Sustain. SustainThe level it settles at while you keep holding. ReleaseHow long it takes to fade after you let go. Say it as a story. Attack is the first rise. Decay is the fall from the peak. Sustain is the level it holds at. Release is the fade after you let go. Sustain is a level, not a time. The other three are times."}, {"c": "The main envelope", "u": "main-envelope.html", "h": "", "t": "#Three recipes The same tone becomes three instruments. Shape the envelope and nothing else. 123 Pluck: a quick strike, low Sustain.1Fast attack 2Decay falls 3Low sustain 123 Pad: a slow swell and a long tail.1Slow attack 2High sustain 3Long release 12 Keys: a clear strike, then the body settles.1Distinct attack 2Body settles SoundAttackDecaySustainRelease PluckQuickClear, mediumLowShort to medium PadLongMediumHigh enough to hold a chordLong KeysQuick, but audibleMediumMedium, not fullShort to medium Tip For a pluck, keep Sustain low and let Decay do the work. For a keys sound, do not leave Sustain at the top, or the note never settles."}, {"c": "The main envelope", "u": "main-envelope.html", "h": "", "t": "#When it seems broken Three things sound like faults. They are just the envelope doing what you asked. A very long Attack can make a short tap seem silent. The note has not finished rising before you let go. A very low Sustain makes a held note fade away while your finger is still down. A long Release keeps a voice busy longer, and can blur a fast chord change. Check the drawing. If the shape shows what you hear, the knob you set is the one to change. The waveform card while notes with a long Release overlap. Each note's tail is still sounding when the next one starts."}, {"c": "The main envelope", "u": "main-envelope.html", "h": "", "t": "#Sensitivity: how much your touch counts Sensitivity sits first in the section. It works on your touch, not on time. It decides how much your pressure counts. Turn it down and you need a firmer press to get a full sound. Turn it up and a light touch is enough. 12 The same light press, with Sensitivity low and then high.1Sensitivity low 2Sensitivity high Settings has a TOUCH box with a Sensitivity slider too, next to Smoothness and Curve. Same name, different job. 12 Two places, two jobs. This picture was made on a Mac, so Settings shows Mouse expression. On your phone the TOUCH box shows Sensitivity, Smoothness and Curve.1Settings, TOUCH (Mac view) 2Sound tab, ENVELOPE The one in Settings adapts to your hand and your device. It is the same for every sound (more in chapter 7). The one on the Sound tab belongs to the sound. A bass, a pad and a lead can each have the range they need. Set the Settings one first. Then give each sound its own. Under the hood The envelope works on the pressure-sensitive low-pass gate as well as on loudness, so Attack and Release shape brightness too. The drawing reads the values the engine really uses for the note you pressed last. Later, when you link things in the Matrix (chapter 17), those values move and the drawing moves with them. Where the real note leaves the line, a pencil band shows the range it covered."}, {"c": "The main envelope", "u": "main-envelope.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSPluck, pad, organ On the Sound tab, load a sustained sound and scroll to ENVELOPE. Pluck: Attack down, Decay medium, Sustain low. Play a note and watch the drawing. Pad: raise Attack and Release, and Sustain high. Play a chord. Organ: Attack down, Sustain right up, Release short. There is a second envelope for making things move. It stays silent until you link it to something (chapter 18)."}, {"c": "The filter", "u": "filter.html", "h": "", "t": "The filter is the most useful part of INK to play with. Turn one knob and a sound goes from a bright buzz to a soft murmur. You tried it in the first lap. Now you will meet the rest of it."}, {"c": "The filter", "u": "filter.html", "h": "", "t": "#Where it lives Open the Sound tab and jump to FILTER. It has the same four knobs and one tick box on every generator, even the sampler. 12345 The FILTER section: a Band-pass box and four knobs.1Band-pass 2Cutoff 3Mode 4Resonance 5Filter Spread This is the SEM-style Filter. It works on each voice after the sound's own gate (the LPG Filter of chapter 11). The two do not replace each other."}, {"c": "The filter", "u": "filter.html", "h": "", "t": "#Cutoff: bright to dark Cutoff is the one to reach for first. In the normal low-pass mode, turn it down and the highs fade away. The sound gets darker and softer. Turn it up and the shine returns. 123 Cutoff wide open, partway down, and nearly closed.1Cutoff high 2Cutoff middle 3Cutoff low The value reads in Hz, then kHz, from the low bass range up to 21 kHz. Careful The top of the knob is not \"filter off\". At 21 kHz the filter is still there. It just sits above almost everything you can hear. Try it Hold a chord and sweep Cutoff slowly from top to bottom, then back."}, {"c": "The filter", "u": "filter.html", "h": "", "t": "#Resonance: make it sing Resonance stresses the sound right at the cutoff. A little adds definition to a sweep. A lot makes a narrow band stand out, and the sound can get louder or thinner. 12 Resonance at 0, then at 60%, with Cutoff in the middle.1Resonance 0 2Resonance 60% Turn Resonance up and sweep Cutoff again. The sound sings around the point you are moving. It stays stable. It rings, but it will not run away into a whistle on its own."}, {"c": "The filter", "u": "filter.html", "h": "", "t": "#Mode: low, notch, high Mode is a slider, not a switch. It blends the filter's three faces. The value reads LP, LP / Notch, Notch, Notch / HP, then HP. 123 Mode at LP, at Notch and at HP.1LP 2Notch 3HP LP keeps the lows and removes the highs. This is the usual dark-and-soft sound. Notch takes a band out of the middle and keeps both sides. It sounds hollow and phasey. HP keeps the highs and removes the lows. The sound gets thin and airy. In this mode, Cutoff turned up takes away more bass. The notch can sound quieter than the ends. Parts of the sound cancel each other, and INK does not hide that. More Resonance makes the notch narrower, not louder. Tip Later you can let a slow wobble move Mode, so the sound keeps changing its balance (chapter 19). Keep the swing small. A full sweep into high-pass takes the bottom out of a sound."}, {"c": "The filter", "u": "filter.html", "h": "", "t": "#Band-pass: only the middle Tick the Band-pass box. The filter now keeps a band around Cutoff and drops the lows and highs on both sides. Resonance gives a clear peak. 12 Band-pass ticked: Mode is greyed out.1Band-pass ticked 2Mode greyed out While Band-pass is on, Mode turns grey, because it is not used. Untick the box and Mode returns, with the value you left it at."}, {"c": "The filter", "u": "filter.html", "h": "", "t": "#Filter Spread: a little different each side Filter Spread nudges each voice's left and right cutoffs apart, in opposite directions. Voices take turns in a fixed pattern. At the top it moves the cutoffs about 15% either way. 12 Filter Spread at 0 and at maximum.1Spread 0 2Spread maximum A little makes a pad less uniform. A lot, with a resonant sweep, gives a clear moving stereo texture. Tip Listen in mono once. If the sound has to anchor your mix, check that it still holds up. Under the hood The filter has two stages and a feedback path, modelled on the circuit of the original module. It is tuned by ear against recordings. Resonance stops short of free self-oscillation on purpose. Band-pass is stored with the sound, like the original's own switch."}, {"c": "The filter", "u": "filter.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSSweep, sing, notch Hold a chord on a sustained sound and scroll to FILTER. Sweep Cutoff down and up. Add Resonance, and sweep again. Slide Mode to the notch, then to HP. Tick Band-pass. Watch Mode grey out. Untick it. Filters like to move. Soon you will link your touch to Cutoff, so the sound opens as you press (more in chapter 17). A sampler texture with a bright, busy top often likes a slow-moving low-pass (more in chapter 21)."}, {"c": "Chorus and phaser", "u": "chorus-and-phaser.html", "h": "", "t": "A chorus makes one note sound like several. A phaser makes it swirl. Both are a few turns away, and a pad is the best place to hear them. Start with a Pad on Engine 1. Open the Sound tab and tap a section title to open the section list, then choose CHORUS / PHASER."}, {"c": "Chorus and phaser", "u": "chorus-and-phaser.html", "h": "", "t": "#One slot, two types Each engine has one slot for this effect. You fill it with a chorus or a phaser. Not both. The drop-down at the top of the section picks which one: Chorus or Phaser. 12345678 The CHORUS / PHASER section with Chorus chosen.1Type drop-down 2Mix 3Center 4Depth 5Rate 6Feedback 7Tone 8Stereo Turn Mix up from zero. At zero the slot does nothing, so the sound passes through untouched. As you raise it, the effect joins in. Tip Start with Mix near 40%, Rate low and Depth in the middle. Slow and gentle sounds like a bigger room. Fast and deep sounds like a wobble. 12 Mix at 0%, where the slot is off, and at 50%.1Mix 0%: no effect 2Mix 50%"}, {"c": "Chorus and phaser", "u": "chorus-and-phaser.html", "h": "", "t": "#The seven knobs AT A GLANCEChorus and Phaser knobs MixHow much of the effect joins the sound. At 0% you hear none of it. CenterWhere the movement is centred. On Phaser this knob is called Sweep. DepthHow far the movement swings around that centre. RateHow fast it moves. Slow is lush. Fast is a wobble. FeedbackSends some of the effect back into itself. It can go negative as well as positive. ToneShapes the colour of the effect itself. StereoHow differently the left and right sides move. Higher values spread the sound wider. The same seven knobs serve both types. Only the units change: milliseconds for the chorus, hertz and octaves for the phaser."}, {"c": "Chorus and phaser", "u": "chorus-and-phaser.html", "h": "", "t": "#Switch to Phaser Open the drop-down and choose Phaser. Watch the knob called Center. It is now called Sweep, and the numbers under the knobs change units. 123 The same section with Phaser chosen. Center has become Sweep.1Type drop-down: Phaser 2Sweep, once Center 3Feedback Play a held chord and listen. The chorus thickens. The phaser sounds hollow and moves like a slow wave."}, {"c": "Chorus and phaser", "u": "chorus-and-phaser.html", "h": "", "t": "#Feedback has a sign Feedback is the one knob that goes below zero. The sign changes the colour of the effect, not just its amount. A negative setting is not a quieter positive one. Try about 30% on each side and compare. On the phaser, more feedback makes it sharper and more vocal. 12 Phaser Feedback, negative and positive.1Negative Feedback 2Positive Feedback Careful A fast, wide sweep on a bass makes the note unsteady. Keep bass sounds gentle, or leave Mix at zero."}, {"c": "Chorus and phaser", "u": "chorus-and-phaser.html", "h": "", "t": "#Where it sits The slot comes after the filter and before the delay and the reverb. So the echoes and the room (chapter 15 and chapter 16) hear the swirl and add to it. 1234 The order: the sound, then Chorus or Phaser, then delay and reverb.1Sound and filter 2Chorus or Phaser 3Delay 4Reverb Later you can link a macro to these knobs, so one turn raises Mix and Depth together (more in chapter 17). Under the hood Above Mix 0 the effect is added to the dry sound, so it gets a little louder as it comes in. Everything below about 120 Hz stays dry, so the bass keeps its weight and the effect cannot hollow it out. In mono, the dry sound is never cancelled. Chorus Center runs from about 2 to 16 ms and Depth up to about 6 ms. Phaser Sweep runs from about 120 to 1,800 Hz and Depth up to about 3.2 octaves. Rate runs from 0.02 to 3 Hz for both. Feedback stops at about 35% (chorus) and 85% (phaser)."}, {"c": "Chorus and phaser", "u": "chorus-and-phaser.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSSwirl a pad On a Pad, open the CHORUS / PHASER section. Make sure Chorus is chosen. Set Mix to about 40%, Rate low and Depth in the middle. Hold a chord. Raise Stereo and listen to the sound spread. Choose Phaser. Compare the swirl with the chorus. Turn Feedback negative, then positive. Which do you like? Double-tap Mix to send it back to the value the sound was saved with."}, {"c": "Delays", "u": "delays.html", "h": "", "t": "A delay repeats what you play. Each engine has one, in three flavours: plain echoes, echoes that play backwards, and echoes that climb higher each time. Open the Sound tab on any engine, tap a section title and choose DELAY."}, {"c": "Delays", "u": "delays.html", "h": "", "t": "#Pick a type The drop-down at the top of the section offers Classic, Reverse and Octave. 123456 The DELAY section with Classic chosen. All five knobs are live.1Delay type 2Send 3Time 42nd Tap 5Wobble 6Feedback Five knobs shape the echoes. Which ones work depends on the type. AT A GLANCEThe delay knobs SendHow much of the sound goes into the delay. At zero, nothing does. TimeThe gap between echoes. Right of zero it is free. Left of zero it locks to a beat. FeedbackHow much of each echo comes back round for another. More Feedback, more repeats. 2nd TapSets the time of the right-hand echo compared with the left. At 100% they match. WobbleMakes the echo time drift, so the echoes waver. Classic only. A send is how an effect is fed. Lower it and no new sound goes in, but the effect still finishes its tail."}, {"c": "Delays", "u": "delays.html", "h": "", "t": "#Time: free or synced Turn Time. On the right of centre the knob reads like \"43 free\". Time is free: you set it by ear. On the left it reads like \"8 /32 sync\". Now the echo locks to a beat, counted in thirty-seconds of a whole note. So 8 /32 is a quarter note, and 16 /32 is a half note. 12 Time on the free side, then on the synced side.1Free: a plain number 2Synced: a /32 label Tip For echoes that answer your tempo, turn Time left and pick a label. The beat itself comes from the Arp and Seq tabs (more in chapter 24)."}, {"c": "Delays", "u": "delays.html", "h": "", "t": "#Classic Classic is the original: straightforward repeats. A short Time thickens the sound. A synced one gives an echo that answers your rhythm. Add Wobble slowly. The echo time starts to drift, and big, fast drift makes the echoes wander in pitch and timing. Keep it small, or leave it at zero for clean repeats. 2nd Tap works with Classic and Octave. Lower it and the right echo comes sooner than the left, so the repeats land at different moments on each side. 12 2nd Tap at 100%, where both sides match, and lower.12nd Tap 100% 22nd Tap lower"}, {"c": "Delays", "u": "delays.html", "h": "", "t": "#Reverse Choose Reverse. The echoes now play backwards, swelling up into each repeat. Time sets how long the backwards phrase is. A very short time and a long one feel like different instruments. Reverse plays Send, Time and Feedback. 2nd Tap and Wobble turn grey, because Reverse does not use them. 123 Reverse: 2nd Tap and Wobble are greyed.1Reverse chosen 22nd Tap, grey 3Wobble, grey Try it on a short, clear pluck with a modest Send. You hear the pluck, then the backwards echo swelling toward the next one."}, {"c": "Delays", "u": "delays.html", "h": "", "t": "#Octave Choose Octave. Each repeat climbs higher than the last. It shines on sparse notes and plucked chords, where the rising tail has room. 2nd Tap works here, and Wobble is grey. 123 Octave: 2nd Tap is live, Wobble is greyed.1Octave chosen 22nd Tap, live 3Wobble, grey With Feedback at zero you hear one rising echo. Raise it and you get more repeats, each one higher than the last. Under the hood Octave's pitch process cannot repeat faster than about 154 ms. If you ask for less on Time or 2nd Tap, it stops at that minimum. If two short settings sound the same, that is why. The synced labels run about 2.4% long: an echo marked 8 /32 comes back slightly late against a host's grid. For exact rhythms, use a free time or a delay in the host. This is a known issue."}, {"c": "Delays", "u": "delays.html", "h": "", "t": "#Which knobs does each type use? TypeSendTimeFeedback2nd TapWobble Classicyesyesyesyesyes Reverseyesyesyesgreygrey Octaveyesyesyesyesgrey A greyed knob keeps its value. Switch back and it returns."}, {"c": "Delays", "u": "delays.html", "h": "", "t": "#Stopping the echoes Lower Send and no new sound goes in, but echoes already in the delay keep going. Lower Feedback to make them die away sooner. The delay also feeds the reverb. So when you add reverb (chapter 16), a delay tail gets softer and more spread out."}, {"c": "Delays", "u": "delays.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSRising echoes Play a low, short chord. Choose Octave. Set Send to a modest amount and Feedback to the middle. Play once and let the tail rise before you play again. Turn 2nd Tap down a little. The two sides separate. Switch to Reverse and play a short pluck. Hear the echo swell backwards. Switch to Classic, turn Time left and pick a /32 label."}, {"c": "Reverb and mix", "u": "reverb-and-mix.html", "h": "", "t": "Reverb is the room around the sound. The MIX section is where you set how loud, how wet, how low and how wide the finished sound is before it leaves its engine. Open the Sound tab, tap a section title and choose REVERB. Play a chord while you turn the knobs."}, {"c": "Reverb and mix", "u": "reverb-and-mix.html", "h": "", "t": "#Reverb 1234 The REVERB section: four knobs.1Send 2Time 3Shimmer 4Wobble AT A GLANCEReverb SendHow much of the sound goes into the room. At zero, no new sound enters it. TimeHow long the tail rings. Short is a small room. Long is a hall. ShimmerAdds pitched, rising sparkle to the tail. WobbleAdds movement to the tail. Shimmer is the unusual one. Turn Shimmer up slowly and the tail starts to glow above your chord. Too much and it covers the chord. 12 Shimmer at zero, then turned well up.1Shimmer 0 2Shimmer high For an intimate keys sound, use low Send, modest Time and no Shimmer. For an evolving pad, raise Time first. Then add Shimmer until it supports the chord. Tip Long tails carry across chord changes. Judge them while you play a progression, not on one note. The delay feeds the reverb too (chapter 15). So a delay tail gets softer and wider when you add reverb."}, {"c": "Reverb and mix", "u": "reverb-and-mix.html", "h": "", "t": "#Mix Now choose MIX in the section list. These four knobs finish the sound. 1234 The MIX section: four knobs that finish the sound.1Synth Lvl 2Main Wet/Dry 3High Pass 4Stereo Width AT A GLANCEMix Synth LvlHow loud the raw synth is inside the engine's own mix. Main Wet/DryThe balance between the dry sound and the delay and reverb. Centre leaves both as they are. High PassCuts low notes out of the sound. At zero it does nothing you can hear. Stereo WidthFrom mono (0%) to the normal stereo picture (100%)."}, {"c": "Reverb and mix", "u": "reverb-and-mix.html", "h": "", "t": "#Main Wet/Dry Main Wet/Dry sits in the middle for most sounds. There both are full. Turn it left and the echoes and room fade, leaving the dry sound. Turn it right and the dry sound fades, leaving the echoes. Try it Play a note with a long reverb. Turn Main Wet/Dry right and the note dissolves into the room."}, {"c": "Reverb and mix", "u": "reverb-and-mix.html", "h": "", "t": "#High Pass High Pass removes weight from the bottom. It is gentle at first. Most of its work happens in the upper half of the turn. 12 High Pass at 0 and at 30%.1High Pass 0 2High Pass 30% Use a little to tidy a muddy pad. Do not use it on a bass you want to keep."}, {"c": "Reverb and mix", "u": "reverb-and-mix.html", "h": "", "t": "#Stereo Width Stereo Width goes from mono to the normal stereo picture. It cannot go wider than normal. Use mono for a focused bass. Use full width for a pad. Check it after you add Chorus, Filter Spread and long effects, because they all spread the sound. 12 Stereo Width at 0% (mono) and at 100% (normal).1Stereo Width 0%: mono 2Stereo Width 100%: normal"}, {"c": "Reverb and mix", "u": "reverb-and-mix.html", "h": "", "t": "#Which volume knob does what? INK has several things that sound like volume. They sit at different places. 1234 From the synth to your ears: Synth Lvl, Main Wet/Dry, the engine's Level and Main.1Synth Lvl: the raw synth 2Main Wet/Dry: dry against effects 3Level: the whole engine 4Main Synth Lvl and Main Wet/Dry live in the sound, on the Sound tab. They are saved with it. Level is on the All page. It sets the whole engine, effects included, against the other two engines. Your device's volume sets how loud it is in the room. Tip To balance engines against each other, use Level on the All page. Use Synth Lvl and Main Wet/Dry to shape one sound."}, {"c": "Reverb and mix", "u": "reverb-and-mix.html", "h": "", "t": "#Gain and space Balance the dry sounds first. Then add only as much effect as each one needs. Three long reverbs at once blur into mud. Give the bass less effect. Keep the widest, longest space for the engine that should fill it. If a sound feels thin, check High Pass, Mode (in the filter) and Main Wet/Dry before you raise any level. You can also send engines to separate outputs in a host app (more in chapter 33). Under the hood High Pass is steep. At 30% it cuts below about 85 Hz, at 50% about 600 Hz, and at 100% about 8 kHz. Stereo Width keeps the middle of the sound whole and only changes the sides. The knob runs from mono (0%) to normal (100%). A sound saved with a value above normal still plays at normal."}, {"c": "Reverb and mix", "u": "reverb-and-mix.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSRoom, balance, width Play a chord on a Pad with REVERB Send at zero. This is the dry sound. Raise Send to about a third. Raise Time and listen to the room grow. Add a little Shimmer. Stop when it starts to cover the chord. In MIX, turn Main Wet/Dry left and right. Put it back in the middle. Load a Bass. Pull Stereo Width to mono. The bass gets tight and central. Raise High Pass to 30% and hear the bottom thin out. Then double-tap it."}, {"c": "Matrix first routes", "u": "matrix-first-routes.html", "h": "", "t": "Until now a knob set a sound and that was that. Now you give your fingers, and later the sound itself, a way to push knobs around while you play. In a few minutes your own pressure will open the filter."}, {"c": "Matrix first routes", "u": "matrix-first-routes.html", "h": "", "t": "#What the Matrix is Open the Matrix tab. You see a grid. The Matrix is where every wire lives. 12345 The Matrix. Sources run across the top, controls run down the side.1Dice 2Source letters 3Group title 4Control name and its starting value 5One cell Across the top are seven letters. Each one is a source. Down the left are the controls you can push, grouped under titles such as FILTER and REVERB. Each control is a destination. Where a column meets a row there is a cell. A cell is one wire. The number in it says how hard that source pushes that control. The list is long, so it scrolls. Tap a group title and the section list opens, just like on the Sound tab (chapter 4)."}, {"c": "Matrix first routes", "u": "matrix-first-routes.html", "h": "", "t": "#The seven sources AT A GLANCESources, left to right EnvEnvelope 2. It rises and falls with each note (more in chapter 18). PrsPressure. How hard you press each pad. AMacro A, your first performance knob (more in chapter 20). BMacro B, your second. XLFO X, a wobble that runs by itself (more in chapter 19). YLFO Y, a second wobble. RndA random number, new for each note (more in chapter 18). Careful X and Y are not the left-right and up-down of your finger. They are two wobbles that run without you."}, {"c": "Matrix first routes", "u": "matrix-first-routes.html", "h": "", "t": "#Set a depth Drag a cell up or down. That sets the depth of the wire. Up is more. Go slowly: a quick swipe that starts on a cell scrolls the page instead. 123 The same cell at +50, at 0 and at -50. Minus cells are hatched.1Plus: pushes up 2Zero: no wire 3Minus: pushes down, hatched A plus number pushes the control up. A minus number pushes it down. We call that its polarity. A zero shows as a dot and means no wire. The number is a share of that control's whole range. +50 means \"half the way across\", not \"half as fast\". Double-tap a cell and it clears to zero. That is different from a knob, where a double-tap goes back to its reference value (more in chapter 4). 12 Double-tap a cell and the wire is gone.1Before: +50 2After: 0 A row with at least one wire in it is written in bold. Scroll the list and the bold rows show what the sound is doing."}, {"c": "Matrix first routes", "u": "matrix-first-routes.html", "h": "", "t": "#Your first wire Press harder, get brighter. That is one wire from Prs to Cutoff. 123 Cutoff row, Prs column set to +50. The small text under the name is Cutoff's starting value.1Cutoff row, bold because it has a wire 2Starting value 3Prs cell at +50 The text under a control's name is its starting value, the base value. That is the knob setting from the Sound tab. The wire adds to it. Wires need room. Lower Cutoff on the Sound tab first, then wire Prs to it, then press."}, {"c": "Matrix first routes", "u": "matrix-first-routes.html", "h": "", "t": "#Leave some headroom Here is a surprise. You wire Prs to Cutoff at +100 and hear nothing. Why? Cutoff was already fully open. A plus wire can't open it further. That spare room is headroom. Env and Prs only push up from nothing, so the base value must start low enough to leave room. 1234 Cutoff fully open, wired to Prs at +50: nothing to open. Cutoff at a lower value: room to move.1Starting value: wide open 2Same wire, no effect 3Starting value: lower 4Same wire, brighter Two ways out. Turn the base value down. Or make the wire negative, so the sound gets darker when you press. Sources that swing both ways, such as Macro A, sit at zero in the middle. They use the room on both sides. Tip Wire doing nothing? Check the starting value before you check the wire. A knob at its limit can't move any further."}, {"c": "Matrix first routes", "u": "matrix-first-routes.html", "h": "", "t": "#Voices and effects Some controls answer each note on its own. Press one pad hard and another softly, and each note gets its own brightness. That is true for the tone, the filter and the envelopes. Effects such as Delay and Reverb are shared by every note in the engine. They can't answer each note on its own. They follow the strongest pressure and the loudest envelope among the notes you play. Under the hood A voice is one note. Each voice has its own pressure, envelope and random value. The effect bus has just one of each, so it takes the strongest of the voices."}, {"c": "Matrix first routes", "u": "matrix-first-routes.html", "h": "", "t": "#On the Chords surface On a chord keyboard, two rows change names. Stride becomes Voicing and Degree becomes Spread. They shape how the next chord is built, instead of moving single notes (more in chapter 10). Their knobs on the Sound tab are greyed. 12 On a Chords engine the Matrix rows read Voicing and Spread.1Voicing (was Stride) 2Spread (was Degree)"}, {"c": "Matrix first routes", "u": "matrix-first-routes.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSPressure opens the filter Load a Pad and open the Sound tab. Turn Cutoff down to about the middle. Open the Matrix tab. In the FILTER group find Cutoff. Drag the cell in the Prs column up to about +50. Go back and hold a note. Press softly, then firmly. It opens as you press. Drag the same cell to about -50. Now pressing darkens the sound. Double-tap the cell. The wire clears. QUICK STEPSOne knob, two jobs Turn Cutoff down a little and put a small plus number in the A column of Cutoff. In the REVERB group, put a smaller plus number in the A column of Send. Play a chord and turn Macro A to the right. The sound opens and grows into a bigger space. Turn it to the left of the middle. The sound closes and dries up. The middle of the knob is zero: no push at all. Before you save, check the whole sweep of the knob, not just the middle of it."}, {"c": "Envelope 2 and Random", "u": "envelope-2-and-random.html", "h": "", "t": "A plucked string starts bright and softens. A pad grows. Envelope 2 gives any control that kind of life. You will build a pluck in a few minutes, then make each note a little different."}, {"c": "Envelope 2 and Random", "u": "envelope-2-and-random.html", "h": "", "t": "#A second envelope The main envelope (chapter 12) decides how loud a note is. Envelope 2 has the same stages, but it makes no sound by itself. It only moves other controls. Open the Sound tab and find the ENVELOPE 2 section. 123456 The ENVELOPE 2 section: a drawing and five knobs.1Envelope drawing 2Env Level 3Attack 4Decay 5Sustain 6Release Env Level is how tall the envelope is. At zero it sends nothing. Attack, Decay, Sustain and Release work as they do on the main envelope. Nothing happens yet. Envelope 2 only works once a wire from Env reaches a control. Every note has its own Envelope 2. A chord's notes each rise and fall on their own timeline."}, {"c": "Envelope 2 and Random", "u": "envelope-2-and-random.html", "h": "", "t": "#Make a pluck Brightness that falls after the strike is the classic pluck. 123 A pluck: quick Attack, short Decay, low Sustain.1Quick attack 2Short decay 3Low sustain Set up Envelope 2 first: a quick Attack, a short Decay and a low Sustain. Then turn Cutoff down so the sound is dark when the envelope is at rest. Now open the Matrix tab and put a plus number in the Env column of Cutoff. 12 Env to Cutoff at +60. The row is bold because it has a wire.1Cutoff row 2Env cell at +60 Each note now starts bright and settles toward the dark cutoff you chose. Watch the drawing on the Sound tab while you play. The inked line is your last note. 12 Envelope 2 at the strike, and 300 ms later. The inked line is your note.1At the strike: Envelope 2 is high, so Cutoff is pushed up 2Later: it has settled, so Cutoff is back near its base Tip Want the bite longer? Raise Decay. Want the note to stay bright while you hold it? Raise Sustain."}, {"c": "Envelope 2 and Random", "u": "envelope-2-and-random.html", "h": "", "t": "#Make a pad breathe The same envelope can run slowly. Take a Wavetable pad. Give Envelope 2 a long Attack and a high Sustain. Then put a moderate plus number in the Env column of the first OSC row. On a Wavetable sound that row is called Position. As the chord swells, the tone changes colour with it. The main envelope still decides how fast the loudness arrives. 123 A long Attack on Envelope 2, wired to Position. The colour moves as the chord grows.1Long Attack 2Env cell on the Position row 3The waveform on the card"}, {"c": "Envelope 2 and Random", "u": "envelope-2-and-random.html", "h": "", "t": "#When Env does nothing Three things to check, in this order. The three checks. Env Level. At zero the envelope is flat. Raise it. The depth. Is the number in the Env cell zero? Drag it. The starting point. Is the control already at its limit? A plus wire can't open a Cutoff that is wide open (more in chapter 17)."}, {"c": "Envelope 2 and Random", "u": "envelope-2-and-random.html", "h": "", "t": "#Random Now something that is not a shape at all. Rnd gives each new note its own random number. Wire it to a control and no two notes are quite the same. Try it on the first OSC row (Detune, Width or Position). Put a small plus number in the Rnd column. Play the same note again and again. The tone shifts a little each time. Plus and minus behave differently here: A plus number only ever adds. Each note lands somewhere above the starting value. A minus number spreads both ways. Each note lands above or below the starting value, mostly close to it. 12 Rnd to the first OSC row: plus pushes up only, minus spreads around the starting value.1Plus: notes land above 2Minus: notes land around Tip For a gentle human wobble, use a small minus number on pitch-like or tone rows. For one-sided brightness, use a plus. Under the hood A new random number is drawn each time a note starts. Each voice keeps its own, so a chord's notes differ from each other too. Effects share one number for the whole engine."}, {"c": "Envelope 2 and Random", "u": "envelope-2-and-random.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSA filter pluck Load a bright sound. On the Sound tab open ENVELOPE 2. Set a quick Attack, a short Decay, a low Sustain and make sure Env Level is up. Turn Cutoff down until the sound is dull. Open the Matrix tab. Put about +60 in the Env column of Cutoff. Play short notes. Each one pops open and closes. Raise Decay to stretch it. In the same row, set the Rnd cell to a small minus number. Now each pluck opens a little differently."}, {"c": "LFOs and Freeze", "u": "lfos-and-freeze.html", "h": "", "t": "An envelope moves when you play a note. An LFO moves all the time. In a few minutes you will sweep the filter without touching it, then make each chord land on a different colour."}, {"c": "LFOs and Freeze", "u": "lfos-and-freeze.html", "h": "", "t": "#Four wobbles INK has four. They live on the Sound tab, each in its own section: MOD A, MOD B, LFO X and LFO Y. 1234567 The LFO X section: a Freeze box, a strip that draws the wobble, and five knobs.1Freeze 2The strip 3Depth 4Rate 5Shape 6Warp 7Offset X and Y are two free LFOs. In the Matrix they are the columns X and Y. Mod A and Mod B ride along with Macro A and Macro B. Their wobble is added to the knob (more in chapter 20). In the Matrix they come out in the columns A and B. Careful X and Y are not where your finger is on the screen. They are two wobbles that run by themselves."}, {"c": "LFOs and Freeze", "u": "lfos-and-freeze.html", "h": "", "t": "#Five knobs AT A GLANCEOne LFO, five knobs DepthHow strong the wobble is. At zero there is no wobble. RateHow fast it goes. Slow is a lazy drift. Fast is a shiver. ShapeThe pattern of the wobble. There are thirteen. WarpLeans each cycle toward one part of its shape. OffsetAdds a steady amount to the wobble. Only on X and Y. Two numbers have to let it through. The strip's Depth sets how much wobble exists. The number in the Matrix cell sets how much of it reaches the control. If either is zero, you hear nothing. 12 Wire X to Cutoff at +40 and the strip's wobble moves the filter.1LFO X strip, Depth up 2Matrix cell X to Cutoff, +40"}, {"c": "LFOs and Freeze", "u": "lfos-and-freeze.html", "h": "", "t": "#Thirteen shapes Turn Shape and the strip redraws. These are the shapes, in the order the knob steps through them. All thirteen shapes, each drawn by the real strip. ShapeWhat it doesGood for TriangleRises and falls evenly.Steady sweeps. SineRounded, gentle at the turns.Subtle life. SmthRndRandom levels joined by smooth lines.Organic drift. StepRndRandom levels that jump.Changing colour, or grain position. BiSquareFlips between two opposite levels.Switching back and forth. SquareFlips between zero and a plus level.On and off. CastleSteps through plus, zero and minus.Staircase moves. SawA ramp.Sweeps with a reset. BiTrigsShort blips, alternately plus and minus.Alternating accents. TrigsShort plus blips.Repeating accents. EnvA curved rise and fall.Pulsing movement. GeigerClicks at random moments, each dying away.Crackle and sparkle. PerlinRandom levels joined by very smooth curves.The gentlest drift. Which one is right? Try them. Put one on Cutoff, then change the shape while a chord rings."}, {"c": "LFOs and Freeze", "u": "lfos-and-freeze.html", "h": "", "t": "#Lean the wave with Warp Warp bends each cycle. The wobble spends more time on one side of its shape. 12 A Triangle with Warp in the middle, then pushed far to one side.1Warp in the middle: even 2Warp pushed far: the cycle leans"}, {"c": "LFOs and Freeze", "u": "lfos-and-freeze.html", "h": "", "t": "#Read the drawing The strip is a window on time. The line down the middle is now. 123 Left of the line: what the wobble just did. Right of it: what it is about to do.1The now line 2The past, a second and a half 3The guess for the next second and a half The left side is what the wobble really did. The right side is a guess, made from the knobs as they are now. If something else moves the knobs, the guess is wrong. The left side shows what really happened. Up is plus, down is minus, and the middle is zero. A very fast LFO draws as a band, because it moves faster than the picture can follow. A flat line does not mean the Matrix is broken. Depth may be zero, Freeze may be holding it, Offset may be parked, or the audio may be stopped. 12 A fast LFO draws as a band. A flat line has several causes.1Fast: a band 2Flat"}, {"c": "LFOs and Freeze", "u": "lfos-and-freeze.html", "h": "", "t": "#Rates that follow the beat Some sounds are saved with a synced LFO. Then Rate stops showing Hz and shows a note length instead, such as 1/4 or 2 bars. The wobble follows the engine's tempo. 1 A synced Rate prints a note length.1Rate reads a division There is no switch for this on the Sound tab. It comes with the sound. The Sync knob on the Arp and Seq tabs belongs to the pattern, not to the LFOs (more in chapter 25)."}, {"c": "LFOs and Freeze", "u": "lfos-and-freeze.html", "h": "", "t": "#Freeze A wobble keeps moving while you hold a chord. Sometimes you want the opposite: each chord with its own steady colour. That is Freeze. Tick Freeze above the strip. When you play, the LFO's value is caught at that moment. It stays caught while any note sounds. When all notes have died away, the LFO is free again, ready to catch a new value. 123 Freeze off: the wobble keeps moving through the chord. Freeze on: it holds still, then catches a new value.1Freeze off: moving 2Freeze on: held 3A new value for the next chord Use it with a slow random shape. Route X to Cutoff, tick Freeze, and play chords with gaps. Each one settles into a different colour and stays there. Tip Freeze needs silence to let go. Switch Hold off, and lift your fingers fully before the next chord. Under the hood The held value is Depth and Offset included, and it goes to every destination. The LFO keeps running underneath, so a synced one stays on the beat. A note counts as sounding until its release tail is nearly silent. Delay and reverb tails do not count. With the arp on, its steps count as the notes. With a short arp Gate they leave gaps, and Freeze lets go in them (more in chapter 24 and chapter 25). Strum acts like normal playing."}, {"c": "LFOs and Freeze", "u": "lfos-and-freeze.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSSweep, drift, freeze Load a Pad and turn Cutoff to about the middle. In LFO X set Shape to Sine, raise Depth, and set a slow Rate. In the Matrix put +40 in the X column of Cutoff. Hold a chord. The filter sweeps by itself. Change Shape to SmthRnd. The sweep wanders. Tick Freeze. Switch Hold off. Play three chords with gaps between them. Each has its own steady colour."}, {"c": "Macros and dice", "u": "macros-and-dice.html", "h": "", "t": "You now know every kind of wire. This chapter puts them together: one knob that changes several things at once, a wobble that wakes up when you ask, and a dice roll that hands you a patch to improve."}, {"c": "Macros and dice", "u": "macros-and-dice.html", "h": "", "t": "#One knob, many jobs You met Macro A and Macro B on the Sound tab and on the All page (chapter 4). Each sound decides what they do. The Matrix is where it decides: the A and B columns. Wire one knob to several controls, with different signs, and it becomes a single move: \"tight to spacious\". 123 Macro A wired to three controls: Cutoff up, Reverb Send up, Distort down.1Cutoff: A at +40 2Send (REVERB): A at +30 3Distort: A at -30, hatched Turn Macro A to the right. The sound opens and floats into a bigger room, and the grit fades. Turn it to the left and it does the opposite. The middle of the knob is zero: no push. Tip Give A the main change and B a smaller one, such as space or looseness. Two clear knobs are easier to play than six sweeps hidden behind one. A and B also answer to your controller: Macro A is MIDI CC 1 (the mod wheel on most keyboards) and Macro B is CC 30 (more in chapter 37)."}, {"c": "Macros and dice", "u": "macros-and-dice.html", "h": "", "t": "#Manual only Sometimes A keeps moving after you let go of the knob. Why? Each macro has an LFO riding along with it: MOD A and MOD B on the Sound tab (chapter 19). The wobble adds to the knob. For a knob you play by hand and nothing else, turn that section's Depth down to zero. 12 MOD A with Depth at zero, and at half.1Depth at 0: only your hand moves A 2Depth at 50%: the wobble adds"}, {"c": "Macros and dice", "u": "macros-and-dice.html", "h": "", "t": "#Wobbles that wake up An LFO has controls too. In the Matrix, the groups MOD A, MOD B, LFO X and LFO Y have rows named LFO depth, LFO rate, LFO shape and LFO warp. So a source can steer a wobble. Here is a classic. LFO X is wired to the sound's tone, but its Depth is zero, so nothing moves. Then Macro A is wired to X's LFO depth. Turn A up and the movement wakes up. 12 A to LFO depth (in the LFO X group), and X to Position: a chain.1A cell in the LFO X group, row LFO depth 2X cell in the first OSC row, here Position Now A doesn't move the tone. It decides how much the tone moves on its own. That is more musical than a plain sweep. Tip Add a small wire from A to the Mix row in the CHORUS / PHASER group as well. At the low end the sound is focused. At the high end it is wide and alive."}, {"c": "Macros and dice", "u": "macros-and-dice.html", "h": "", "t": "#Steps and jumps Most rows glide. A few rows step. A source that moves them makes the sound jump. Octave Degree and Stride Scale Sample LFO shape in each LFO group Mode and Division in the ARP group Jumps suit planned changes, like a note that climbs an octave when you turn A. But a wobble wired to a stepped row clicks from state to state. Keep depths small, or do it on purpose. Menus are different. The sound generator, the wavetable bank, and the effect-type menus (Chorus or Phaser, and the delay type) are choices. They are not rows in the Matrix, and no source can move them."}, {"c": "Macros and dice", "u": "macros-and-dice.html", "h": "", "t": "#The dice Top left of the Matrix, in the corner where the rows begin, there is a dice. Tap it. 123 The dice, after three different rolls. The face it lands on sets how many wires it adds.1Face 1: few wires 2Face 3: a fair number 3Face 6: many It tumbles, lands on a face from one to six, and the Matrix fills with a brand-new set of wires. The higher the face, the more wires, not just stronger ones. Middle faces come up most often. Careful A roll replaces every wire in the Matrix. Yours go first, then the new ones come in. The knobs on the Sound tab don't change. One edge swipe undoes the whole roll (chapter 5). 12 The Matrix before a roll (left) and after (right).1Before: a few wires 2After: a new set The dice is tuned to be useful. Its wires are mostly modest. Cutoff gets mostly minus wires, so a wide-open filter doesn't just go silent. It leaves out the stepped rows, Root among them, and also Pitch, Interval, Noise, the delay times and the Chorus and Phaser rows. Those make a random patch sound out of tune or crackly. A roll is a starting point. Play it. Scroll the bold rows. Double-tap the wires you don't like. Then turn the best one into something you steer with A and B."}, {"c": "Macros and dice", "u": "macros-and-dice.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSA macro that wakes up the tone Load a Wavetable sound from the Library (set the Engine filter to Wavetable). Pick one that doesn't move much by itself. In LFO X set Shape to Sine. Turn Depth right down to zero. In the Matrix put a plus number in the X column of the first OSC row, which reads Position. Also in the Matrix, put a plus number in the A column of LFO depth, in the LFO X group. Hold a chord and turn Macro A to the right. The tone starts to move. Turn it back. The tone settles. QUICK STEPSRoll the dice Tap the die in the Matrix and wait for it to land. Play. Scroll through the bold rows to see what it wired. Double-tap the wires you don't like. Don't like any of it? Swipe in from the edge to undo the roll. Found one you love? Save it as new."}, {"c": "Granular basics", "u": "granular-basics.html", "h": "", "t": "The Granular sampler does not play a recording from start to finish. It plays tiny pieces of it, over and over, and the pieces overlap into one smooth sound. You steer where those pieces come from. Hold one note and you can freeze a moment, crawl through it, or run it backwards. Open Engine 1 and load Low cello from the Library (set the Engine filter to Granular to find it and its friends). 123456789 The SAMPLER section of the Sound tab, with Low cello loaded.1Generator drop-down 2Tape or Pitched 3Play style 4Kit button 5Sample 6Scrub 7Grain Sz 8Rate 9Timestretch The SAMPLER section leads the Sound tab. Under it sits SAMPLER JITTER. Every other section works as before, so the filter, the envelopes and the effects all shape the sampler's sound too."}, {"c": "Granular basics", "u": "granular-basics.html", "h": "", "t": "#Slot, slice, grain Three small words keep this chapter tidy. Each one sits inside the one before it. A kit gives the sound eight places for recordings. Each place is a sample slot, or just a slot. Every slot's sample is cut into eight slices. A slice is a place to start, not a separate file. Each slice also has a root note, the pitch it was recorded at. A grain is one tiny playback window. The sampler plays a stream of them. 1234 Slot 6 of the Chamber kit: one sample, eight slice lines, and the eight pads that play them.1Slot tabs 1 to 8 2The slot's sample 3Slice lines 1 to 8 4Slice pads with root notes So: Sample chooses the slot. The slice decides where a note begins. Everything else, Scrub and the grain knobs, works inside the sample. Tip If a knob seems to do nothing, ask which of the three it works on. Mixing them up is the usual reason."}, {"c": "Granular basics", "u": "granular-basics.html", "h": "", "t": "#Choose a slot with Sample Turn Sample to pick slot 1 to 8. The kit view shows what is in each slot (more in chapter 22 and chapter 23). The sampler always plays a slot. It never goes silent because Sample says \"nothing\". Under the hood The Sample knob has no Off step. If a sound's saved Sample value reads Off, INK plays slot 1. If that slot is empty, pick a slot that holds a recording, or another kit."}, {"c": "Granular basics", "u": "granular-basics.html", "h": "", "t": "#Tape or Pitched This drop-down decides what your pads mean. Tape: the pad you touch picks the slice. Think of cutting a tape into eight pieces and playing them with eight fingers. Good for drums, found sounds and loops. Pitched: the note you play picks the slice whose root note is closest, and INK transposes it the rest of the way. Good for instruments and anything you want to play as a melody. New samples you record or bring in start as Pitched. In Tape mode, Pitch (chapter 11) retunes the slices but keeps their speed, so a loop keeps its length. Rate changes both together, like a tape. Root never changes which slice a pad plays. 12 Tape, on the kit Day textures.1Tape 2Slice pads 12 Pitched, on the kit Chamber. The pads show each slice's root note.1Pitched 2Slice pads with root notes Careful In Pitched mode a wrong root note makes every note wrong by the same amount, even if the pad labels look right. Chapter 23 shows how to fix a root."}, {"c": "Granular basics", "u": "granular-basics.html", "h": "", "t": "#Play style Next to it, the second drop-down says what a note does at the end of its region. AT A GLANCEPlay style Play slicePlays the slice once and stops at its end. Loop slicePlays the slice again and again. Play allPlays across the whole sample once. It does not wrap. Loop allPlays the whole sample and wraps round. The envelope still decides how long a note is heard. Loop slice can make a short recording last as long as you hold the pad. Pick a stretch of the recording that holds one steady tone, or the loop will sound like a stutter. Tape or Pitched and the play style belong to the sound. Change them and the sound shows an asterisk. They also appear in the kit view."}, {"c": "Granular basics", "u": "granular-basics.html", "h": "", "t": "#Move through the recording Four knobs decide where the grains read from and how they behave. 123 Scrub at 0 and at 70. The little marks on the card's waveform show where the grains read.1Scrub low 2Scrub high 3Grain marks Scrub moves the starting point. Hold a note and turn it. You hear different moments of the same recording, one after another. A static Scrub near a vowel, a bow stroke or a noisy hit gives very different sounds from one sample. Rate is playback speed and direction. 100 is normal speed. Negative numbers play backwards. It reaches plus and minus 200. Timestretch is how quickly the read point travels along the sample on its own. A low value lingers. Zero stops it dead, so Scrub is the only thing that moves you. Negative values travel backwards. Grain Sz is the size of each grain. Short grains sound buzzy and textured. Long grains keep more of the original waveform, so they sound smoother and more like the recording. An order that works Set Rate first. Then change Timestretch to decide how the sound unfolds. Last, hunt with Scrub for the best place. Under the hood Rate and Timestretch are not perfect pitch and length controls. Both go to plus and minus 200 and they affect each other, so expect to adjust one after you move the other."}, {"c": "Granular basics", "u": "granular-basics.html", "h": "", "t": "#Add a little chaos Under it, SAMPLER JITTER has three knobs: Scrub jit., Grain Sz jit. and Rate jit.. Jitter gives each grain its own random nudge. 123 The three jitter knobs.1Scrub jit. 2Grain Sz jit. 3Rate jit. Scrub jit. spreads where grains read from. Grain Sz jit. varies their length. Rate jit. varies their speed, so the pitch wobbles. Turn them up one at a time first, to learn what each does. For a calm, pitched cloud keep Rate jitter small and Scrub jitter gentle. For a broken, turbulent sound, shorten the grains and push Scrub and size jitter. The filter comes second Find the grain sound you like first. Then use Cutoff to tame a busy top end, ideally with a slow movement on it (more in chapter 13). Don't use a dark cutoff to hide the grains."}, {"c": "Granular basics", "u": "granular-basics.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSFreeze a cello On Engine 1, load Low cello. Hold one note. Turn Timestretch toward zero. The sound stops travelling and hangs. Turn Scrub slowly. The hanging note moves to different places in the recording. Set Grain Sz to a middle value, then raise Scrub jit. a little. Turn Rate below zero and hear it backwards. Want more? Open the Matrix, send Macro A to the Scrub row and Pressure to Cutoff. One held note now becomes a playable texture (more in chapter 17)."}, {"c": "Bring in audio", "u": "bring-in-audio.html", "h": "", "t": "The sampler is only as good as what you feed it. In this chapter you fill a slot with your own sound: a file from your phone, your voice, or INK itself. Each takes under a minute. Standalone app only Import and Record live in the standalone INK app. In an AUv3 host you can play your kits and change how a sound plays them, but you cannot bring in new audio there (more in chapter 33)."}, {"c": "Bring in audio", "u": "bring-in-audio.html", "h": "", "t": "#Open the kit view Load any Granular sound, such as Low cello, and tap Kit in the SAMPLER section. That opens the kit view. 1234567 The kit view. The slot tabs run across the top, and the bar at the bottom brings in audio.1Kit name 2Slot tabs 1 to 8 3Slot field 4Waveform with slice lines 5Slice pads 6Import 7Record Tap a tab, 1 to 8, to choose a slot. That is where a new sound will land. Tapping a tab also plays the sample in it, briefly. A faint tab is an empty slot. The bar at the bottom has two buttons: Import and Record."}, {"c": "Bring in audio", "u": "bring-in-audio.html", "h": "", "t": "#Bring in a file Choose a slot, tap Import and pick Files. Choose one or more audio files and wait while they come in. 123 Import offers Files, and Gallery for the sound of a video.1Import 2Files 3Gallery The file lands in your chosen slot and plays at once. There is no Keep button and no Discard. If you don't like it, tap Undo. Pick several files and the first goes into the chosen slot. The next ones fill the empty slots after it. Any left over go into Yours without taking a slot. The line under the kit's name says what happened, such as \"3 files · 1 only in Yours\". 123 After importing three files. The status line reports it, and Undo waits at the right.1Status line 2Undo 3Slot field INK reads WAV, AIFF, CAF, MP3, M4A, AAC, ALAC and FLAC files, and the sound track of MOV and MP4 videos (Gallery). Tip Undo here puts the slots back as they were. The new samples stay in Yours, ready to use again. It goes away once you choose another slot or leave the kit view. It does not come back after you close the app. Under the hood INK stores samples at 31,250 Hz, the rate the whole app runs at. One sample holds up to 67.1 seconds. A longer file is cut and the slot says \"Cut at 67 s\". A stereo file keeps both channels, so you can pick left, right or both later (more in chapter 23). The sampler plays one mono mix."}, {"c": "Bring in audio", "u": "bring-in-audio.html", "h": "", "t": "#Record a new sample Choose a slot, then tap Record. The record screen opens, titled RECORD SLOT and the slot's number. 1234567 The record screen. Pick a source, watch the level, then Arm or Record.1Cancel 2Sources: Mic, INK, Other apps 3Level and threshold 4State and time left 5Where the take's waveform will appear 6Arm 7Record First choose where the sound comes from. Mic is the microphone, or a USB audio interface if one is plugged in (the button then reads USB). INK records INK itself. Hear it in the next section. Other apps records the sound of another app, after you allow it in the system's sharing sheet. Not every build of INK offers it, so use the sources your screen shows. For a mic or interface, set Gain so the loud moments get close to the right of the meter without hitting the end. Monitor plays the input through INK. Leave it off if your interface already lets you hear yourself, or you will hear everything twice."}, {"c": "Bring in audio", "u": "bring-in-audio.html", "h": "", "t": "#Arm or Record There are two ways to start. Record starts at once. Arm waits. When the sound gets louder than a threshold, recording begins by itself. Drag the little marker along the meter to set it. Tap Arm again to cancel, and you are back at Ready. 123 The threshold marker sits on the level meter. The dB number is its level.1Level meter 2Threshold marker 3Threshold in dB Both keep about 50 milliseconds from before you started, so the first bit of a hit is not clipped. Arm is the easy way to catch a clap or a spoken word without fumbling for the button."}, {"c": "Bring in audio", "u": "bring-in-audio.html", "h": "", "t": "#Slice as you go While recording, tap Slice at the moments you want a slice to start. Each tap marks the place. The state line shows which slice you are up to, such as \"Slice 2/8\". Tap Stop when you are done, or let the eighth tap end the take. 1234 A take in progress. The state line counts the slices you have tapped.1Recording time 2Slice 3/8 3Slice 4Stop Slices you do not tap are spread evenly over the rest of the recording. Tap one slice per syllable, drum hit or note and you get a ready-made kit, one hit per pad. The record screen also shows the time left. If you reach the limit, the take stops and says \"Cut at 67 s\". A take with no sound in it says \"Nothing recorded\" and is not kept. When you stop, the take lands in your slot and in Yours, and plays. Undo is there if you want it back out."}, {"c": "Bring in audio", "u": "bring-in-audio.html", "h": "", "t": "#When something goes wrong Input changed or Input lost: the mic or interface changed or was unplugged mid-take. INK stops there and keeps what it got. Not saved: the recording exists in memory but could not be stored. Tap Try again and wait for the label to clear before you rely on it."}, {"c": "Bring in audio", "u": "bring-in-audio.html", "h": "", "t": "#Resample INK Choose INK as the source and tap Arm or Record. INK returns to the All page, with a card on top that shows the state, the slot, the time left and the level. Now play. Hold a chord, let the delay and reverb ring, add a second engine. Slice and Stop are on the card. Stop, and your performance is a sample. 1234 Recording INK: the card sits over the All page while you play.1Record card 2State and time left 3Slice and Stop 4The All page This captures everything, effects included. A chord with its tail becomes new material the sampler can slow down and scatter. The result is mono, so listen before you rely on a wide stereo effect."}, {"c": "Bring in audio", "u": "bring-in-audio.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSFill a kit Load a Granular sound, tap Kit, and choose tab 2. Tap Import, then Files, and pick a short audio file. Play it. Choose tab 3 and tap Record. Set the source to Mic, tap Arm, then say three short words. Tap Slice on each, then Stop. Choose tab 4. Tap Record, set the source to INK, tap Record, play a chord with some reverb, and tap Stop. Play each slot in turn with Sample on the Sound tab."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "You have a recording in a slot. Now make it behave. You will cut the ends, move the slice lines to the good bits, fix the tuning, and keep everything tidy so nothing goes missing. Open the kit view with Kit on the Sound tab (more in chapter 22). Pick a slot that holds one of your own samples. Tip The factory samples cannot be trimmed or renamed. To try everything here, record or import a sample first."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "#Trim the ends The waveform shows your whole recording. Anything you have cut away is faded out at the edges. Drag the trim handles to choose the stretch to keep. 1234 A sample with its trim handles at both ends. Only what sits between the handles is kept.1Left trim handle 2Cut-away part, outside the handle 3Right trim handle 4Slice lines The handles settle on the nearest zero crossing, so the cut doesn't click. They never leave less than a tiny sliver of sound. Drag both handles back to the very ends and the whole recording returns."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "#Normalise and choose channels Tap the three dots beside the sample's name. The menu has Rename, Normalised and Delete. 1234 The sample's menu. Normalised shows its state at the right: here On, with a change of -0.1 dB.1Three-dots menu 2Rename 3Normalised: On 4Delete Normalised lifts a quiet sample so its loudest moment reaches a fixed level. New samples have it on. The menu reads Off, On, or On with the change in dB, as in the picture. It makes a quiet recording easier to use. It does not clean up a clipped one or remove noise. If the sample is stereo, a drop-down shows L+R, Left or Right. 123 For a stereo sample, choose both channels or just one.1L+R 2Left 3Right L+R mixes both channels together. If the sound turns thin or hollow, one side may be cancelling the other. Try Left or Right instead of turning it up. Samples are shared Trim, Normalised and L+R, Left or Right change the sample itself, in every sound and kit that uses it. Tape or Pitched, the play style, slice lines and roots belong to the sound you are editing. To change a sample without touching the others, duplicate it first (see the sample list below)."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "#Move the slice lines The eight numbered lines on the waveform are where the slices begin. 123 Slice 5 dragged to a new place. A double-tap sends it home.1Slice line 2Dragged line 3Double-tap to reset Drag a line sideways to move it. Lines are allowed to overlap. Double-tap a line and it goes back to where it started. Press the waveform and hold. You hear the slice under your finger, raw and unprocessed, until you let go. Use it to hunt for an attack or a clean loop point."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "#Tune the slice pads The eight pads under the waveform play each slice through your sound, with its envelopes, filter and effects. They show each slice's root note. 12 The slice pads. One root has been dragged up, so it shows bold and underlined.1Slice pads 2Changed root Drag a pad up or down to change its root by whole semitones. Double-tap to put it back. A root you changed is bold and underlined. This matters in Pitched mode (chapter 21). The root tells INK how far to transpose a slice. A root that is too high makes that slice play too high. INK finds the roots of your own samples by listening to them. It does this when the sample first arrives, and again after you trim it, change its channels or move a line. If a slice has no clear pitch, it keeps the note it had. Factory samples keep their prepared roots. Check by ear Detection is a good first guess, not the last word. Drums, chords and noisy starts can fool it. A root one octave off can sound fine alone and then break a melody. Play a scale and fix the odd one out."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "#Choose a sample Tap the slot's field, the one with its name and length. The sample list opens. 123456 The sample list. Yours on top, then the factory samples, grouped by kit.1Yours 2Factory 3Play mark 4Circled slots 5Used in 6Three-dots menu Yours lists everything you recorded or imported, newest first. Factory lists the supplied samples under each kit's name. Tap a row's play mark to hear the sample raw. Tap again to stop. Tap the row itself to put that sample in the slot. Each row says how long the sample is and where it is used. Slots that already hold it are circled. On your own samples, the three dots offer Rename, Duplicate and Delete. Duplicate before you change something shared."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "#Manage kits Tap the kit's name at the top. The Kits list opens. 12345 The Kits sheet. New and Duplicate at the top, your kits first, then the factory ones.1New 2Duplicate 3Your kits 4Factory kits 5Changed mark New makes a kit of eight empty slots. Duplicate copies the current kit. Tap a kit to put your current sound on it. The sound's settings stay, and the new kit gives it different material. Your own kits can be renamed, duplicated or deleted from the three dots. A factory kit can be duplicated, and if you changed it, Reset to factory puts it back. A kit you changed shows \"changed\" beside its name. Tip Name kits after the material and sounds after the result. A kit called Glass recordings could feed Soft glass pad, Broken glass rhythm and Glass shimmer."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "#Empty, Missing, Not saved A slot can say three things, and they are not the same. 123 Three kinds of trouble. Empty has nothing, Missing lost something, Not saved has not been kept yet.1Empty 2Missing 3Not saved Empty: nothing was ever put there. Missing: the slot points to a sample INK cannot find. Choose another from the sample list, bring the sample back with its pack, or choose another kit. Turning up a level will not fix it. Not saved: a take or a file exists only in memory. Tap Try again, and wait until the label clears."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "#Delete safely Deleting a sample or a kit asks first. Read the sheet. 1234 Deleting a kit lists where its sounds move. Its samples stay in Yours.1Its sounds move to 2Its samples 3Delete 4Cancel Deleting a sample lists the kits and sounds that use it. Their slots will then read Missing. Deleting a kit tells you where each of its sounds will go, and its samples stay in Yours. Nothing changes until you confirm. To share a sound together with its recordings, export it from the Library (more in chapter 31)."}, {"c": "Trim, slices and kits", "u": "trim-slices-and-kits.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSTidy a slot Open the kit view on a slot with one of your own samples. Drag the right trim handle in a little. Hear the end disappear. Drag it back. Drag a slice line, then double-tap it to send it home. In Pitched mode, drag one pad up by a semitone. Play the Grid and hear that slice change. Double-tap the pad to put its root back. Tap the sample's three dots and choose Duplicate before you trim it again."}, {"c": "Arp", "u": "arp.html", "h": "", "t": "Hold a few notes and let INK play them one after another, over and over. That is an arp. In a few minutes you will pick the order, change the speed and watch the notes go by."}, {"c": "Arp", "u": "arp.html", "h": "", "t": "#Free, Arp or Strum Open the Arp tab. The first thing you see is a switch with three choices. 12345 The Arp tab: the switch, the trace, and the knobs.1Free / Arp / Strum 2The trace 3Mode 4Division 5Octaves AT A GLANCEThe switch FreeNotes play the moment you touch them. This is normal playing. ArpHeld notes play one after another, again and again. StrumA group of notes plays once, in order, then rings. (More in chapter 26.) Choose Arp, hold three pads and listen. You can also tap the key at the top of the screen and use the same switch there. It is one choice, in two places. 1 The key menu has the same switch.1Free / Arp / Strum You do not need Hold. Keep your fingers down and the arp plays them. With Hold on, the notes stay and your hand is free (more in chapter 7). Tip Switching between Free, Arp and Strum does not mark the sound as changed. It is a way of playing, not a change to the sound."}, {"c": "Arp", "u": "arp.html", "h": "", "t": "#Watch the trace Under the switch sits a strip that draws the notes as they play. It is the trace. 1234 Left of the line: what just played. Right: what comes next.1A note, a dot and its line 2Now 3A guess at the next notes 4Octave marker Each pad column gets a row, with the lowest at the bottom. Each note is a dot, followed by a thin line for as long as it sounds. A hollow dot with +1 beside it is a note the arp moved up an octave. The vertical line in the middle is now. Left of it is the last second and a half. Right of it is a guess at the next second and a half, fading out. Where the arp rolls dice, the guess stops. It cannot know. Try it Choose Arp, hold three pads and watch the dots walk to the line and past it."}, {"c": "Arp", "u": "arp.html", "h": "", "t": "#Mode: the order Mode decides which note comes next. Turn it and the name changes. Hold three notes and try these. 123 Three orders, same three notes: Up, Up/Down and Pedal Up.1Up: low to high, then jump back 2Up/Down: there and back 3Pedal Up: one note keeps returning AT A GLANCEPitch-order modes Up,DownLowest to highest, or the other way. Then it starts again. Up/DownUp, then down, without playing the top or bottom note twice. Up/Down RepeatThe same, but the top and bottom notes sound twice at the turn. Pedal Up,Pedal Down,Pedal Up/DownThe same runs, with one pedal note returning between the others. RndOne random note at a time. It plays every held note once before any repeats. 2xRndTwo random notes at a time. ChordAll the held notes at once, on every step. These modes put the notes in order by pitch. It does not matter which pad you touched first."}, {"c": "Arp", "u": "arp.html", "h": "", "t": "#Eight-step modes The other five modes say 8 Steps in their name: Up 8 Steps, Down 8 Steps, Up/Down 8 Steps, Rnd 8 Steps and 2xRnd 8 Steps. They do not sort by pitch. They walk the grid's eight columns from left to right, one column a step. A column with no held note is an empty step. 123 Eight Steps: a column with no note leaves a gap.1Column with a note 2Empty column: a gap 3Pitch order has no gap So pitch order asks which notes did I hold, low to high? Eight Steps asks which columns, left to right? Use Eight Steps when you want gaps and a fixed layout. Use the others when you want a tidy run."}, {"c": "Arp", "u": "arp.html", "h": "", "t": "#Division: how fast Division sets the space between notes. Turn it and the arp speeds up or slows down. 1234 Division counts in thirty-seconds of a whole note.13/32 24/32 38/32 416/32 The right half of the knob shows values like 4/32. These are synced: they lock to the tempo. Bigger is slower. 4/32 is eighth notes. 8/32 is quarter notes. 16/32 is half notes. 3/32 is a dotted sixteenth, and 6/32 a dotted eighth. The left half of the knob is free speed. The value is a plain number with no \"/32\", and a bigger number is faster. A free arp ignores the tempo and the beat. 12 One knob, two halves: free speeds on the left, synced values on the right.1Free: a plain number 2Synced: N/32 Under the hood A wire in the Matrix can move Division (more in chapter 17), but it stays inside the half its knob is set to. Pressure or an LFO never jumps between free and synced. You will meet Tempo and Sync in chapter 25."}, {"c": "Arp", "u": "arp.html", "h": "", "t": "#Octaves: how far it climbs Octaves goes from 1 to 4. At 1 the arp plays only the notes you hold. Higher, it repeats the run higher up. 12 Octaves 1, then Octaves 3: the same notes, a longer climb.1Octaves 1 2Octaves 3, with +1 and -1 marks At 3 and 4 the run also dips below your notes. The trace marks moved notes with +1 or -1. A long run can get busy fast, especially with a long release or a delay. Keep the notes you hold few while you learn."}, {"c": "Arp", "u": "arp.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS Hold three notes on the grid. Open the Arp tab and choose Arp. Turn Mode to Up, then Up/Down, then Rnd. Watch the trace. Turn Mode to Up 8 Steps. On the Grid, hold one pad in each of columns 1, 2 and 4, counting from the left. Listen for the gap where column 3 would be. Set Division to 4/32, then 8/32. Hear it slow down. Raise Octaves to 3. Choose Free to go back to normal playing."}, {"c": "Arp rhythm and clock", "u": "arp-rhythm-and-clock.html", "h": "", "t": "A plain arp is a machine gun: every step, same weight. This chapter makes it breathe. You add gaps, you add chance, and you decide who keeps the time. You need the arp running. If you have not met it yet, read chapter 24 first."}, {"c": "Arp rhythm and clock", "u": "arp-rhythm-and-clock.html", "h": "", "t": "#Tempo and Sync Tempo and Sync are the first two controls on the Arp tab. They are the same two controls you find on Seq. 123 Tempo and Sync, with Sync on and then off.1Tempo 2Sync on 3Sync off Tempo is the speed of the beat. A synced Division counts from it, so a quarter note is one beat. Sync says who keeps the time. Sync on: the engine follows the shared clock. Its own Tempo is shown, but it does not drive anything. Engine 1 keeps the clock if nothing else does. Sync off: the engine runs on its own clock at its own Tempo. Tempo and Sync belong to the engine's current pattern, not to the sound (more in chapter 27). A pattern can have its own Tempo, and an arp over it follows. How several engines keep one beat is in chapter 29. A free Division ignores all of this. It has no beat to follow."}, {"c": "Arp rhythm and clock", "u": "arp-rhythm-and-clock.html", "h": "", "t": "#Inside a host When INK runs inside another app that supplies a tempo, Tempo shows that tempo with the word host beside it, and the knob stops responding. Change the tempo in the host. 12 Inside a host, Tempo shows the host's number and goes quiet.1Host tempo 2The knob is inactive"}, {"c": "Arp rhythm and clock", "u": "arp-rhythm-and-clock.html", "h": "", "t": "#Add chance: Probability Probability decides how often a step plays. At 100% every step plays. Lower it and more steps stay out. 12 Probability at 100%, then at 60%. Fewer dots on the left, and the notes ring through the gaps. The faint dots on the right are only the guess at what comes next.1100%: every step 260%: some steps miss A step that does not play does not move the arp on. With Gate at 100% the last note simply rings through the gap. With Gate lower, the gap is silent (more on Gate below). Either way the result is looser and a little human."}, {"c": "Arp rhythm and clock", "u": "arp-rhythm-and-clock.html", "h": "", "t": "#Add gaps: Euclid Euclid spreads steps evenly over a short loop, the way many drum rhythms are built. It is a whole number, positive or negative, up to 16 either way. At 0 it is off. Think of it as a loop that is that many steps long. Probability then says how many of the steps in the loop play, spread out as evenly as possible. So Euclid 5 with Probability 50% plays two or three of every five steps, with an even rhythm instead of random misses. 123 Euclid off, Euclid 5 and Euclid -5, all at Probability 50%.1Euclid 0: random misses 2Euclid 5 3Euclid -5 Positive and negative sound different. Positive (such as 5): on a gap, the arp stays where it is. The last note rings on, unless a lower Gate cuts it. Negative (such as -5): the arp keeps walking through its notes, but the gap steps are silent. Those notes are skipped, so the melody lands differently. Careful At Probability 100% every step is a hit, so Euclid seems to do nothing. Lower Probability to hear the gaps. Under the hood Euclid needs a length of 2 or more. At 0 or 1 it is off, and Probability rolls dice step by step instead. Euclid never rolls dice: the same pattern repeats every loop."}, {"c": "Arp rhythm and clock", "u": "arp-rhythm-and-clock.html", "h": "", "t": "#Gate: how long each note lasts Gate is the share of each step that a note sounds. At 100% (all the way up) the note lasts until the next step. Lower it and each note ends sooner and releases. 12 Gate at 100%, then at 50%. The lines end where the notes end.1Gate 100%: the line runs to the next dot 2Gate 50%: the line stops halfway The trace shows it: each note's line ends where the Gate ends it. A gap after every note makes an arp sound crisp. With a long Release you still hear the tail. Gate is the same knob as on Seq, so a change on one is a change on the other. It also affects the pattern's own notes (more in chapter 27). Careful Strum ignores Gate. The arp follows it. If a strum seems stuck, see chapter 26. Tip With Gate under 100%, the silence between notes gives LFO Freeze a place to let go (more in chapter 19)."}, {"c": "Arp rhythm and clock", "u": "arp-rhythm-and-clock.html", "h": "", "t": "#Arp settings when you change sounds INK keeps your arp settings apart from a sound's own. A sound that has an arp built in plays its own Mode, Division, Probability, Euclid and Octaves. A sound without an arp plays yours. This lets you audition sounds over an arp you set up, without rebuilding it each time. If you turn an arp setting on a sound that has no arp, you change your setup. If you turn it on a sound that has an arp, you change that sound, and its name gets an asterisk. Switching Free, Arp or Strum never counts as an edit. 12 Which arp plays: yours, or the sound's own.1Sound without an arp: your setup 2Sound with an arp: its own settings Surprised that the order or spacing changed when you loaded a sound? Check that sound's Mode and Division. The clock did not change."}, {"c": "Arp rhythm and clock", "u": "arp-rhythm-and-clock.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS Hold three notes, choose Arp, and set Division to 4/32. Set Euclid to 5 and Probability to 50%. Listen to the gaps. Turn Euclid to -5. The rhythm changes, and so do the notes. Raise Probability to 100%. The gaps go away. Lower Gate to about half. Each note is shorter, and the trace lines stop short."}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "An arp loops. A strum does not. Touch a chord and the notes enter one by one, then ring on like a chord you played by hand. Do it again and you get another strum. Use a pluck or a keys sound at first. You will hear every attack."}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "#Switch it on Open the Arp tab and choose Strum. The same switch is in the key menu. 1234 The Arp tab with Strum chosen. Octaves is greyed out.1Strum 2Octaves, greyed 3Mode 4Division Now touch three pads at once. Instead of landing together, the notes arrive in a quick run, low to high. Then they ring, each with its own envelope, until you let go. 123 The trace in Strum: each note starts with a dot, then its line keeps going.1The first note: right away 2Later notes enter in order 3The lines stack and ring on Try it Pick a Pluck. Choose Strum. Touch three pads together, lift, and do it again."}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "#What counts as a strum Notes you touch within about 40 thousandths of a second of each other form one strum group. Press a chord and your fingers never land at exactly the same moment, so this catches them. The first note sounds at once. INK never waits to see what else you are going to play. The rest follow. A single note on its own is not a strum. It plays as it would on Free. A late single note can still join. If a strum is still running, a note you touch next joins the queue and gets its turn. Once the strum has finished, a new single note plays normally."}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "#Order and speed Mode sets the order, as it does for the arp. Strum only offers the orders that play each note once: Up and Down Rnd and 2xRnd Up 8 Steps, Down 8 Steps, Rnd 8 Steps and 2xRnd 8 Steps As you turn Mode, it skips the others. Up/Down, Up/Down Repeat, the Pedal modes, Chord and Up/Down 8 Steps would play a note twice, or all at once, and neither is a strum. If a Matrix route sends Mode to one of those, the strum plays as Up. Division sets the gap between notes, as it did for the arp (see chapter 24). 12 A fast free Division strums like a guitar. 4/32 spaces the notes like a rhythm.1Free Division: a fast strum 24/32: separate entrances A fast free speed gives a guitar-like strum, with only a few thousandths of a second between notes. A synced value such as 4/32 spaces the notes clearly: one note every eighth note. A synced strum counts from the moment you strike. It does not wait for the next beat or bar. To land exactly on the beat, time your touch to it, or let a pattern play the chord (more in chapter 27). Octaves is greyed. Each note has one voice and rings once, so it cannot also be sounding in another octave."}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "#Gaps in a strum Euclid adds gaps between the notes. Notes already ringing carry on through those gaps. With Euclid at 2 or more, Probability sets the rhythm's density. Turn it down for more gaps. It never rolls dice: the same settings give the same rhythm. Rnd and 2xRnd can still choose a random note order. Division sets the length of one step. Euclid sets how many steps the rhythm repeats over. Euclid 3 repeats after three steps; Euclid 7 repeats after seven. It does not set the number of notes. 123 Euclid 4 at 50%: a note every second step.1Strike 2Gap 3Strike"}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "#Read the rhythm ● starts the next note. · waits one step. Read left to right, then repeat until every note has started. Only the rhythm repeats; the notes each start once. ProbabilityEuclid 3Euclid 4Euclid 5Euclid 7 25%● · ·● · · ·● · · · ●● · · · ● · · 50%● · ●● · ● ·● · ● · ●● · ● · ● · ● 75%● · ●● · ● ●● · ● ● ●● · ● ● ● · ● 100%● ● ●● ● ● ●● ● ● ● ●● ● ● ● ● ● ● Short loops have only a few possible rhythms. Euclid 3 gives the same pattern at 50% and 75%. The percentage is a density setting, not an exact hit count in every loop. At 100%, every step starts the next note. At 0%, the first note still starts immediately, but the remaining notes keep waiting. With Euclid at 0 or 1, Probability does nothing in Strum: every step starts the next note. Try it Choose Up, set Probability to 50%, and use a slow Division. Strum with Euclid at 4, then 5. Four stays evenly spaced. Five puts two strikes together where the rhythm wraps."}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "#Strum again A new strum starts a new run from the beginning of its order. 123 A new strum cuts the notes of the old one that were still waiting.1Old strum, still waiting 2New strum starts 3Notes already ringing carry on Notes of the old strum that had not yet had their turn are dropped. Notes already ringing carry on, and the new strum stacks on top of them. A note you lift before its turn is skipped. A quick tap plays only the notes that had their turn. This is why a strum feels like playing, not like a recording."}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "#Strum on the Chords surface On Chords, every new chord is a fresh strum. The old chord lets go into its release, like a guitarist lifting a hand, and the new chord is struck. 12 Chords with Strum on: a tile struck, and the notes entering on the chord piano.1The tile struck 2The chord piano, with notes lighting in order With Hold on, the new chord is struck and then held. If an engine follows another one, it can re-strum when the leader strikes. A follower with just one note moves along with the leader and is not struck again (more in chapter 30)."}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "#Gate and strums The arp's Gate does nothing to a strum. Each note rings as you play it. A pattern is different. When a recorded step feeds a strum, the step's own Gate still applies. A step that ends too soon loses the notes that have not had their turn. 123 A pattern step that is too short: the late notes never sound.1Notes that sounded 2Gate ends the step 3Notes that never got their turn Tip If only the first strings of a strummed pattern step sound, lengthen the step or raise its Gate. Or speed up Division so the strum finishes sooner. Under the hood A sound saved with Strum counts as an arp sound, so it keeps its Strum when you play it."}, {"c": "Strum", "u": "strum.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS Load a Pluck and open the Arp tab. Choose Strum and set Mode to Up. Set Division to a fast free speed. Touch three pads together. Hear the guitar. Set Division to 4/32. Touch the pads again. The notes now arrive one by one. Set Euclid to 4 and Probability to 50%. Strum again and hear the longer gaps. Set the engine to Chords in Settings (chapter 9), then play a chord and then another."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "A pattern is a loop of notes that plays itself. You can place the notes one by one, or just play and let INK write it down. Both end up on the same page: Seq. Every engine has its own patterns, so pick the engine first. Then open its Seq tab."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Read the Seq tab 1234567 The Seq tab. Eight lanes of notes, a strip of four pages above them, controls underneath.1Transport row 2Page strip 3Ruler 4Lanes 5Note bar 6Edit bar 7Knobs A pattern is up to 64 steps, in four pages of sixteen. The eight lanes are the Grid's eight columns, with column 8 on top. A note in a lane is a note you would play in that column. The row at the top is the transport. AT A GLANCEThe transport row Play / StopStarts and stops this engine's pattern. Stop waits for the end of the current step. RecordStarts recording. What it does depends on whether the pattern is playing (more below). Previous step / Next stepMove one step back or forward: the cursor while you record, the selected step otherwise. UndoTakes back your last pattern edit (more in chapter 28). PatternsShows the pattern's number, such as A1, and its name, with \"edited\" when it has unsaved changes. Tap it to open the pattern list. Inside a host, Play is replaced by a label that says \"DAW plays\" or \"DAW stopped\" (more in chapter 29)."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Read a note bar Each note is a bar in its lane. Its left end is where it starts. Its length is how long it lasts. 1234 One note bar. Hatching shows how hard the note was pressed, slice by slice. The slanted stroke is a slide.1Start 2Length 3Pressure hatching 4Slide link The hatching inside the bar follows your pressure. Where you pressed hard, it is dense. A note you entered by step has even hatching, because every step note has the same strength. A slanted stroke from one note to the next is a slide: the pitch glides over instead of starting fresh. You will make one in chapter 28."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Pages and loop The page strip shows the whole pattern at a glance. Tap a box to look at that page. 1234 The strip shows the four pages. Steps outside the loop are hatched. The playing step moves along.1Page shown 2Loop edge 3Hatched: outside the loop 4Playing step Not every step plays. The loop is the stretch that repeats. A new pattern loops eight steps. The bar under the lanes tells you the loop when nothing is selected, as \"Loop 1-8\". While the pattern plays, the view follows the playing page. Tap another page to stay there."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Start an empty pattern Tap Patterns. The pattern list opens. Choose Init. You get an empty pattern in the first free slot and go back to Seq. 123 The pattern list. Init sits next to the bank letters.1Banks A-D 2Init 3Done Tap Done if you only wanted to look. Saving, banks and the rest of this sheet are in chapter 28."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Put notes in by hand QUICK STEPSEnter notes step by step Tap an empty spot in a lane, or a number on the ruler. That step is selected. Play a pad, a key or a chord. A note appears at that step. Keep pressing and the note grows one step at a time, at the pattern's tempo. Lift your finger. The next step is selected, ready for the next note. Repeat. Play several pads together to enter a chord. Select a step and tap Loop end when your phrase is done. 1234 A selected step. The edit bar says Step 3, and the Grid marks the notes that start there.1Selected step 2Edit bar 3Marked pads 4Done When a step is selected, the Grid edits it instead of just playing. A pad in an empty column adds a note. Another pad on a note's first step changes its pitch. The pad it already has removes it. If an earlier note is still sounding at your step, a new note cuts it there. You still hear every pad you press, but nothing latches, even with Hold on. In portrait, a Done button over the Grid ends the edit. With nothing selected, the Grid plays normally again."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Record step by step Stop the pattern, then tap Record. The pattern is not playing, so INK waits for you. The bar under the lanes says \"Step\" and a number, and shows Rest and Stop rec.. 12345 Step recording. A dashed cursor marks the step. Rest and Stop rec. are in the bar.1Cursor 2Step number 3Rest 4Stop rec. 5Previous step / Next step Play a note or chord. Hold it to make it longer. When you let go, the cursor moves past it. Rest leaves a step empty and moves on. Previous step and Next step move the cursor by hand. Stop rec. ends it. Step notes all have the same strength. It also works from any tab, so you can leave Seq and keep entering."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Record live Tap Play to start the pattern, then tap Record. Now play. INK writes down your notes and your expression as the loop goes round. A dot blinks beside the Seq tab's name for as long as you record, wherever you look. Tap Record again to stop. Your take becomes the pattern. It shows \"edited\" until you save it (more in chapter 28). Tip Record something simple first. Add the pressure and slides once the loop and Division feel right. On a sound with a long Release, the notes ring longer than they are written, so check the bars."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Tighten it with Quantize Live takes are as loose as your hands. Quantize fixes that. It is the last knob in the Seq tab's row. At 0% your take stays as played. At 100% every note lands on a step. In between, you tighten the timing and keep some feel. 12 The same take at 0% and at 100%. The note bars line up with the steps.1Quantize 0% 2Quantize 100% It moves only the notes the take added, and it works on your last take until you edit it. Once you change a note by hand, Quantize leaves the take alone. Under the hood Quantize is a setting of the app, not of a sound or a pattern. It is saved with your session, and it is never an undo step."}, {"c": "Record patterns", "u": "record-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS Open Seq on Engine 1. Tap Patterns, then Init. Tap the ruler at step 1. Play a pad, lift, play another pad, lift, and again. That is three notes. Step 4 is already selected. Tap Loop end. The loop is now four steps. Tap Play. Your three notes loop. Tap Record and play a fourth note live. Tap Record to stop. Turn Quantize up and watch the note bars snap to the steps."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "A recorded pattern is never final. Tap a note, and you can nudge it, stretch it, glide it into the next one or throw it away. Then you save it, and it stays. Open Seq on an engine with a pattern in it. If you have none, chapter 27 makes one in two minutes."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Select a note Tap a note bar. Its pad is marked on the Grid, and the edit bar shows its pitch and length. 123456 A selected note. The edit bar shows its pitch, its length and its buttons.1Selected note 2Pitch 3Length 4Shorter / Longer 5Slide 6Delete AT A GLANCEThe edit bar for a note Shorter / LongerDrawn as − and +. They change the length. Below one step they halve or double it. From one step up they take away or add a whole step. SlideGlides the pitch into the next note instead of starting that note fresh. DeleteRemoves the note."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Move it, stretch it Drag a note sideways to move it. It jumps by whole steps. If it would land on another note in its lane, the move is refused. With a note selected, drag its right end to change its length. Whole steps are the usual size, and a shorter note can go down to an eighth of a step. 1 Before and after dragging the end of a selected note. Shorter and Longer do the same in smaller bites.1Drag the end Careful A long note cuts or replaces the notes it covers in the same lane. After you stretch a phrase, look at the next note. If it vanished, tap Undo. On a touchscreen there is a second way. Hold a note with one finger and tap a step with another. The note now ends there. You can change page with your free hand first."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Slide into the next note Select a note and tap Slide. A slanted stroke now joins it to the following note. The pitch glides over instead of starting again. 12 Slide off, then Slide on. The slanted stroke joins the two notes.1Normal boundary 2Slide link Slide only works when a note follows in the same lane. How long the glide takes depends on the sound's Glide."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Delete and clear Delete removes the selected note. Select an empty step instead and the edit bar offers Clear, which empties that whole step. There is a third Clear in the pattern list. It empties the entire pattern. Read the edit bar before you tap any of them."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Loop, offset and direction Select a step and tap Loop start or Loop end in the edit bar. The loop can be short or span several pages. A short loop is handy for working on one bar. Two knobs on the Seq tab change how the loop is read. 123 Step offset 0, then 2. The same loop, entered at another step.1Loop 2Step offset knob 3Playing step starts here Step offset turns the loop like a wheel. The notes stay put, but the pattern begins from another step. Direction chooses the path: AT A GLANCEDirection ForwardPlays from the first step to the last, then starts again. ReverseThe same, backwards. PingpongForward, then back. The last step plays once at each end. PingPong RepLike Pingpong, but each end step plays twice before turning. RandomVisits every step of the loop once in shuffled order, then shuffles again. PauseStops the progress. The notes are still there. 12 Pingpong and PingPong Rep over four steps. The arrows show the path.1Pingpong: 1-2-3-4-3-2-1 2PingPong Rep: 1-2-3-4-4-3-2-1-1 Division, Gate, Probability and Euclid sit in the same row. Division sets how long a step is. Gate shortens how much of each step sounds, without touching the saved notes. Probability and Euclid work as on the arp (chapter 25). If a note seems missing, set them back to full first."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Undo, just for patterns The Undo button on Seq steps back through your pattern edits and recorded takes. It is separate from the screen-edge swipe (chapter 5). Use it right after a stretch covers a note, or a take you did not like."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Keep a pattern An edited pattern shows \"edited\" on the Patterns button. Open the list and look at the bottom. 123456 An edited pattern. Save, Save as new and Discard appear at the bottom.1Banks A-D 2Edited pattern 3Clear 4Discard 5Save as new 6Save Save keeps your changes in this pattern's slot. Save as new puts a copy in a free slot and leaves the original as it was. Discard throws your changes away and goes back to the saved version. If you pick another pattern while this one is edited, INK asks first. The question is \"Pattern A1 has changes\", and it offers the same three buttons, plus Cancel. A pattern keeps its own timing: Division, Tempo, Direction, Probability, Euclid, Step offset, Gate, the loop and Sync. Changing the sound never changes how a saved pattern plays. Careful Two engines can use the same saved slot. Then saving a change changes both. Use Save as new before you make a variation."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Banks and factory patterns Patterns live in four banks, A to D, with 24 patterns each. In the Factory project, bank A holds the 24 factory patterns and B, C and D are yours. Your own projects start with four banks of their own (more in chapter 32). You can change a factory pattern. If you do, Restore factory brings back the original. A factory pattern was written for a sound. When you pick one while another sound plays, INK asks whether to load that sound, replacing the current one. Choose Load sound to hear it as written. Choose Keep current sound to try the pattern on your own. Cancel changes nothing. When you save a pattern, it also remembers the saved sound playing on its engine. That is the sound INK offers to load later. 123 The question for a factory pattern: load its sound, or keep yours.1Load sound 2Keep current sound 3Cancel"}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Bring patterns from another project In the pattern list, tap Import next to Done. It takes two steps. 1234 Import patterns, here with no other project to bring from yet. Once there is one, pick it under FROM and its tiles fill in.1FROM: source project 2Banks 3Pattern tiles 4Next First choose the project under FROM, then the bank, then tap the patterns you want. Tap Next. Now choose a bank in this project, and tap the slot where the first pattern should land. Tap Import. If a slot is taken, INK asks whether to Replace it. Only saved patterns can be brought over. An unsaved edit stays with its own project, so save it there first. The factory bank A cannot be a destination."}, {"c": "Edit patterns", "u": "edit-patterns.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS On a pattern with at least two notes in one lane, tap the first note. Drag its right end across the next note. See the next note get cut. Tap Undo. It comes back. Tap Slide on the first note. The stroke appears. Turn Direction to Pingpong and press Play. Tap Patterns, then Save as new. You have a copy."}, {"c": "Keep engines in time", "u": "keep-parts-in-time.html", "h": "", "t": "Three engines can each play their own pattern. Sync decides whether they play together or drift apart. Both are useful."}, {"c": "Keep engines in time", "u": "keep-parts-in-time.html", "h": "", "t": "#One clock for all Each engine has its own Sync switch, next to Tempo on the Arp and Seq tabs. With Sync on, the engine follows the shared clock. That clock is the master clock. Press Play on a synced engine while nothing else plays. It starts at once, on beat 1."}, {"c": "Keep engines in time", "u": "keep-parts-in-time.html", "h": "", "t": "#Bring in another engine on the next bar Press Play on another synced engine while the first is playing. It does not jump in off the beat. It arms. 123 Engine 2 is armed: a dashed ring on its tab. Engine 1 is playing: a dot.1Playing: dot 2Armed: dashed ring 3Not playing At the first beat of the next bar, it starts. Changed your mind? Press Play again before the bar arrives. The ring disappears and nothing starts. The tab dot also tells you who is playing, even from another page. It changes size on every step."}, {"c": "Keep engines in time", "u": "keep-parts-in-time.html", "h": "", "t": "#In a host or with an outside clock In a host, the host's tempo becomes the clock. Seq shows a label where Play was. It says \"DAW plays\" or \"DAW stopped\". Start and stop from the host (more in chapter 33). 1 Inside a host, the Play button gives way to a label. Here the host is stopped, so it reads DAW stopped. While the host runs it reads DAW plays.1DAW stopped The same goes for a MIDI clock arriving from outside. The first start waits for the clock's next pulse, and a later start waits for the next bar. If an engine stays armed for ever, check that the other device is really sending Start and Clock, not only a tempo."}, {"c": "Keep engines in time", "u": "keep-parts-in-time.html", "h": "", "t": "#Run one engine free Turn Sync off for an engine, and it plays on its own Tempo. This is how you get a layer that drifts against the others. A tempo change elsewhere then no longer moves it. 123 Sync on, then off. With Sync off the engine has its own Tempo.1Sync on 2Sync off 3Tempo"}, {"c": "Keep engines in time", "u": "keep-parts-in-time.html", "h": "", "t": "#Different lengths, new rhythms Even with Sync on, each engine has its own loop. A loop of eight steps and one of seven share the beat, but they do not line up every bar. The seven-step loop slips one step each time round. 123 Eight steps against seven. They line up again only after 56 steps.1Loop of 8 2Loop of 7 3Slipping apart That slow shift is a polyrhythm. You made it just by setting the loops. Tip Build with the same Division first, then vary loop lengths. Change one thing at a time, so you can hear what each does."}, {"c": "Keep engines in time", "u": "keep-parts-in-time.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS Give Engine 1 and Engine 2 each a pattern, with the same Division. Set Engine 1's loop to 8 steps and Engine 2's to 7. Press Play on Engine 1. Press Play on Engine 2. Watch its tab ring, then start on the bar. Listen to the two loops slide against each other. Turn Engine 2's Sync off. Change its Tempo and hear it drift free."}, {"c": "Key lock and Follow", "u": "key-lock-and-follow.html", "h": "", "t": "Three engines in one key sound tidy. Three engines that move together when you change chords sound like a band. This chapter gives you both, and the way to split them when you want to. You need a key (more in chapter 6) and, for Follow, the Chords surface (chapter 9)."}, {"c": "Key lock and Follow", "u": "key-lock-and-follow.html", "h": "", "t": "#Keys travel together Tap the key at the top left to open the key menu. By default the three keys are linked. Change Root or Scale on any engine and the other two change with it. That is handy: you pick a key once. Sometimes you want one engine to stay put. That is what the padlock is for. 12345 The key menu, here for Engine 2. The padlock sits in the top right corner. Follow is the bottom row.1Root 2Scale 3Lock 4Free / Arp / Strum 5Follow"}, {"c": "Key lock and Follow", "u": "key-lock-and-follow.html", "h": "", "t": "#Lock an engine's key Open the key menu and tap the padlock. It closes. This is Key Lock. Now that engine keeps its key. A closed padlock also appears in front of its key name on the page and the All page. Locking changes no key. It only changes what happens next. 123 Engine 2 is locked. Its key shows a closed padlock. Engines 1 and 3 are not.1Engine 1: unlocked 2Engine 2: locked 3Engine 3: unlocked The rules are short: No locks: a change on any engine changes all three. You change a locked engine: every unlocked engine takes the new key. You change an unlocked engine while another is locked: that engine changes alone. The locked one stays. A locked engine ignores the keys that its patterns carry. Patterns can bring a key along when you choose them. If the engine is locked, it keeps its own. So a locked engine can lead the others but nobody can push it around. To check what you have, look at the key shown on each engine. Tip Want bass in A minor and a pad in C major? Set each, then lock both. Choose patterns freely. The keys stay where you put them. Under the hood Each engine's Root, Scale and Lock belong to the project. They are not part of a sound, so browsing sounds never moves your key."}, {"c": "Key lock and Follow", "u": "key-lock-and-follow.html", "h": "", "t": "#Make an engine follow your chords Follow lets one engine play the chords while the others move with them. The engine that plays the chords is the leader. An engine that moves with it is a follower. QUICK STEPSSet up a follower Put Engine 1 on the Chords surface. It is the leader. On Engine 2, hold some notes with Hold, or start a pattern. It is the follower. Open Engine 2's key menu. In the Follow row, tap 1. Play chords on Engine 1. Engine 2 moves with them. The row offers Off and the numbers of the other engines. You never see your own number. An engine on Chords has no Follow row, because it leads. 123 Engine 2's key menu with Follow set to 1.1Follow row 2Off 31 chosen"}, {"c": "Key lock and Follow", "u": "key-lock-and-follow.html", "h": "", "t": "#What the follower plays The follower keeps its shape. Every note moves by the same number of scale steps as the chord did. It does not turn into the chord's notes. Say Engine 2 holds C, F and G in C major. Engine 1 plays I, vi, IV, V. 12345 Held C, F, G following I, vi, IV and V in C major. The shape stays the same; the notes move.1Chord on Engine 1 2C F G 3A D E 4F B C 5G C D Held C, E and G does the same: C E G, then A C E, F A C and G B D. Whatever you hold, it moves with the chord. The move picks the nearer direction, so a bass line does not leap an octave for no reason. It is immediate, and the follower sound's Glide sets how it travels. An arp keeps running. It does not restart when the chord changes. A pattern follows too, while the saved pattern stays exactly as it was. Only the chord's column counts. A different row, Spice or voicing on the same column moves nothing. When the leader lifts its finger, the follower stays where the last chord put it. Off, or moving the leader away from Chords, puts every note back where you played it. 123 Left: the chord piano on Engine 1. Right: the Grid of Engine 2, with its held notes moved to match.1Chord sounding on Engine 1 2Held notes before 3Held notes after"}, {"c": "Key lock and Follow", "u": "key-lock-and-follow.html", "h": "", "t": "#Good to know The follower uses its own scale. If it is locked in a different key, it still moves by the same number of steps in that scale. Follow moves what you play on INK's own surfaces, plus held notes and patterns. Notes from an outside MIDI keyboard are not moved. Careful Follow is a setting you can forget. If a bass seems to be playing the wrong notes, open its key menu and look at the Follow row."}, {"c": "Key lock and Follow", "u": "key-lock-and-follow.html", "h": "", "t": "#Three layers, one progression Put Engine 1 on Chords. Give Engine 2 one held bass root and set Follow to 1. Give Engine 3 an arpeggiated upper line and set Follow to 1 too. Now one chord progression moves all three layers. Each keeps its own rhythm and its own tone. 123 The All page: Engine 1 on Chords leads, Engines 2 and 3 follow.1Engine 1: Chords (leader) 2Engine 2: bass, Follow 1 3Engine 3: arp, Follow 1"}, {"c": "Key lock and Follow", "u": "key-lock-and-follow.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPS Set Engine 1's keyboard to Chords in Settings. On Engine 2, hold three notes with Hold. Open its key menu and set Follow to 1. Play chords on Engine 1. Hear Engine 2 move. On Engine 3, hold one low note, set Arp and Follow to 1. Open Engine 3's key menu and tap the padlock. Change Engine 1's Root. Engine 3's key stays. Set Follow to Off on Engine 2. Its notes return."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "You made a sound you like. This chapter keeps it, tidies it, and lets you hand it to someone else. Nothing here changes how a sound plays. It only decides what stays."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#The asterisk Change anything on a sound and an asterisk (*) appears after its name. It means: this is no longer exactly the saved sound. Nothing is lost yet. The change is only in the engine. Tap Save, the button beside the sound name, to open the save choices. 12345 An edited sound and its save choices.1Asterisk after the name 2Save 3Save as new... 4Restore factory version 5Cancel AT A GLANCEThe save choices SaveReplaces the saved sound with what you hear now. Save as new...Keeps the original and makes a second sound. It asks for a name. Restore factory versionOnly for a factory sound you saved over. Brings back the one INK shipped. Name and category...Only for sounds of your own. Change the name or the kind. CancelClose the choices and change nothing. On a factory sound that you have not saved over yet, Save reads Save over the factory sound. It is the same button with a plainer warning. Careful Save replaces the sound you started from. Use Save as new... when you want to keep both."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#Name it and give it a kind Save as new... opens a small card. Type a name, then pick one of the six kinds: Pad, Lead, Bass, Pluck, Keys or FX. The kind decides where the sound sits under Type in the Library. 123 Name the sound, then pick its kind.1Name 2Six kinds 3Save Init is the empty sound at the top of the Library, under Start from scratch. It can't be saved over. You can only Save as new..., and you must pick a kind."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#Leaving a changed sound Load another sound while the first still has its asterisk, and INK stops to ask. The card names the sound and says it has changes. Save keeps your changes, then loads the new sound. Save as new... keeps both. Discard changes throws your changes away and loads the new sound. Cancel stays where you are. Discard changes only shows up here, when you are about to leave. That is the one moment your edits would be lost. Tip Found something good? Save it before you wander off. Undo forgets when you close the app. A saved sound is still there tomorrow."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#What a sound does not hold A sound is one engine's tone, movement and effects. It does not keep the key (Root and Scale), and it does not keep the notes of a pattern. Those belong to the engine's setup, which a project keeps (chapter 32)."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#Find your own sounds Open the Library by tapping the sound name. Beside the star and Type you met in chapter 1 sit two more drop-downs. Each one picks one value, and the filters combine. 1234567 The Library's filters, and the status line under them.1Favourites star 2Type 3Engine (set to Granular) 4From 5Count and Clear 6Kit, only for Granular 7Sort From says where a sound came from. Choices: Everywhere, Factory, Mine, then one entry for each pack you have brought in. A factory sound you saved over counts as Mine. Engine picks the kind of sound generator, never Engine 1, 2 or 3. When Engine is Granular, a Kit link appears in the status line. It shows only the sounds that play one kit's recordings. Sort sits in the status line and reads \"Sort: Type\". Tap it to choose. Type groups by kind, Name runs A to Z, Newest groups by when the sound arrived: Today, Yesterday, This week, This month, Earlier. Clear switches every filter off. Tap it if the list looks empty."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#Piano Room Piano Room is a pack of seven keys sounds that arrives by itself. Open From and choose Piano Room to see them. They install once. Delete one and it stays deleted."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#Delete and bring back Tap Select at the bottom of the Library. Tick sounds, then use the bar: Star, Export or Delete. INK asks \"Delete 3 sounds?\" first. Answer with Delete or Cancel. Delete a factory sound and it is only hidden. Delete a sound of your own and it is gone. To bring back factory sounds, open From and choose Deleted. This entry appears only while something is hidden. Tick the sounds and tap Restore. 123 From, set to Deleted. Tick sounds and tap Restore.1From: Deleted 2Ticked sounds 3Restore"}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#Send sounds to someone 123 Select mode with three sounds ticked. The bar shows Star and Delete here. On your phone Export sits between them.1Ticks 2Star and Delete (Export sits between them on the phone) 3Select all link Tap Select, tick the sounds you want, then tap Export. A card opens. AT A GLANCEThe Export card PackThe name of the file. It starts with the name of the folder the sounds came from, or \"INK sounds\". ByYour name. It travels with the sounds, so whoever opens them sees who made them. INK remembers it for next time. File, Sounds, Kits, Recordings, Size, Left outLines under the fields that list what the file holds and anything that could not go in. ExportOpens the system share sheet. Save the file to Files, or send it. CancelCloses the card and sends nothing. A pack is the file you send. It ends in .inksounds. If a sound uses a kit, a tick box offers to add the other sounds on that kit too. Tick it when the whole family should travel together."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#Bring sounds in Tap Import at the bottom of the Library and pick a file. You can also open an .inksounds file from another app and choose INK. When it finishes, a card tells you what happened. AT A GLANCEThe import result card CAME INThe new sounds, kits and recordings. CREDITSShows up when the file has more than one author. ALREADY HEREWhat INK did not store again. Import the same pack twice and nothing new arrives. LEFT OUTIn one plain line each, what could not come over. ShowJumps to the sounds that just arrived. UndoTakes the whole import back. DoneCloses the card."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#Import from a Plinky INK is a descendant of the Plinky, and it reads Plinky files. Import accepts a folder, a ZIP, or the loose .uf2 files. A full set holds PRESETS.UF2, SAMPLE0.UF2 to SAMPLE7.UF2 and a wavetable file. Hand over everything together, so converted sounds can find their samples. 123 What a Plinky folder turns into.1Presets become sounds 2Samples become recordings and a kit 3A custom wavetable becomes a user wavetable INK turns each preset into a sound and keeps its arp. The eight samples become recordings in one kit. A custom wavetable becomes a user wavetable. Patterns, sequencer settings and the latch do not come over, and presets left at their starting values are skipped. INK has its own waves, filter and effects, so treat the result as a playable conversion. Listen, then adjust. Under the hood INK reads the files by what is inside them, never by their names. The folder or ZIP name becomes the pack name in From. Sample slots keep their length, slices and root notes from PRESETS.UF2."}, {"c": "Save and share sounds", "u": "save-and-share-sounds.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSSave, discard, share Load a Pad. Turn Cutoff down. An asterisk appears. Tap Save, choose Save as new..., name it and pick Pad. Turn Cutoff down again, then tap the sound name and pick another sound. Choose Discard changes. Open the Library, tap Select, tick your new sound and tap Export. Save the file, then tap Import and pick it again. The card says \"Nothing new\" and lists your sound under ALREADY HERE."}, {"c": "Projects", "u": "projects.html", "h": "", "t": "A sound is one engine's tone. A project is everything: all three engines, how they sit together, and the patterns they play. Save a project at the end of a session and tomorrow you press play where you stopped."}, {"c": "Projects", "u": "projects.html", "h": "", "t": "#Open the Projects list Look at the right end of the tab row. A folder icon and a name sit after All. That is the project you are in. Tap it. 12 The folder and the project's name, at the end of the tab row.1Folder and name 2Asterisk: changed since the last save The Projects list looks like the Library. Each row shows the project's name, the day you saved it and a star. A project that came from a file also shows \"by\" and the author's name. The open project is inked in. 12345 The Projects list on a fresh install. Only Factory is there, and it is the open project, so it is inked in.1Star filter 2New, Save, Save as new... 3The open project, inked in 4FACTORY heading, always last 5Select (Import joins it on the phone) Tap a row to open that project."}, {"c": "Projects", "u": "projects.html", "h": "", "t": "#What a project holds Think of three boxes inside each other. 123 A sound sits inside a project. A project sits inside your host session.1Sound: one engine's tone 2Project: all three engines, keys, mix, patterns 3Host session: the project plus the host's own routing A project keeps: the sound on each engine, as it plays right now, even edits you never saved as a sound each engine's key, its key lock and Follow (chapter 30) the mix: levels, mutes and each engine's Main or Separate output (more in chapter 33) which pattern each engine plays, and the project's own pattern banks Hold and the notes it holds, and a latched arp. They come back sounding A project does not keep the app's own settings: paper, touch, MIDI channels and the Grid, Keys or Chords choice. Those stay with the app (chapter 34). Tip Edited sounds come back still marked with their asterisk. The project remembers your changes. The Library does not, until you save the sound (chapter 31)."}, {"c": "Projects", "u": "projects.html", "h": "", "t": "#Save, Save as new, New Three buttons sit at the top of the list. AT A GLANCEThe top row NewStarts a fresh project named Untitled 1, 2, 3 and so on. Init on all three engines, empty banks, no locks. SaveOpens the save choices: Save, Save as new..., Rename... and Cancel. Save as new...Makes a copy under a name you choose. The copy becomes the open project. The sound card has its own Save. That one saves a sound. This one saves a project. They never mix. Change anything and an asterisk (*) appears after the project's name. It means: different from the last save. Open another project, or tap New, while the asterisk shows, and INK asks. The card has Save, Save as new..., Discard changes and Cancel. Careful Save replaces the saved project with what you hear now. Use Save as new... to keep both."}, {"c": "Projects", "u": "projects.html", "h": "", "t": "#The Factory project A fresh INK opens on Factory. It sits last in the list under its own heading. 12 Factory is listed last. It has no rename and no delete.1Factory heading 2Factory row You can save Factory, and it keeps your changes. You can't rename or delete it. If you want your own, tap Save as new... and name it."}, {"c": "Projects", "u": "projects.html", "h": "", "t": "#Patterns belong to the project Every project has its own pattern banks, A to D. A pattern called B2 in one project can be different music in another. Moving a pattern between projects is a job for pattern import (chapter 28). A pattern has its own Save, apart from the project's (chapter 27). A pattern you edited but did not save stays with the project anyway. Open the project again and it returns still marked as edited, with the saved version waiting behind a Discard."}, {"c": "Projects", "u": "projects.html", "h": "", "t": "#What to save before you stop QUICK STEPSBefore you stop Save any sound you want in the Library. Save the patterns you want to keep as named versions. Save the project. In a host, save the host session too. Export a file if the work has to reach another phone. They overlap, but each has its own job. A host session remembers a detailed copy of the instance. A saved project does not remember your host's effects or the other apps around INK."}, {"c": "Projects", "u": "projects.html", "h": "", "t": "#Share a project Tap Select, tick projects, then tap Export. The file ends in .inkproject. The card works like the sound Export card in chapter 31. AT A GLANCEThe Export card for a project NameThe name of the project in the file. ByYour name. It travels with the file. File, Projects, Sounds, Kits, Recordings, SizeLines that list what goes in. ExportOpens the system share sheet. To bring one in, tap Import on the Projects list, not in the Library. A card tells you what arrived, as it does for sounds. Undo takes the import back. The same bar lets you star projects with Star. Delete them with Delete, and INK asks first. Factory is never counted. Under the hood A project is a folder on your device with its own pattern banks. The app also keeps a checkpoint of where you were. Close INK and open it again, and you are back where you left off, with Hold and its notes sounding. That checkpoint is for the app only. In a host, the host keeps the state."}, {"c": "Projects", "u": "projects.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSSave your Follow setup Set up the engines as in chapter 30, one leader and two followers. Tap the folder at the end of the tab row. Tap Save as new..., name it \"Follow test\" and confirm. Tap New. All three engines go back to Init. Open the list again and tap \"Follow test\". Your setup returns."}, {"c": "Use INK in a host", "u": "use-in-a-host.html", "h": "", "t": "INK runs on its own, and it also runs inside other music apps. A music app that loads instruments is called a host. Inside a host, INK is an AUv3 instrument."}, {"c": "Use INK in a host", "u": "use-in-a-host.html", "h": "", "t": "#Load it and play it Add INK Synth on an instrument track in your host. Send MIDI to it and connect its Main audio output to the host's mixer. That's all you need to hear it. 1234 INK in a host: MIDI goes in, audio comes out of Main.1MIDI from your keyboard 2INK Synth 3Main audio out 4Host mixer The three engines listen to MIDI channels 1, 2 and 3. Tapping a tab in INK only changes what you are editing. It does not change where MIDI goes. Your host decides the audio buffer, the transport and most of how INK behaves in the background. Use its normal track, MIDI and output controls. INK's tabs don't replace them."}, {"c": "Use INK in a host", "u": "use-in-a-host.html", "h": "", "t": "#Choose each engine's MIDI channel Open Settings (the cog). Under MIDI, each engine has a drop-down: Off, or Channel 1 to Channel 16. 12 Settings: AUDIO OUTPUTS and MIDI. One row per engine.1Audio output: Main or Separate 2MIDI channel Put two engines on the same channel when you want them to answer together. That makes a layered sound. An engine set to Off ignores MIDI, but you can still play it on the screen."}, {"c": "Use INK in a host", "u": "use-in-a-host.html", "h": "", "t": "#Automate Macro A, Macro B and Volume The host sees three controls for each engine. 1234 What the host's automation list offers.11: Macro A 21: Macro B 31: Volume 42: and 3: the same They appear as 1: Macro A, 1: Macro B, 1: Volume, then the same with 2: and 3:. That is nine controls in all. Everything else stays inside INK. That is less limited than it sounds. Macro A and Macro B can steer anything you wire to them in the Matrix (more in chapter 20). Wire A to the cutoff and to a little texture, then automate 1: Macro A in the host. One lane moves several things. Tip Test the whole range of a macro before you record a long take. Turn the host lane from 0 to full and listen for surprises."}, {"c": "Use INK in a host", "u": "use-in-a-host.html", "h": "", "t": "#The same three, by MIDI A MIDI controller can reach them too, on the engine's own channel. AT A GLANCEThree preferred controllers CC 1Macro A CC 30Macro B CC 7The engine's Level, the same as Level on the All page Every other controller is listed in the MIDI reference (chapter 37)."}, {"c": "Use INK in a host", "u": "use-in-a-host.html", "h": "", "t": "#Send an engine to its own track By default every engine plays through Main. To record or process one by itself, give it its own output. QUICK STEPSA separate output Open Settings. Under AUDIO OUTPUTS, find the engine. Change its drop-down from Main to Separate. In your host, switch on the output named Engine 1, Engine 2 or Engine 3. Route that output to a track. 123 Three engines. Engine 1 and 2 stay on Main, Engine 3 leaves on its own bus.1Main bus 2Engine 3 bus 3Host mixer strip A separate output carries that engine with its effects and its level. It leaves Main, so you won't hear it twice. Careful A switched-on output isn't always a connected one. If an engine's bus is off in the host, INK plays it through Main instead. If the bus is on but nothing is routed to it, you hear nothing from that engine. Check both ends. The standalone app plays every engine through Main. It still remembers the choice, for when you use it as a plug-in."}, {"c": "Use INK in a host", "u": "use-in-a-host.html", "h": "", "t": "#What reaches INK INK reads ordinary MIDI notes, aftertouch and pitch bend on an engine's channel. It also reads the MIDI clock and Start and Stop (more in chapter 29). 123 What INK takes in, and what it does not do yet.1Notes, aftertouch, bend, CC 1, 7, 30 2Clock, Start, Stop 3Not yet: MPE, MIDI out, Program Change Pitch bend moves every note on its channel together. It is not MPE, where each finger bends on its own. MPE in and out, an improved sequencer, and INK MIDI and INK FX as separate plug-ins, are on the Coming next list. They are not part of INK today. INK ignores Program Change, so you can't switch sounds from a keyboard yet. It doesn't read Song Position either. It doesn't send MIDI out. To capture what it plays, record its audio in the host."}, {"c": "Use INK in a host", "u": "use-in-a-host.html", "h": "", "t": "#Come back to it later Save the host session. It remembers a detailed copy of INK's state, with your sounds as they were. Keep a saved project and your Library sounds too, if you want to use them outside that session (chapter 32). MIDI channels and the Grid, Keys or Chords choice belong to INK's settings, not to a project. Check them when you move to another device. Under the hood The things that need the app, not the plug-in: recording or importing samples, the What's new sheet and the checkpoint that reopens INK where you left it. The AUv3 plays the kits you made in the app. Sound and project Import and Export work in both."}, {"c": "Use INK in a host", "u": "use-in-a-host.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSAutomate Macro A Load INK Synth on a track and send it MIDI. Play a note. On the Matrix tab, set the A column of the Cutoff row to about +50%. In your host, find the automation list for INK Synth. Pick 1: Macro A. Draw a slow curve from low to high, press play and hold a note."}, {"c": "Settings and background", "u": "settings-and-background.html", "h": "", "t": "One sheet holds INK's preferences. Tap the cog at the top left, left of the key. Choices apply at once and the sheet stays open. Close it with Done. 1234567 The whole Settings sheet. It scrolls. This picture is from a Mac, so TOUCH shows the mouse slider; on a phone it shows the play box and three sliders.1PAPER 2TOUCH 3AUDIO OUTPUTS 4MIDI 5KEYBOARD 6Slide control 7ABOUT INK"}, {"c": "Settings and background", "u": "settings-and-background.html", "h": "", "t": "#Every setting, one line each SectionSettingWhat it doesMore PAPERPaper, Dark, BlueprintThe look of the app. Not the sound.chapter 4 TOUCHA box to play inDraws what your finger just played, so you can see the effect of the three sliders.chapter 7 TOUCHSensitivityHow easily a touch makes expression. Less to More.chapter 7 TOUCHSmoothnessEvens out quick jumps, at the cost of a slower answer. Less to More.chapter 7 TOUCHCurveHow your touch range is spread. Heavy to Light.chapter 7 TOUCHMouse expressionOn a Mac with a mouse, this slider stands in for touch pressure.chapter 7 AUDIO OUTPUTSEngine 1, 2, 3: Main or SeparateSends an engine to its own output in a host.chapter 33 MIDIEngine 1, 2, 3: Off or Channel 1 to Channel 16The channel each engine listens to. Engine 1 starts on 1, Engine 2 on 2, Engine 3 on 3.chapter 33 KEYBOARDEngine 1, 2, 3: Grid, Keys, ChordsThe playing surface of each engine. Changing it turns that engine's Hold off.chapter 4 KEYBOARDSlide controlA fold with a box to practise sliding on Keys, and the three choices below.chapter 8 ABOUT INKWhat's newOpens the list of what each version added.below ABOUT INKInstagramOpens INK's Instagram page. The Slide control fold holds three choices, Piano, In tune and Free, and under In tune the Settle slider, from Quick to Slow. On a phone you see Sensitivity, Smoothness and Curve. With a mouse you see Mouse expression instead. The audio outputs row is a plug-in choice. The standalone app says it plays every engine through Ma"}, {"c": "Settings and background", "u": "settings-and-background.html", "h": "", "t": "#Where the other controls live Some things people look for in Settings are somewhere else. Looking forIt is in Root and Scale, key lock, FollowThe key menu: tap the key name (chapter 6) Hold, OctaveTop row, beside the key (chapter 7) Level and Mute for each engineThe All page (chapter 4) Tempo, Sync, patternsThe Seq tab (chapter 27) Recording a sampleThe kit view (chapter 22)"}, {"c": "Settings and background", "u": "settings-and-background.html", "h": "", "t": "#Background playback On iOS, the app can keep sounding when you switch to another app. This is background playback. INK mixes with whatever else is playing. It doesn't stay awake forever. INK has a sleep policy: when nothing is going on, it stops its audio, so iOS can pause the app and save your battery. 123 What keeps INK awake, and what lets it sleep.1Awake: sound, recording or MIDI notes 2Awake through short silences: a pattern, Hold or an arp 3Asleep: nothing for a few seconds AT A GLANCEWhat keeps INK awake SoundWhile sound is coming out, INK stays awake. Sound with the phone's volume at zero doesn't count. RecordingWhile you record a take. MIDI notesFor 30 seconds after the last note you played from MIDI. A MIDI port alone does not count. A pattern, Hold or an arpThrough silences up to a minute. A pattern that makes no sound lets INK sleep. With none of these, INK goes quiet after about five seconds. If the pattern is waiting for a clock that never arrives, INK will sleep. Don't count on it to keep running forever. If something sounds wrong after a long wait, come back to the app. In a host, none of this applies. The host owns the audio session and routing. Under the hood While INK is hidden, its screens stop drawing and catch up when you return. If INK's own plug-in is running in the host, the hidden app leaves MIDI notes to the plug-in so a key doesn't play twice. Note-offs still reach both, so nothing hangs."}, {"c": "Settings and background", "u": "settings-and-background.html", "h": "", "t": "#About INK At the bottom of Settings, ABOUT INK shows INK's version and build number in small pencil text. Tap What's new to open the sheet. It also opens by itself once, the first time you run the app after an update. It doesn't open on a first install, and never inside a host. 1235 The What's new sheet, with its two tabs.1What's new 2Coming next 3A tab for each version 5Instagram What's new lists what each version added, newest first, one plain line each. Small changes sit under ALSO. Coming next lists what is being built. Nothing there is in the app today, and no dates are promised. It names INK MIDI, Keys out as MPE, MPE in, an improved sequencer and INK FX. Close the sheet with Continue when it opened by itself, or Done when you opened it from Settings."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "Ten sounds to build from nothing. Each card lists what to set, the steps, and what you should hear. Do them in any order. These are starting methods, not the factory sounds. Your ears decide the last 10 percent. Where a factory sound is a close cousin, the card names it, so you can load it and compare."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#Before you start Every card begins the same way. QUICK STEPSThe usual start Open the Library and choose Start from scratch. That is the Init sound. Tap the key and set Root and Scale on purpose (more in chapter 6). Keep the level moderate. Add one thing at a time and listen after each. At the end, tap Save, then Save as new.... Init starts still. All four LFOs start with Depth at zero, so nothing moves on its own. Macro A and Macro B move a wire only when you turn them. Tip Starting from another sound instead? Check that Mod A and Mod B Depth are at zero. Otherwise A and B drift by themselves. In a card, a Matrix wire reads like this: Cutoff row, Env column, +40. Open the Matrix tab, find the row, drag the number in that column to about +40 (more in chapter 17)."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#1. Focused pulse bass A tight bass that thumps on every note and opens a little when you lean on it. Start with: Init, then Pulse in the generator drop-down. Compare: Rubber root, Low lantern. 12 The Cutoff row: Env at +40, A at +15. Read the wires. Where Cutoff itself sits is up to you.1Env to Cutoff 2A to Cutoff 123 Envelope 2 for the bass: quick rise, fall to nothing.1Attack at the bottom 2Decay in the middle 3Sustain at the bottom AT A GLANCEWhat to set WidthOSC. Turn it down until it reads about 50%. That is a square wave. IntervalOSC. Leave it at 0. AttackENVELOPE. Quick. DecayENVELOPE. Middle. Sustain: about half. Release: short. ModeFILTER. All the way down, to LP. CutoffFILTER. Low to middle. ResonanceFILTER. Just a little. Env LevelENVELOPE 2. Up. Set its Attack to the bottom, Decay to the middle, Sustain to the bottom. SendREVERB. Down to zero. Init starts with some. Stereo WidthMIX. Near the bottom, almost mono. QUICK STEPSBuild it Set the controls above, from the top down. Matrix wire: Cutoff row, Env column, about +40. Each note now opens, then closes. Matrix wire: Cutoff row, A column, about +15. Turn Macro A to the right to brighten. Play low. Tap Octave down and judge the lowest notes you will use. Save as new. You should hear: a short thump that pops open and closes on every note, and gets bitey under Macro A. If it goes wrong No attack? Raise the Env number or Env Level, or shorten Envelope 2 Attack. Thin? Check Width, High Pass in MIX and Mode."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#2. Supersaw pad with a bloom A pad that swells in, brightens when you press and drifts slowly while you hold. Start with: Init. Supersaw is already chosen. Compare: Distant choir, Silver canopy. 1234 The wires: Prs, A and X on Cutoff, and B on Reverb Send. Read the wires, not the number under Cutoff.1Prs to Cutoff 2X to Cutoff 3A to Cutoff 4B to Reverb Send AT A GLANCEWhat to set DetuneOSC. Moderate, about 10 to 15 ct. AttackENVELOPE. Long. Release: long. Sustain: high and steady. CutoffFILTER. Middle, a little dark. Leave room above and below. DepthLFO X. Up a little. Rate: slow. MixCHORUS / PHASER. Moderate, with the type on Chorus. Rate: slow. SendREVERB. Up until the room is clear. QUICK STEPSBuild it Set the controls above. Matrix wires: Cutoff row, Prs column, +40. Detune row, Prs column, +10. Matrix wire: Cutoff row, X column, +20. The pad now breathes on its own. Matrix wires: Cutoff row, A column, +25. REVERB group, Send row, B column, +25. Play four chords in a row. If they smear into one wash, shorten Release. You should hear: a slow swell, a shine that follows your finger, A for brightness and B for space."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#3. Vocal wavetable lead A single line with a vowel in it. It slides gently and keeps its pitch. Start with: Init, then Wavetable, then the bank Vocal. Compare: Vowel line. 12 The bank menu, with Vocal chosen.1Bank menu 2Position AT A GLANCEWhat to set PositionOSC. Turn it slowly. Find a narrow stretch where the vowel changes smoothly. AttackENVELOPE. Quick. Sustain: high. Release: middle. GlideOSC. A little. SendDELAY. Small, with the type on Classic and a short Time. QUICK STEPSBuild it Choose the bank and find your Position stretch. Matrix wires: Position row, A column, small. Cutoff row, Prs column, +30. Open the cog and set this engine to Keys. Leave Slide control on In tune (more in chapter 8). Play a phrase with small slides. Add a little vibrato with your finger. You should hear: a vowel that opens and closes as you turn A, and a line you can steer by sliding. Tip Keep the wires small. If the pitch loses its centre while you play vibrato, the Position wire is too big."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#4. Glass keys A struck bell that rings, fades and is different every time you play it. Start with: Init, then Wavetable, then the bank FM. Compare: Glass celesta, Mellow tines, Bell piano. 12 The Position row: Env at +25, Rnd at -10.1Env to Position 2Rnd, a small minus AT A GLANCEWhat to set PositionOSC. Turn it until you hear a bell or a tine. AttackENVELOPE. Quick. Decay: you can hear it fall. Sustain: low. Release: short. Env LevelENVELOPE 2. Up. Its Attack: quick. Decay: middle. Sustain: low. SendREVERB. Low. QUICK STEPSBuild it Set the controls above. Matrix wire: Position row, Env column, about +25. Each strike starts bright and settles. Matrix wire: the same row, Rnd column, a small minus, about -10. Each note starts a little different. Play the same key six times. No two should match. You should hear: a bright strike that softens into a round bell, and a small change on every note. If it goes wrong Harsh strike? Make the Env number smaller before you darken the whole sound. Notes blur together? Shorten Release."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#5. Frozen granular atmosphere A held moment from a recording, stretched into a pad you can steer. Start with: Low cello from the Library (set the Engine filter to Granular), or your own sustained recording (more in chapter 22). Compare: Prism cloud, Sleep current, Night shore. 12345 The SAMPLER section set for a frozen pad.1Pitched 2Loop slice 3Timestretch near zero 4Grain Sz 5Scrub AT A GLANCEWhat to set PitchedSAMPLER. The first drop-down. Loop sliceSAMPLER. The play style drop-down. RateSAMPLER. Leave it at the normal forward speed, 100. TimestretchSAMPLER. Down toward 0, until the sound hangs. ScrubSAMPLER. Turn it slowly and stop where the sound is rich and steady. Grain SzSAMPLER. Medium to long. Scrub jit.SAMPLER JITTER. A little. AttackENVELOPE. Long. Release: long. QUICK STEPSBuild it Load the sound and hold one note. Set the controls above. Matrix wire: Scrub row, X column, small. Set LFO X Rate slow and Depth up. Tick Freeze above LFO X. Each phrase now keeps one place in the recording while you hold. Matrix wire: Cutoff row, Y column, small, for a slow filter movement. Set LFO Y Depth up. Add Send in REVERB last, once the sound already works dry. You should hear: a steady, hanging tone that lands somewhere new each time you play a fresh phrase."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#6. Broken tape rhythm A sparse loop of chopped recording, with a bit of chaos on top. Start with: a recording with clear hits, in a Granular sound. 123 The kit view: slice lines placed on the hits.1Slice lines on the attacks 2Slice pads 3Tape and Play slice AT A GLANCEWhat to set TapeSAMPLER. The first drop-down. Each pad now picks a slice. Play sliceSAMPLER. The play style drop-down. ReleaseENVELOPE. Short. Grain SzSAMPLER. Short for a broken texture, long to keep the hits recognisable. Rate jit.SAMPLER JITTER. A touch. QUICK STEPSBuild it Open Kit and drag the slice lines onto the hits (more in chapter 23). Record a sparse pattern on the Seq tab. Use several different pads (more in chapter 27). Matrix wire: Rate row, A column, a minus number. Turn it up until a full A flips the playback backwards. Turn Macro A up at the end of a bar. The tape slows, then flips. You should hear: a loose, chopped groove that slips and reverses when you move A. Tip Keep one plain engine playing a dry beat underneath. It anchors the broken layer."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#7. Chords that carry the band One chord surface leads. A bass and a pad follow it, each in their own shape. Start with: three engines. Compare: none. This one is a setup, not a sound. 123 The All page: Engine 1 on Chords, Engines 2 and 3 holding notes.1Engine 1, Chords 2Engine 2, one held root 3Engine 3, a held shape QUICK STEPSSet it up Open the cog and set Engine 1 to Chords under KEYBOARD. Load a keys sound. Leave the chord engine on Smart. On tab 2, load a bass. Play one root note and tap Hold. Tap the key on Engine 2, find the Follow row, and choose 1. On tab 3, load a pad or an arp sound. Hold a small shape. Tap the key, choose Follow 1. Play I, vi, IV, V on row 1 of Engine 1. Use row 3 or 4 for a passing colour. Save a project, not just a sound (more in chapter 32). You should hear: all three layers move together with each chord, each keeping its own rhythm and tone. Careful A follower keeps its own shape. It does not copy the fancy notes of the lead chord. If an upper note clashes, change the follower's held shape. More about Follow in chapter 30."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#8. One-shot strummed keys A chord that rolls in note by note, like a hand across strings. Start with: a short Keys or Pluck sound. Compare: Paper koto, Old upright. 123 The Arp tab with Strum chosen.1Free / Arp / Strum 2Mode Up 3Division AT A GLANCEWhat to set StrumThe Arp tab switch. ModeArp tab. Up to begin with. DivisionArp tab. A fast free value for a close rake. A synced value, such as 4/32, to hear every note enter. AttackENVELOPE. Short. Release: long enough for the notes to overlap. EuclidArp tab. Keep it at 0 while you find the spacing. QUICK STEPSBuild it Choose Strum and set Mode to Up. Touch three pads together. Turn Division until the rake feels right. Switch the engine to Chords and play a chord. Every new chord is a fresh strum. For gaps, set Euclid to a length and lower Probability. You should hear: a roll across the strings, then the notes ringing on together (more in chapter 26). Tip Late notes missing? Make the held group longer, or speed up Division, before you touch the sound."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#9. Rising octave cloud Each note throws echoes that climb an octave at a time. Start with: a sparse Pluck. Compare: Clang field, Coin drop. 123 DELAY with the type set to Octave.1Delay type 2Send 3Feedback AT A GLANCEWhat to set NoiseOSC. Low. ReleaseENVELOPE. Modest. OctaveDELAY. The type drop-down. SendDELAY. Up until you hear the echoes. FeedbackDELAY. Medium. SendREVERB. A little, added last. Shimmer: a little, also last. QUICK STEPSBuild it Set the controls above, DELAY first. Play one note, then wait. Hear the echoes climb. Leave time between strikes. Matrix wires: DELAY group, Send row, A column, +30. REVERB group, Send row, B column, +30. Play a phrase. On its last note, turn A up and let the tail carry into the next bar. You should hear: a thin trail of echoes that rises by octaves, and a cloud that grows when you turn A. Careful Don't feed a dense chord progression at full strength. The octaves pile up fast."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#10. A macro that adds motion Turn one knob and the sound starts to move, more and more. Start with: Init, then Wavetable. Leave Position where it is. 12 Two wires: X moves Position, and A raises how much X moves.1X to Position 2A to LFO depth, in the LFO X group AT A GLANCEWhat to set ShapeLFO X. Sine or SmthRnd. DepthLFO X. Down to zero. MixCHORUS / PHASER. Leave at zero for now. QUICK STEPSBuild it Matrix wire: Position row, X column, about +40. Nothing moves yet, because LFO X Depth is zero. Matrix wire: LFO X group, LFO depth row, A column, +50. Turn Macro A to the right. The sound starts to move. Stop where it is lively but still yours. Optional: Mix row in CHORUS / PHASER, A column, small. The sound widens as it comes alive. You should hear: a still tone at low A, a living one at high A. Under the hood A does not move Position. It moves how much X moves Position. That is useful when a host automates one lane called 1: Macro A (more in chapter 33)."}, {"c": "Recipes", "u": "recipes.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSMake an eleventh Pick the card you liked best and build it again. Change one ingredient. Swap the generator, or the bank. Roll the dice once in the Matrix and keep one route (more in chapter 20). Save as new, with your own name."}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "Something sounds wrong? Don't guess. Follow the note on its trip, from your finger to the speaker, and check each stop in order. Each section below starts with what you hear. Under it you find if... then check... lines. Work from the top down. The cause is nearly always in the first three. 123456 The no-sound checklist, laid along a note's trip.1Does the note arrive? 2Is the generator making a tone? 3Does the envelope open? 4Is the filter open? 5Is the engine loud and unmuted? 6Does it reach the speaker?"}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#Find your symptom You hearGo to NothingNo sound Notes, but a pattern won't startNotes sound but the pattern is silent A note that won't stopA note keeps sounding A wire that does nothingModulation does nothing The wrong notesThe pitch is wrong Missing chord notesOnly some chord notes sound Harsh, thick or wobblyHarsh, muddy or unstable A preview unlike the soundA preview sounds different Another sound changedA change touched another sound A knob that isn't thereA control is missing"}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#No sound If you hear nothing at all, then check: The meters. Play a note. The vertical bar at the left of the card shows what leaves INK, all engines together. If it is crossed out, the OUTPUT slider is at zero. On the All page, each engine also has a small pencil meter under its Level fader. Mute and Level. Tap All. Is the engine's Mute on? Is its Level right down? A muted engine's meter stays flat. The OUTPUT slider on the All page. Is it down? The engine's tab. Are you playing the engine you think you are? The tab you are on is the one your fingers play. The sound itself. Check the long ones: a very long Attack, a very low Sustain, a closed Cutoff, an extreme High Pass in MIX. Wet only. Is Main Wet/Dry all the way wet while every Send is at zero? Then there is nothing to hear. 123 Engine 1 muted on the All page. Its meter stays flat.1Mute on 2Level 3Its meter, flat If it is the Granular sampler, check that the slot holds a recording. Open Kit and look for Empty or Missing. Pick a slot that has a sample, or another kit (more in chapter 23). If you play from a MIDI keyboard, open the cog. Is the engine's MIDI channel the same as the keyboard's? Off hears nothing. If INK runs in a host, check the host too: monitoring on, the track armed, and the output connected. If an engine is set to Separate, its Engine output must be on and routed in the host (more in chapter 33)."}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#Notes sound but the pattern is silent If notes play but a pattern or arp stays quiet, then check: Play. Is the Seq tab's Play on? In a host it shows DAW plays or DAW stopped instead. Start it from the host. Armed. A dashed ring on the engine's tab means it waits for the next bar (more in chapter 29). With an outside clock, check that the other device sends Start as well as Clock. Sync. With Sync on, the engine follows the shared clock. With it off, it runs at its own Tempo. The loop. Steps outside the loop are hatched. They sit on the page and never play. Direction, Probability, Euclid. Direction on Pause stops it. Probability at 0 skips every step. A Euclid length with a low Probability leaves gaps. The pattern. Is the one you picked empty? 12 A note outside the loop is hatched. It never plays.1Loop edge 2Hatched note"}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#A note keeps sounding If a note won't stop, then check: Hold. Is Hold on for that engine? Double-tap it to let everything go. A running source. Stop the pattern, or switch the arp to Free. A tail, not a note. A long Release and a Delay or Reverb tail keep going after the note ends. Lower the sends, or shorten the Release. A controller. A MIDI controller can switch Hold on (CC 69), or keep notes sounding with its sustain pedal (CC 64) until the pedal lifts. 12 Hold is on. Double-tap it to release every note.1Hold on 2Double-tap to release Careful Mute only makes the engine quiet. Its notes and pattern keep running underneath. Tip On a Mac with a keyboard, the Escape key lets go of the playing notes and stops playback, when the grid has the focus. If a sheet is open, Escape may close the sheet instead."}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#Modulation does nothing If you wired something and hear no change, then check, in this order: The cell. Open the Matrix. Is the number in the cell zero? A zero is a dot. The column. Did you use the source you meant? The letters are Env, Prs, A, B, X, Y and Rnd (more in chapter 17). The source itself. Env needs Env Level above zero. X and Y need their own Depth above zero. A and B only move when you turn Macro A or Macro B. Prs only moves when you press. Headroom. A control already at its limit can't go further in that direction. Move the starting value away from the limit. Freeze. A frozen LFO holds one value for the phrase. The engine. Matrix and Sound belong to one engine. Are you on the right tab? 123 The wire is there, but the cell is zero, shown as a dot.1Source column 2Cell at zero 3Row with no wire Two surprises: A moves by itself. Check Mod A Depth. Above zero, Mod A adds its own wobble to A. The same goes for B. X doesn't follow my finger. X and Y are slow wobbles, not coordinates on the screen (more in chapter 19). Careful On the Granular sampler the oscillator Shape row does not exist. A wire to a control that isn't there does nothing."}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#The pitch is wrong If the notes are not the ones you expect, then check: The key. Root and Scale in the key menu. A padlock before the name means it is locked. Register. Octave, in the key menu or on the Sound tab. The layout. Degree and Stride move where the scale starts and how far apart the strings are. The wobblers. Interval, Microtone, Glide and any wire in the PITCH group of the Matrix. Follow. An engine following another one moves with its leader's chords (more in chapter 30). Keys. Slide control on Free leaves a slide where you stop, between the notes. MIDI. Is a controller sending pitch bend on that channel? Granular, Pitched. A wrong root note on a slice, or an odd Rate, tunes everything the wrong way (more in chapter 23). 123 The first stop for pitch: the key menu.1Root 2Scale 3Lock A follower can run in a different scale from its leader. The same step count then moves it a different number of semitones. That is Follow working, not a fault."}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#Only some chord notes sound If some notes of a chord are missing, then check: Voices. Each engine plays eight notes at once. A chord, a held bass and a running arp can use them all up. Releases. Long Release tails still hold voices while new chords arrive. Samples. On the Granular sampler, a note may point at an empty slice or a missing sample. Strum. A note let go before its turn is skipped. A pattern step that ends too early cuts the late strings (more in chapter 26). Your device. A touch screen only reports so many fingers at once. That limit is the screen's, not the synth's."}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#Harsh, muddy or unstable If it hurts the ears, then lower, one at a time: Distort Resonance in FILTER, and LPG Filter in OSC a very narrow Width on Pulse fast movement on Position Rate jit. on the sampler If it is thick and muddy, then: shorten Release shorten Reverb Time lower delay Feedback raise High Pass in MIX a little, to clear the bottom play the chord with fewer notes, or more open If the pitch wobbles, then look at every pitch wire before you touch the Touch settings. A wire on Root, Pitch, Interval or Microtone is the usual suspect. Tip Compare sounds at the same volume, and listen with the whole band. A pad that feels thin alone often sits right beside a bass. A big solo pad can take up everyone's room."}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#A preview sounds different If a preview doesn't match the sound, then remember: The play button in the Library plays its own little performance, not your gesture. A press on the raw waveform in the kit view skips the grains, the filter and the effects. The play marks in the sample list play the raw recording. The slice pads play through the sound, so they sound like the real thing. Compare like with like before you decide something is wrong."}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#A change touched another sound If you changed one sound and another one changed, then check: A shared sample. Trim, Normalised and the channel mix belong to the sample, wherever it is used. Duplicate the sample first if other sounds must keep it as it was (more in chapter 23). A shared pattern. A pattern slot saved from one engine is the same slot for any engine that uses it. Use Save as new for the pattern when you want a separate version (more in chapter 28). A sound stays its own, even when the recording under it is shared."}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#A control is missing If a knob is not where you expect it, then check: The generator. Granular hides Shape (Detune, Width, Position) and LPG Filter. It leads with the SAMPLER section instead. A greyed knob. Greyed means \"not used right now\": Mode while Band-pass is ticked. Octaves on Strum. Stride and Degree on Chords. Their jobs are Voicing and Spread there. Wobble and 2nd Tap in the delay types that don't use them. A renamed knob. Center becomes Sweep on Phaser. Shape reads Detune, Width or Position. Another place. Scroll the Sound tab, or tap a section title to jump. The app and the plug-in. Recording and importing audio belong to the standalone app. Separate outputs belong to hosts. The What's new sheet shows in the app only. Not here yet. Anything listed under Coming next in What's new is not in the app today. 12 On the Granular sampler the Sound tab starts with SAMPLER. Shape is gone.1SAMPLER section first 2No Shape knob"}, {"c": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSFind a silent engine Tap All. Turn Mute on for Engine 1. Play a note on Engine 1's keyboard band. Nothing sounds, and its small meter stays flat. Turn Mute off. The note sounds, and the meter moves. On the Sound tab, turn Cutoff right down and play again. The sound is dull and very quiet. This time the cause is in the sound, not the mixer."}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "Plug in a controller, pick a channel, turn a knob. Three controls work best: Macro A, Macro B and Volume. The rest of this page is a lookup table."}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Set the channel Each engine listens on one MIDI channel. Open the cog and find MIDI. Choose Off or Channel 1 to Channel 16 for each engine. 1234 Settings, MIDI: one channel per engine.1Engine 1 2Engine 2 3Engine 3 4Off or Channel 1 to 16 By default Engine 1 listens on channel 1, Engine 2 on 2 and Engine 3 on 3. Put two engines on the same channel and both answer, layered. Off means that engine hears no MIDI at all. Everything below goes to the engine whose channel matches the message."}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#The three to start with 123 Three knobs on your controller, three controls in INK.1CC 1, Macro A 2CC 30, Macro B 3CC 7, Volume CCControlName in a host 1Macro A1: Macro A 30Macro B1: Macro B 7Volume1: Volume The names in a host start with the engine number. Engine 2 shows \"2: Macro A\", and so on (more in chapter 33). Volume is the engine's whole level, the same as its fader on the All page. It is not Synth Lvl in MIX. Macro A and B move whatever you wired to them in the Matrix. That makes them the best way to reach anything that has no CC of its own (see below). Tip Want to play the filter from a knob? In the Matrix, put a number in the A column of the Cutoff row. Then send CC 1. Turning your knob turns Macro A, and Macro A opens the filter."}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Switches CCControlSend 101Hold0 for off, 127 for on 102Arp switch0 selects Free, 127 turns Arp on. If Strum was chosen, it stays on Strum."}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Direct controls These controls have a CC of their own. A CC moves the control just like turning its knob: 0 is the lowest value, 127 the highest. Controls that step, such as Mode or Division, jump from step to step."}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Tone and pitch CCControlOn screen 2NoiseOSC 3SensitivityENVELOPE 4DistortOSC 5GlideOSC 9PitchOSC 13Shape (reads Detune, Width or Position)OSC 14IntervalOSC 71LPG FilterOSC"}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Main envelope CCControl 72Release 73Attack 74Sustain 75Decay"}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Envelope 2 CCControl 19Env Level 20Attack 21Decay 22Sustain 23Release"}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#LFOs CCMod AMod BLFO XLFO Y Rate24277679 Depth25287780 Offset26297881 Mod A and Mod B have no Offset knob on the Sound tab. Their Offset CCs are in the map, but you can't see them."}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Effects and mix CCControlOn screen 8Main Wet/DryMIX 31High PassMIX 12TimeDELAY 94SendDELAY 95FeedbackDELAY 1122nd TapDELAY 113WobbleDELAY (Classic) 91SendREVERB 92TimeREVERB 93ShimmerREVERB 114WobbleREVERB"}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Sampler CCControlOn screen 82Sample (the slot)SAMPLER 15ScrubSAMPLER 16Grain SzSAMPLER 17RateSAMPLER 18TimestretchSAMPLER 116Scrub jit.SAMPLER JITTER 117Grain Sz jit.SAMPLER JITTER 118Rate jit.SAMPLER JITTER"}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Arp and Seq CCControlOn screen 103ModeArp 104DivisionArp 105ProbabilityArp 106EuclidArp 107OctavesArp 11GateArp and Seq (one knob) 108DirectionSeq 109DivisionSeq 110ProbabilitySeq 111EuclidSeq 83PatternSeq 85Step offsetSeq"}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Not in the map These have no CC of their own: the FILTER section: Cutoff, Mode, Resonance and Filter Spread the CHORUS / PHASER section the key's Scale and Octave, and every cell of the Matrix Wire what you want to Macro A or Macro B in the Matrix, then play it with CC 1 or CC 30."}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#What else INK listens to INK understands: notes, from any controller on the engine's channel aftertouch and pitch bend on that channel MIDI clock, Start, Continue and Stop (more in chapter 29) Bend and aftertouch act on the whole channel. They are not per-finger MPE. Not in this version INK sends no MIDI. There is no MIDI Learn and no MPE. Program Change is ignored, so you can't switch sounds from a controller. Song Position is not read: Continue picks up where INK stopped. INK MIDI, MPE, an improved sequencer and INK FX appear under Coming next in What's new."}, {"c": "MIDI controller reference", "u": "midi-controller-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSPlay the filter from a knob Open the cog. Check that Engine 1's MIDI channel matches your controller. Load a Pad. On the Sound tab, turn Cutoff down to about the middle. Open the Matrix tab. Put about +50 in the A column of Cutoff. Assign a knob on your controller to CC 1. Hold a chord and turn the knob. The filter opens and closes. Under the hood CC 1, CC 7 and CC 30 are INK's own. They never reach the old Plinky map, which used CC 7 for Synth Lvl. Every other CC moves a control's starting value, the same as turning its knob (more in chapter 3). Save the sound if you like where it ended up."}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "One page of tables. Look up, glance, go back to playing. Every row points to the chapter that explains it."}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Which control for the job? You want toStart hereMore Make a saw thickerDetune on Supersawch 11 Make a pulse tone moveWidth, plus a small X wire to the Width rowch 11 Move through harmonic coloursPosition on Wavetablech 11 Explore a recordingScrub, Timestretch and Grain Szch 21 Darken or brightenCutoffch 13 Make it singResonancech 13 Open the tone when you pressA Prs wire to Cutoffch 17 Shape the tone after each strikeAn Env wire to Cutoffch 18 Make every note a little differentAn Rnd wirech 18 Add a slow wobbleAn X or Y wirech 19 Change how a note starts and endsAttack and Releasech 12 Add echoesSend in DELAYch 15 Add spaceSend in REVERBch 16 Keep notes after you liftHoldch 7 Repeat a held groupArpch 24 Play the group once, in orderStrumch 26 Make engines follow chord degreesFollow in the follower's key menuch 30 Set up a new chord arrangementThe Voicing and Spread rails, before the strikech 10 Change how slides landSettings, KEYBOARD, Slide controlch 8 Play in another scaleThe key menu, Scalech 6 Give a host one musical controlA Matrix wire to Macro A or Macro Bch 33 Change one engine's loudnessLevel on the All page, or CC 7ch 16 Process engines separatelySettings, AUDIO OUTPUTS, Separate, plus the hostch 33 Keep a soundSave as new...ch 31 Keep the whole setupSave the projectch 32 Take it backSwipe in from the left edgech 5"}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Gestures at a glance Do thisGet this Drag a knob up or downChange it. Slow is fine, a quick sweep is big. Rest on a knob, then dragTiny steps. With a mouse, hold Shift. Double-tap a knobBack to its reference value Double-tap a Matrix cellThe wire clears to zero Swipe fast starting on a knobThe page scrolls Tap a section titleThe section list opens Tap the keyThe key menu opens Tap the sound's nameThe Library opens Tap a held padThat note lets go Double-tap HoldEvery held note lets go Tap the chevronThe card folds to one row Drag the Knobs handleThe controls get more room, the grid less Tap the waveform on ChordsThe chord piano opens Swipe in from the left edgeUndo Swipe in from the right edgeRedo Swipe left or right with three fingersUndo or redo, anywhere (useful in a host)"}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Where things live AT A GLANCEAlong the top SettingsThe cog. Look, touch, MIDI, outputs, keyboards, About. KeyTap for Root, Scale, Lock, Free / Arp / Strum and Follow. HoldKeeps notes sounding. 1 2 3 AllThe three engines, and the mixer. PlaceWhat is in it The cardWaveform, output level bar, voice dashes, sound name, Save, previous and next arrows Sound tabThe tone: generator, filter, envelopes, LFOs, effects, mix Matrix tabWires from sources to controls, and the dice Arp tabFree, Arp or Strum, and their knobs Seq tabPatterns: record, edit, play AllMixer on top, a keyboard for each engine below SettingsPAPER, TOUCH, AUDIO OUTPUTS, MIDI, KEYBOARD, ABOUT INK"}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#The Sound tab, section by section 123456789 The Sound tab's sections, in the order you scroll through them.1OSC 2FILTER 3ENVELOPE 4ENVELOPE 2 5MOD A, MOD B, LFO X, LFO Y 6CHORUS / PHASER 7DELAY 8REVERB 9MIX SectionControls SAMPLER (Granular only, first)Tape or Pitched, play style, Kit, Sample, Scrub, Rate, Grain Sz, Timestretch SAMPLER JITTERScrub jit., Grain Sz jit., Rate jit. OSCGenerator, Macro A and B, Detune / Width / Position, Distort, Octave, Glide, Interval, Noise, LPG Filter, Degree, Microtone, Stride FILTERBand-pass box, Cutoff, Mode, Resonance, Filter Spread ENVELOPESensitivity, Attack, Decay, Sustain, Release ENVELOPE 2Env Level, Attack, Decay, Sustain, Release MOD A, MOD BDepth, Rate, Shape, Warp LFO X, LFO YFreeze box, Depth, Rate, Shape, Warp, Offset CHORUS / PHASERType, Mix, Center (Sweep), Depth, Rate, Feedback, Tone, Stereo DELAYType, Send, Time, 2nd Tap, Wobble, Feedback REVERBSend, Time, Shimmer, Wobble MIXSynth Lvl, Main Wet/Dry, High Pass, Stereo Width"}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#The Matrix and the other tabs Matrix sources EnvEnvelope 2 PrsPressure A, BMacro A, Macro B X, YLFO X, LFO Y RndA random number for each note Matrix groups: OSC, FILTER, PITCH, AMP ENV, ENV 2, SAMPLER, SAMPLE JITTER, MOD A, MOD B, LFO X, LFO Y, ARP, CHORUS / PHASER, DELAY, REVERB, MIX. TabKnobs, left to right ArpTempo, Sync, Mode, Division, Probability, Euclid, Octaves, Gate SeqTempo, Sync, Direction, Division, Probability, Euclid, Gate, Step offset, Quantize"}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Lists to keep handy Generators: Supersaw, Pulse, Wavetable, Granular sampler. Wavetable banks: Basic, Organ, Acoustic, Vocal, FM, Chip, Fold, Grain. Filter Mode: LP, LP / Notch, Notch, Notch / HP, HP. Delay types: Classic, Reverse, Octave. Chorus or Phaser: one slot, two types. LFO shapes (13): Triangle, Sine, SmthRnd, StepRnd, BiSquare, Square, Castle, Saw, BiTrigs, Trigs, Env, Geiger, Perlin. Arp Mode (15): Up, Down, Up/Down, Up/Down Repeat, Pedal Up, Pedal Down, Pedal Up/Down, Rnd, 2xRnd, Chord, Up 8 Steps, Down 8 Steps, Up/Down 8 Steps, Rnd 8 Steps, 2xRnd 8 Steps. Seq Direction: Pause, Forward, Reverse, Pingpong, PingPong Rep, Random. Playing surfaces: Grid, Keys, Chords. Themes: Paper, Dark, Blueprint."}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Build a sound in order 123456 Six steps, from the source to the space around it.1Generator and register 2Main envelope 3Filter 4One expressive wire 5Macro A and B 6Effects Choose the source and the register. Generator, then Octave (chapters 11 and 6). Shape the main envelope (chapter 12). Find the filter tone (chapter 13). Add one expressive wire. Pressure or Envelope 2 (chapters 17 and 18). Design A and B (chapter 20). Add effects last. Chorus, delay, reverb, mix (chapters 14 to 16). Then check the sound. Play low notes and high notes, soft and firm, one note and a full chord."}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Build a performance in order Give each engine a job. Bass, chords, pad, lead (chapter 3). Balance the levels on the All page. Connect the timing (chapter 29). Connect the harmony. Use Follow where a shape should move with the chords. Use a separate pattern where a line should stand alone (chapter 30). Save the project at a point you like, before you try something wild (chapter 32)."}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Keep the pairs apart 12345 Five pairs people mix up.1Hold and Release 2Arp and Strum 3Sample and sound 4Kit and project 5Slide control and Glide Not thisBut this Hold keeps notes in memory.Release is the tail of the sound after a note ends. Arp repeats a group again and again.Strum plays the group once, in order. A sample is a recording.A sound is how INK plays it. A kit is a set of eight samples.A project is the whole setup. Slide control decides which notes your finger lands on.Glide decides how the sound travels between them. Level is the whole engine's loudness, tails included.Synth Lvl is how loud the raw synth is inside the sound's mix. Mute silences the engine.Hold and the pattern keep running underneath. Save replaces the sound you started from.Save as new... keeps both. Undo is for exploring.Save is for keeping. Engine 1, 2, 3 are the three instruments.A generator is how one of them makes its tone."}, {"c": "Quick reference", "u": "quick-reference.html", "h": "", "t": "#Try it QUICK STEPSFind your way with only this page Make a sound darker. Find the control in the first table, then do it. Find the gesture that lets go of every held note. Find which tab holds Euclid. Name the five things you add last in a sound. That is the guide. Go play."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-all-page", "h": "All page", "t": "The tab that shows all three engines together: a small mixer on top and a keyboard for each."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-arms", "h": "arms", "t": "Waits for the next bar before starting, so it lands in time. Its tab shows a dashed ring while it waits."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-attack", "h": "Attack", "t": "How fast a note rises when you play it."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-audition", "h": "audition", "t": "Hear a sound without loading it. Your own sound stays as it was."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-auv3-instrument", "h": "AUv3 instrument", "t": "INK as a plug-in. You load it on an instrument track in a host and send it MIDI."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-background-playback", "h": "background playback", "t": "INK keeps making sound after you leave it, and mixes with the other app's audio."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-band-pass", "h": "Band-pass", "t": "A tick box that makes the filter keep only a band around Cutoff, in place of the Mode blend."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-bank", "h": "bank", "t": "One of eight sets of wave shapes a Wavetable sound can read. Basic, Organ, Acoustic, Vocal, FM, Chip, Fold and Grain."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-base-value", "h": "base value", "t": "Where a control starts. Modulation pushes it up and down from there; the knob shows only this starting point."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-cell", "h": "cell", "t": "One square of the Matrix. It holds the depth of one wire, from one source to one destination."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-chord-engine", "h": "chord engine", "t": "The method INK uses to choose and voice chords. There are three: Smart, Smooth and Stock."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-chord-piano", "h": "chord piano", "t": "The card's waveform turns into three octaves of piano keys, with a dot on every note sounding now."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-chorus", "h": "chorus", "t": "An effect that doubles the sound with slightly moving copies, so one note sounds like several."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-cutoff", "h": "Cutoff", "t": "Where the filter acts on the sound. In low-pass mode, turning it down removes the highs and darkens the sound."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-decay", "h": "Decay", "t": "How fast a note falls from its peak toward the Sustain level."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-degree", "h": "Degree", "t": "Where the Grid starts in the scale. Raise it and the whole layout starts higher up."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-degrees", "h": "degrees", "t": "The steps of the key's scale, numbered from the root. The Chords surface has a column for each one."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-density", "h": "density", "t": "With Euclid in Strum, Probability sets how many of the loop's steps are strikes. The rest are gaps. It never rolls dice."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-depth", "h": "depth", "t": "How much a wire pushes. It shows as a number from -100 to +100 in the cell."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-destination", "h": "destination", "t": "A control a source can push. Destinations are the rows of the Matrix."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-detune", "h": "Detune", "t": "How far apart the Supersaw's saw waves are tuned. Low is tight and focused, high is wide and beating."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-dice", "h": "dice", "t": "The die in the Matrix corner. Tap it and it rolls a random set of wires to start a patch from."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-direction", "h": "Direction", "t": "The order the sequencer walks through the loop: forwards, backwards, to and fro, or shuffled."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-distort", "h": "Distort", "t": "Adds grit and density to the tone. It can also make the sound louder, so compare at the same volume."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-edit-bar", "h": "edit bar", "t": "The strip under the lanes. It shows what is selected and the buttons that change it."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-engine", "h": "engine", "t": "One of INK's three instruments, tabs 1, 2 and 3. Each has its own sound, notes, MIDI channel and effects."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-env", "h": "Env", "t": "The first column of the Matrix. It carries Envelope 2 to whatever you wire it to."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-envelope-2", "h": "Envelope 2", "t": "A second envelope that does not control loudness. It only exists to push other controls, through the Env column of the Matrix."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-face", "h": "face", "t": "The number the dice shows, one to six. A higher face means more wires."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-factory", "h": "Factory", "t": "The project INK opens on the first time. It holds the factory patterns, and it can be saved but never renamed or deleted."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-filter-spread", "h": "Filter Spread", "t": "Sets the left and right sides of each voice's filter to slightly different Cutoffs, so the sound gets wider and voices differ from each other."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-first-note-sounds-at-once", "h": "first note sounds at once", "t": "In Strum, the first note of a group plays the instant you touch it. Nothing waits for the rest of the chord."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-follow", "h": "Follow", "t": "A setting that makes an engine move its notes along with the chords another engine plays."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-follower", "h": "follower", "t": "An engine that moves its notes by the leader's chord changes."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-free-speed", "h": "free speed", "t": "An arp speed that ignores the tempo and runs at its own pace. On the Division knob it shows as a plain number with no /32."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-freeze", "h": "Freeze", "t": "A box on LFO X and LFO Y. It catches the wobble's value when a phrase starts and holds it until every note has gone quiet."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-glide", "h": "Glide", "t": "How long a note takes to travel to a new pitch."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-grain", "h": "grain", "t": "A very short piece of the sample, a few milliseconds up to a fraction of a second, played in overlap with many others."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-grid", "h": "Grid", "t": "INK's default playing surface: eight columns by eight rows of pads."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-hatching", "h": "hatching", "t": "The pencil shading inside a note bar. Denser hatching means you pressed harder at that moment."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-headroom", "h": "headroom", "t": "The distance a control can still move in the direction a wire pushes. A knob already at its limit has none."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-high-pass", "h": "High Pass", "t": "A filter that cuts the lowest notes and lets the rest through."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-hold", "h": "Hold", "t": "Latches notes so they keep sounding after you lift your finger."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-host", "h": "host", "t": "A music app that loads other instruments inside it, such as AUM. INK runs inside it as an AUv3."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-host-session", "h": "host session", "t": "The state INK saves inside your host app's session, so it comes back when you reopen the song."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-init", "h": "Init", "t": "The empty starting sound, listed under Start from scratch. Load it to build a sound from nothing."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-interval", "h": "Interval", "t": "Moves some of a voice's oscillators up or down by a number of semitones, so one note sounds like two pitches together."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-jitter", "h": "Jitter", "t": "A small random wobble added to a setting for every grain, so no two grains are quite the same."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-key", "h": "key", "t": "The home note (Root) and the family of notes (Scale) an engine plays in, such as C Major."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-key-lock", "h": "Key Lock", "t": "Protects an engine's key from changes made on the other engines and from keys carried by patterns."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-key-menu", "h": "key menu", "t": "The card that opens when you tap the key. Its two big knobs are Root and Scale."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-kit", "h": "Kit", "t": "Eight sample slots a sound can use."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-kit-view", "h": "kit view", "t": "The screen where you see a kit's eight slots, fill them, and edit their samples."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-lanes", "h": "lanes", "t": "One of the eight rows on the Seq tab. Each lane is one column of the Grid, and the top lane is column 8."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-leader", "h": "leader", "t": "The engine on the Chords surface whose chord changes the followers copy."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-lfo", "h": "LFO", "t": "A wobble that runs by itself, round and round, whether you play or not. It moves whatever you wire it to."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-library", "h": "library", "t": "The list of every sound INK has, factory and your own. Tap the sound name to open it."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-linked", "h": "linked", "t": "Engines whose keys move together: change one, and the others change with it."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-loop", "h": "loop", "t": "The stretch of steps that repeats. Steps outside it are hatched and stay silent."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-low-pass-gate", "h": "low-pass gate", "t": "A gate in every voice that opens and brightens the tone as the note gets louder. It comes from Plinky."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-lpg-filter", "h": "LPG Filter", "t": "How resonant the built-in low-pass gate sounds. Not the same as the Filter section."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-macro", "h": "macro", "t": "A knob made for playing. Every engine has two, Macro A and Macro B, and each sound decides what they change."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-main", "h": "Main", "t": "The final output where the three engines meet. It is the sound you hear."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-main-envelope", "h": "main envelope", "t": "The curve that shapes each note's loudness over time: how it starts, how long it holds and how it ends. It also steers the gate's brightness."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-main-wet-dry", "h": "Main Wet/Dry", "t": "The balance between the dry sound and what comes back from the delay and reverb."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-master-clock", "h": "master clock", "t": "The beat the synced engines share. With no host or outside clock, it is Engine 1's own."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-matrix", "h": "Matrix", "t": "The tab where you wire things together: a grid with sources across the top and controls down the side."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-microtone", "h": "Microtone", "t": "How free the pitch is between the Grid's notes."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-mode", "h": "Mode", "t": "Slides the filter between low-pass (keeps the lows), notch (cuts a band in the middle) and high-pass (keeps the highs)."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-noise", "h": "Noise", "t": "Mixes noise into each voice. A little adds breath and attack detail; a lot makes a rough texture."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-octave", "h": "octave", "t": "A jump of twelve notes: the same note, higher or lower."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-pack", "h": "pack", "t": "A set of sounds exported together as one .inksounds file, with a name and an author."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-pad", "h": "pad", "t": "One square on the Grid. Touch it to play a note."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-page-strip", "h": "page strip", "t": "Four small boxes above the lanes, one per page of sixteen steps. The page you see is outlined."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-pattern", "h": "Pattern", "t": "Notes and movement you recorded, plus their timing."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-phaser", "h": "phaser", "t": "An effect that sweeps hollow notches through the sound, so it seems to swirl."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-piano-room", "h": "Piano Room", "t": "A free pack of seven keys sounds that comes with INK. Find it under From."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-pitch", "h": "Pitch", "t": "Tunes the whole sound up or down, up to twelve semitones either way. It is part of the sound, like Octave. The key's home note is Root."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-pitched", "h": "Pitched", "t": "Sample mode where INK plays the slice whose root note is nearest the note you ask for, then transposes it."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-playing-surface", "h": "playing surface", "t": "The way an engine is played: Grid, Keys or Chords. You choose one per engine in Settings."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-polarity", "h": "polarity", "t": "Whether a wire pushes a control up (plus) or down (minus)."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-polyrhythm", "h": "polyrhythm", "t": "Two or more loops of different lengths playing together, so their pattern keeps shifting."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-position", "h": "Position", "t": "Where you are along the bank, from 1 to 8. It changes the tone's harmonics, not a recording's play point."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-pressure", "h": "pressure", "t": "How hard you press a pad, which INK reads from how much of your fingertip touches the glass."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-project", "h": "project", "t": "The whole instrument saved under one name: all three engines, their sounds, keys, mix and patterns."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-quantize", "h": "Quantize", "t": "A knob on Seq that moves recorded notes toward the nearest step. 0% leaves them as played, 100% snaps them."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-rails", "h": "rails", "t": "The two bars under the Chords tiles, Voicing and Spread. Each is a row of eight boxes that fill as you drag."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-read-at-the-strike", "h": "read at the strike", "t": "Rails shape the next chord you play. They never change a chord that is already sounding."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-redo", "h": "redo", "t": "Bring back a change you just undid. Swipe in from the right edge."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-reference-value", "h": "reference value", "t": "The value a sound was saved with. Double-tap a knob to return to it. It is not the same as zero."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-release", "h": "Release", "t": "How long a note takes to fade after you let go."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-resonance", "h": "Resonance", "t": "Pushes up the sound around the Cutoff point, so a sweep gets a ringing, vocal edge."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-rnd", "h": "Rnd", "t": "The last column of the Matrix. Every new note draws its own random number, so each note can be a little different."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-root", "h": "Root", "t": "The home note of the key. It runs from C to B, one note at a time."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-root-note", "h": "root note", "t": "The pitch a slice was recorded at. Pitched mode uses it to work out how far to transpose."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-sample", "h": "Sample", "t": "A recording the granular sampler can play."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-sample-slot", "h": "sample slot", "t": "One of the eight places in a kit that can hold a sample."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-scale", "h": "Scale", "t": "The set of notes the pads play. Major, Minor and Hirajoshi are scales."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-scale-steps", "h": "scale steps", "t": "The distance between neighbouring notes of the scale. Moving up one step goes to the next note of the key, not the next semitone."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-scrub", "h": "Scrub", "t": "Moves the point in the sample where grains read from. Slowly turning it explores the recording."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-section-list", "h": "section list", "t": "The list that opens when you tap a section title. Choose a name to jump straight there."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-sem-style-filter", "h": "SEM-style Filter", "t": "INK's main filter, modelled on the filter of a classic Oberheim synth. It sits after each voice's own gate and works on every generator."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-send", "h": "send", "t": "A knob that decides how much of a sound goes into an effect. Zero sends nothing."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-settings", "h": "Settings", "t": "App preferences: look, touch feel, MIDI channels and which playing surface each engine uses."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-settle", "h": "Settle", "t": "How quickly In tune brings a stopped slide onto its note, from Quick to Slow."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-shape", "h": "Shape", "t": "The one tone knob of an oscillator sound. It is drawn as Detune for Supersaw, Width for Pulse and Position for Wavetable."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-shimmer", "h": "Shimmer", "t": "A reverb tail with a rising, pitched glow added to it."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-sleep-policy", "h": "sleep policy", "t": "The rule that lets INK go quiet and sleep when nothing is happening, so it doesn't drain your battery."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-slices", "h": "slices", "t": "One of the eight regions inside a sample. A slice marks where a note starts playing."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-slide-control", "h": "Slide control", "t": "A setting for Keys with three choices. Piano plays each key you cross as a new note. In tune slides and settles on the note when you stop. Free slides and stays where you stop."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-slot", "h": "slot", "t": "The single place in an engine's effects where Chorus or Phaser lives. It holds one at a time."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-sound", "h": "sound", "t": "A ready-made setup for an engine: its tone, its movement and its effects. Pad, Lead and Bass are kinds of sound."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-sound-generator", "h": "sound generator", "t": "The piece of an engine that makes the raw tone. INK has four: Supersaw, Pulse, Wavetable and Granular sampler."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-source", "h": "source", "t": "Something that moves: your pressure, an envelope, a macro, a slow wobble or a random number. Sources are the columns of the Matrix."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-spice", "h": "Spice", "t": "A button that adds colour to the next chord, such as a jazz or soul tension. It keeps the chord's job and dresses it up."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-spread", "h": "Spread", "t": "A rail that widens a chord. High settings double notes in other octaves."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-step", "h": "step", "t": "Jump from one setting to the next instead of gliding. Octave, Scale and Sample are stepped controls."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-step-offset", "h": "Step offset", "t": "Shifts where the loop starts reading, without moving any note. It gives the same pattern another entrance."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-steps", "h": "steps", "t": "One tick of a pattern. Each note starts on a step. Steps are shown in four pages of sixteen."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-stereo-width", "h": "Stereo Width", "t": "How wide the sound sits between the two speakers, from mono to its normal stereo."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-stride", "h": "Stride", "t": "How far one string sits from the next, counted in notes of the scale. A bigger Stride puts the strings further apart."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-string", "h": "string", "t": "One column of the Grid. Eight strings sit side by side, and notes rise from the bottom of a string to the top."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-strum-group", "h": "strum group", "t": "Two or more notes touched together, about 40 milliseconds apart at most. They play one after another, once."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-sustain", "h": "Sustain", "t": "The level a note holds at while you keep touching it."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-sync", "h": "Sync", "t": "A switch next to Tempo. On, the engine follows the shared clock. Off, it runs at its own Tempo."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-tape", "h": "Tape", "t": "Sample mode where the pad you touch chooses which slice plays. Pitch stays that of the recording."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-tempo", "h": "Tempo", "t": "How fast the beat runs, in beats per minute. It decides how long a synced Division lasts."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-threshold", "h": "threshold", "t": "A level you set on the meter. An armed recording starts when the sound first crosses it."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-tile", "h": "tile", "t": "One rectangle on the Chords surface. Touch it to play a chord. The tile shows the chord's name."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-timestretch", "h": "Timestretch", "t": "How fast the read point travels through the sample while a note plays. At zero it stands still."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-trace", "h": "trace", "t": "The strip on the Arp tab that draws the notes the arp plays: a dot for each note, and a short line for as long as it sounds."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-trim-handles", "h": "trim handles", "t": "The two handles at the ends of a sample's waveform. Everything outside them is cut from the sample."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-undo", "h": "undo", "t": "Take back your last change. Swipe in from the left edge of the screen."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-vibrato", "h": "vibrato", "t": "A small, steady wobble in pitch. On Keys you make it with tiny sideways movements."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-voice", "h": "voice", "t": "One note's worth of sound. Each engine has eight voices, so INK has 24 in all."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-voicing", "h": "Voicing", "t": "A rail that changes the order and spacing of a chord's notes: which note is lowest, and how the others stack above it."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-width", "h": "Width", "t": "How wide the Pulse wave's high part is. 50% is a square; narrower sounds thinner and more nasal."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-yours", "h": "Yours", "t": "The list of every sample you recorded or imported. Samples in Yours can be used by any kit."}, {"c": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#term-zero-crossing", "h": "zero crossing", "t": "A point where the sound wave passes through silence. Cutting there avoids a click."}];